{"Id":"CHECK_0529","Title":"Ensure 'Configure Windows spotlight on lock screen' is set to 'Disabled' (Automated)","Assessment":"Automated","Kind":"Registry","Targets":[{"Hive":"HKU","Key":"[USER SID]\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent","Name":"ConfigureWindowsSpotlight","Types":["DWord"]}],"Operator":"Equals","Values":[2],"Locator":"","Notes":"","Optional":[]},
{"Id":"CHECK_0530","Title":"Ensure 'Do not suggest third-party content in Windows spotlight' is set to 'Enabled' (Automated)","Assessment":"Automated","Kind":"Registry","Targets":[{"Hive":"HKU","Key":"[USER SID]\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent","Name":"DisableThirdPartySuggestions","Types":["DWord"]}],"Operator":"Equals","Values":[1],"Locator":"","Notes":"","Optional":[]},
{"Id":"CHECK_0531","Title":"Ensure 'Do not use diagnostic data for tailored experiences' is set to 'Enabled' (Automated)","Assessment":"Automated","Kind":"Registry","Targets":[{"Hive":"HKU","Key":"[USER SID]\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent","Name":"DisableTailoredExperiencesWithDiagnosticData","Types":["DWord"]}],"Operator":"Equals","Values":[1],"Locator":"","Notes":"","Optional":[]},
{"Id":"CHECK_0532","Title":"Ensure 'Turn off all Windows spotlight features' is set to 'Enabled' (Automated)","Assessment":"Automated","Kind":"Registry","Targets":[{"Hive":"HKU","Key":"[USER SID]\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent","Name":"DisableWindowsSpotlightFeatures","Types":["DWord"]}],"Operator":"Equals","Values":[1],"Locator":"","Notes":"","Optional":[]},
{"Id":"CHECK_0533","Title":"Ensure 'Turn off Spotlight collection on Desktop' is set to 'Enabled' (Automated)","Assessment":"Automated","Kind":"Registry","Targets":[{"Hive":"HKU","Key":"[USER SID]\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent","Name":"DisableSpotlightCollectionOnDesktop","Types":["DWord"]}],"Operator":"Equals","Values":[1],"Locator":"","Notes":"Expected REG_DWORD 1 is inferred from the Enabled policy mapping. the registry audit procedure specifies the registry location but omits the type and numeric value; confirm the mapping against CloudContent.admx.","Optional":[]},
{"Id":"CHECK_0534","Title":"Ensure 'Prevent users from sharing files within their profile.' is set to 'Enabled' (Automated)","Assessment":"Automated","Kind":"Registry","Targets":[{"Hive":"HKU","Key":"[USER SID]\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer","Name":"NoInplaceSharing","Types":["DWord"]}],"Operator":"Equals","Values":[1],"Locator":"","Notes":"","Optional":[]},
{"Id":"CHECK_0535","Title":"Ensure 'Prevent Codec Download' is set to 'Enabled' (Automated)","Assessment":"Automated","Kind":"Registry","Targets":[{"Hive":"HKU","Key":"[USER SID]\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsMediaPlayer","Name":"PreventCodecDownload","Types":["DWord"]}],"Operator":"Equals","Values":[1],"Locator":"","Notes":"","Optional":[]}
]
'@ | ConvertFrom-Json


$reportCatalogue = @'
[
{"Id":"CHECK_0001","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Enforce password history' is set to '24 or more password(s)'","Expected":"At least: 24","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Enforce password history\n\nCheck that 'Enforce password history' is set to 24 or more password(s).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Enforce password history\n\nRead the displayed setting.\nAccount scope: local account policy.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends enforcing password history to prevent reuse of old, potentially compromised passwords.","Impact":"May increase password-reset support requests and encourage users to record passwords or choose predictable variations."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0002","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Maximum password age' is set to '365 or fewer days, but not 0'","Expected":"Range: 1 | 365","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Maximum password age\n\nCheck that 'Maximum password age' is set to 365 or fewer days, but not 0.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Maximum password age\n\nRead the displayed setting.\nAccount scope: local account policy.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"The longer a password exists the higher the likelihood that it will be compromised by a brute force attack, a threat actor gaining general knowledge about the user, or by the user sharing the password.","Impact":"If the Maximum password age setting is too low, users are required to change their passwords often."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0003","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Minimum password age' is set to '1 or more day(s)'","Expected":"At least: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Minimum password age\n\nCheck that 'Minimum password age' is set to 1 or more day(s).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Minimum password age\n\nRead the displayed setting.\nAccount scope: local account policy.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends a minimum password age to prevent rapid password changes being used to bypass password history.","Impact":"Users cannot immediately change a recently set password; administrator resets may require change at next logon to avoid this delay."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0004","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Minimum password length' is set to '14 or more character(s)'","Expected":"At least: 14","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Minimum password length\n\nCheck that 'Minimum password length' is set to 14 or more character(s).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Minimum password length\n\nRead the displayed setting.\nAccount scope: local account policy.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends longer passwords to increase resistance to dictionary attacks and exhaustive password guessing.","Impact":"May increase mistyped or forgotten passwords, account lockouts and support requests; passphrase guidance can ease adoption."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0005","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Password must meet complexity requirements' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Enabled","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Password must meet complexity requirements\n\nCheck that 'Password must meet complexity requirements' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Password must meet complexity requirements\n\nRead the displayed setting.\nAccount scope: local account policy.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Passwords that contain only alphanumeric characters are extremely easy to discover with several publicly available tools.","Impact":"If the default password complexity configuration is retained, additional help desk calls for locked-out accounts could occur because users might not be accustomed to passwords that contain non-alphabetic characters."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0006","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Relax minimum password length limits' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Relax minimum password length limits\n\nCheck that 'Relax minimum password length limits' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Relax minimum password length limits\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\System\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\SAM\nValue name: RelaxMinimumPasswordLengthLimits\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting will enable the enforcement of longer and generally stronger passwords or passphrases where MFA is not in use.","Impact":"The Minimum password length setting may be configured higher than 14 characters."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0007","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Store passwords using reversible encryption' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Disabled","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Store passwords using reversible encryption\n\nCheck that 'Store passwords using reversible encryption' is set to Disabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Password Policy\\Store passwords using reversible encryption\n\nRead the displayed setting.\nAccount scope: local account policy.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Enabling this policy setting allows the operating system to store passwords in a weaker format that is much more susceptible to compromise and weakens your system security.","Impact":"If your organization uses either the CHAP authentication protocol through remote access or IAS services or Digest Authentication in IIS, you must configure this policy setting to Enabled."},
{"Id":"LOCKOUT_0008","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Account lockout duration' is set to '15 or more minute(s)'","Expected":"At least: 15","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Account Lockout Policy\\Account lockout duration\n\nCheck that 'Account lockout duration' is set to 15 or more minute(s).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Account Lockout Policy\\Account lockout duration\n\nRead the displayed setting.\nAccount scope: local account policy.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends a defined lockout duration to interrupt repeated password guessing while allowing accounts to recover automatically.","Impact":"Once lockout is enabled, affected users lose access for at least 15 minutes unless an administrator unlocks their account."},
{"Id":"LOCKOUT_0009","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Account lockout threshold' is set to '5 or fewer invalid logon attempt(s), but not 0'","Expected":"Range: 1 | 5","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Account Lockout Policy\\Account lockout threshold\n\nCheck that 'Account lockout threshold' is set to 5 or fewer invalid logon attempt(s), but not 0.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Account Lockout Policy\\Account lockout threshold\n\nRead the displayed setting.\nAccount scope: local account policy.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends an account lockout threshold to limit repeated online password guessing.","Impact":"May cause accidental or deliberately triggered lockouts, interrupt access and increase administrative unlock requests."},
{"Id":"LOCKOUT_0010","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Allow Administrator account lockout' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Enabled","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Account Lockout Policies\\Allow Administrator account lockout\n\nCheck that 'Allow Administrator account lockout' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Account Lockout Policies\\Allow Administrator account lockout\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Enabling account lockout policies for the built-in Administrator account will reduce the likelihood of a successful brute force attack.","Impact":"The built-in Administrator account will be subject to the policies in Section 1.2 Account Lockout Policy of this configuration guidance."},
{"Id":"LOCKOUT_0011","Area":"Password and lockout policy","AuditCheck":"'Reset account lockout counter after' is set to '15 or more minute(s)'","Expected":"At least: 15","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Account Policies\\Account Lockout Policy\\Reset account lockout counter after\n\nCheck that 'Reset account lockout counter after' is set to 15 or more minute(s).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Account Policies\\Account Lockout Policy\\Reset account lockout counter after\n\nRead the displayed setting.\nAccount scope: local account policy.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends retaining failed-logon counts for a reasonable interval to make repeated password guessing trigger account lockout.","Impact":"With account lockout enabled, failed attempts accumulate for longer, increasing the chance of accidental lockouts and support requests."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0012","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Access Credential Manager as a trusted caller' is set to 'No One'","Expected":"Set equals: (No One)","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Access Credential Manager as a trusted caller\n\nCheck that 'Access Credential Manager as a trusted caller' has no assigned accounts or groups (No One).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Access Credential Manager as a trusted caller\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"If an account is given this right the user of the account may create an application that calls into Credential Manager and is returned the credentials for another user.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0013","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Access this computer from the network' is set to 'Administrators, Remote Desktop Users'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators | Remote Desktop Users","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Access this computer from the network\n\nCheck that 'Access this computer from the network' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n- Remote Desktop Users\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Access this computer from the network\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting network logon to administrators and approved remote users to limit unnecessary access to workstation resources.","Impact":"Authenticated users who are not administrators or Remote Desktop users can no longer reach shares or services on this workstation over the network; confirm remote support and software-deployment requirements first."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0014","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Act as part of the operating system' is set to 'No One'","Expected":"Set equals: (No One)","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Act as part of the operating system\n\nCheck that 'Act as part of the operating system' has no assigned accounts or groups (No One).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Act as part of the operating system\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"The Act as part of the operating system user right is extremely powerful.","Impact":"There should be little or no impact because the Act as part of the operating system user right is rarely needed by any accounts other than the Local System account, which implicitly has this right."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0015","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Adjust memory quotas for a process' is set to 'Administrators, LOCAL SERVICE, NETWORK SERVICE'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators | LOCAL SERVICE | NETWORK SERVICE","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Adjust memory quotas for a process\n\nCheck that 'Adjust memory quotas for a process' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n- LOCAL SERVICE\n- NETWORK SERVICE\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Adjust memory quotas for a process\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting memory-quota rights to required administrators and services to reduce application disruption through misuse of process resource limits.","Impact":"Removing required service or IIS assignments may disrupt applications; validate legitimate service dependencies before removing access."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0016","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Allow log on locally' is set to 'Administrators, Users'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators | Users","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Allow log on locally\n\nCheck that 'Allow log on locally' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n- Users\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Allow log on locally\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends limiting local logon to administrators and standard users so that guest and other unintended accounts cannot sign in at the console.","Impact":"Accounts outside Administrators and Users cannot sign in locally; confirm any kiosk, shared-device or service sign-in requirements."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0017","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Allow log on through Remote Desktop Services' is set to 'Administrators, Remote Desktop Users'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators | Remote Desktop Users","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Allow log on through Remote Desktop Services\n\nCheck that 'Allow log on through Remote Desktop Services' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n- Remote Desktop Users\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Allow log on through Remote Desktop Services\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting Remote Desktop logons to approved administrators and users to limit unnecessary interactive access to workstations.","Impact":"Users relying on removed groups will lose Remote Desktop access; confirm authorised administration and support access remain available."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0018","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Back up files and directories' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Back up files and directories\n\nCheck that 'Back up files and directories' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Back up files and directories\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting backup rights to reduce unauthorised copying and disclosure of sensitive data.","Impact":"Backup operators or software may lose required access, causing backup jobs to fail unless justified service-account permissions are retained."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0019","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Change the system time' is set to 'Administrators, LOCAL SERVICE'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators | LOCAL SERVICE","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Change the system time\n\nCheck that 'Change the system time' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n- LOCAL SERVICE\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Change the system time\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting time-changing rights to protect event timestamps and prevent disruption to time-dependent authentication.","Impact":"Normally little impact with automated time synchronisation; delegated users will no longer be able to set the clock manually."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0020","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Create a pagefile' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Create a pagefile\n\nCheck that 'Create a pagefile' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Create a pagefile\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Users who can change the page file size could make it extremely small or move the file to a highly fragmented storage volume, which could cause reduced computer performance.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0021","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Create a token object' is set to 'No One'","Expected":"Set equals: (No One)","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Create a token object\n\nCheck that 'Create a token object' has no assigned accounts or groups (No One).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Create a token object\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"A user account that is given this user right has complete control over the system and can lead to the system being compromised.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0022","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Create global objects' is set to 'Administrators, LOCAL SERVICE, NETWORK SERVICE, SERVICE'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators | LOCAL SERVICE | NETWORK SERVICE | SERVICE","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Create global objects\n\nCheck that 'Create global objects' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n- LOCAL SERVICE\n- NETWORK SERVICE\n- SERVICE\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Create global objects\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Users who can create global objects could affect Windows services and processes that run under other user or system accounts.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0023","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Create permanent shared objects' is set to 'No One'","Expected":"Set equals: (No One)","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Create permanent shared objects\n\nCheck that 'Create permanent shared objects' has no assigned accounts or groups (No One).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Create permanent shared objects\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Users who have the Create permanent shared objects user right could create new shared objects and expose sensitive data to the network.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0024","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Create symbolic links' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators (NT VIRTUAL MACHINE\\Virtual Machines accepted when Hyper-V is installed)","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Create symbolic links\n\nCheck that 'Create symbolic links' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\nNT VIRTUAL MACHINE\\Virtual Machines is accepted as an additional entry only when Hyper-V is installed.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Create symbolic links\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Users who have the Create symbolic links user right could inadvertently or maliciously expose your system to symbolic link attacks.","Impact":"In most cases there will be no impact because this is the default configuration."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0025","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Debug programs' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Debug programs\n\nCheck that 'Debug programs' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Debug programs\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"The Debug programs user right can be exploited to capture sensitive computer information from system memory, or to access and modify kernel or application structures.","Impact":"If you revoke this user right, no one will be able to debug programs."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0026","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Deny access to this computer from the network' to include 'Guests, Local account'","Expected":"Contains: Guests | Local account","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Deny access to this computer from the network\n\nCheck that 'Deny access to this computer from the network' includes every entry below. Additional entries are permitted for this check:\n- Guests\n- Local account\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Deny access to this computer from the network\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends denying Guest and local-account network logons to reduce unauthorised access and lateral movement with reused local credentials.","Impact":{"Workgroup":"Will block all network logons using local accounts on this standalone workstation, including remote administration with local administrator accounts; retain a tested console-access route before applying.","Shared":"Blocks network logons with local accounts and may disrupt remote administration or maintenance jobs that use local credentials."}},
{"Id":"CHECK_0027","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Deny log on as a batch job' to include 'Guests'","Expected":"Contains: Guests","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Deny log on as a batch job\n\nCheck that 'Deny log on as a batch job' includes every entry below. Additional entries are permitted for this check:\n- Guests\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Deny log on as a batch job\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends denying Guest batch logons to prevent untrusted accounts being used for scheduled jobs.","Impact":"Scheduled jobs using accounts covered by this deny assignment may fail; check Guests membership and delegated task requirements."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0028","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Deny log on as a service' to include 'Guests'","Expected":"Contains: Guests","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Deny log on as a service\n\nCheck that 'Deny log on as a service' includes every entry below. Additional entries are permitted for this check:\n- Guests\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Deny log on as a service\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends denying Guest service logons to prevent untrusted accounts being used by services.","Impact":"Services running under denied accounts, including accounts in Guests, may fail to start."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0029","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Deny log on locally' to include 'Guests'","Expected":"Contains: Guests","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Deny log on locally\n\nCheck that 'Deny log on locally' includes every entry below. Additional entries are permitted for this check:\n- Guests\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Deny log on locally\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends denying console logons to accounts in Guests to prevent unnecessary interactive access to workstations.","Impact":"Accounts covered by this deny assignment lose console access; check group membership and delegated administration requirements."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0030","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Deny log on through Remote Desktop Services' to include 'Guests, Local account'","Expected":"Contains: Guests | Local account","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Deny log on through Remote Desktop Services\n\nCheck that 'Deny log on through Remote Desktop Services' includes every entry below. Additional entries are permitted for this check:\n- Guests\n- Local account\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Deny log on through Remote Desktop Services\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends denying Guest and local-account Remote Desktop logons to reduce misuse of local credentials and unauthorised interactive access.","Impact":{"Workgroup":"Will block Remote Desktop access using local accounts, including local administrators; retain console access or another tested administration route.","Shared":"Local accounts, including local administrators, lose Remote Desktop access; verify domain-based administration and emergency access procedures."}},
{"Id":"CHECK_0031","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Enable computer and user accounts to be trusted for delegation' is set to 'No One'","Expected":"Set equals: (No One)","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Enable computer and user accounts to be trusted for delegation\n\nCheck that 'Enable computer and user accounts to be trusted for delegation' has no assigned accounts or groups (No One).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Enable computer and user accounts to be trusted for delegation\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Misuse of this user right could allow unauthorized users to impersonate other users on the network.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0032","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Force shutdown from a remote system' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Force shutdown from a remote system\n\nCheck that 'Force shutdown from a remote system' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Force shutdown from a remote system\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting remote shutdown rights to trusted administrators to reduce deliberate or accidental service interruption.","Impact":"Delegated operators or maintenance tools may lose remote shutdown and restart capability; confirm required operational access."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0033","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Generate security audits' is set to 'LOCAL SERVICE, NETWORK SERVICE, RESTRICTED SERVICES\\PrintSpoolerService'","Expected":"Set equals: LOCAL SERVICE | NETWORK SERVICE | RESTRICTED SERVICES\\PrintSpoolerService","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Generate security audits\n\nCheck that 'Generate security audits' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- LOCAL SERVICE\n- NETWORK SERVICE\n- RESTRICTED SERVICES\\PrintSpoolerService\nOn Windows 11 builds where RESTRICTED SERVICES\\PrintSpoolerService does not exist, that entry is not expected.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Generate security audits\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting audit-generation rights to trusted services to reduce fabricated events that obscure or overwrite security evidence.","Impact":"Removing required IIS application-pool assignments can disrupt web-service functionality; validate legitimate audit-generation and service requirements."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0034","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Impersonate a client after authentication' is set to 'Administrators, LOCAL SERVICE, NETWORK SERVICE, SERVICE, RESTRICTED SERVICES\\PrintSpoolerService'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators | LOCAL SERVICE | NETWORK SERVICE | SERVICE | RESTRICTED SERVICES\\PrintSpoolerService","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Impersonate a client after authentication\n\nCheck that 'Impersonate a client after authentication' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n- LOCAL SERVICE\n- NETWORK SERVICE\n- SERVICE\n- RESTRICTED SERVICES\\PrintSpoolerService\nWhen IIS is installed, IIS_IUSRS is accepted as an additional entry.\nOn Windows 11 builds where RESTRICTED SERVICES\\PrintSpoolerService does not exist, that entry is not expected.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Impersonate a client after authentication\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"A threat actor with the Impersonate a client after authentication user right could create a service, trick a client to make them connect to the service, and then impersonate that client to elevate the threat actor's level of access to that of the client.","Impact":"In most cases this configuration will have no impact. If IIS is installed, the user right also needs to be assigned to IIS_IUSRS."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0035","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Increase scheduling priority' is set to 'Administrators, Window Manager\\Window Manager Group'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators | Window Manager\\Window Manager Group","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Increase scheduling priority\n\nCheck that 'Increase scheduling priority' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n- Window Manager\\Window Manager Group\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Increase scheduling priority\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"A user who is assigned this user right could increase the scheduling priority of a process to Real-Time, which would leave little processing time for all other processes and could lead to a DoS condition.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0036","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Load and unload device drivers' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Load and unload device drivers\n\nCheck that 'Load and unload device drivers' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Load and unload device drivers\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting driver-loading rights because malicious or untrusted drivers can execute highly privileged code.","Impact":"Delegated operators may lose permission to install or manage device and printer drivers, requiring administrator assistance."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0037","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Lock pages in memory' is set to 'No One'","Expected":"Set equals: (No One)","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Lock pages in memory\n\nCheck that 'Lock pages in memory' has no assigned accounts or groups (No One).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Lock pages in memory\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Users with the Lock pages in memory user right could assign physical memory to several processes, which could leave little or no RAM for other processes and result in a DoS condition.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0038","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Log on as a batch job' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Log on as a batch job\n\nCheck that 'Log on as a batch job' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Log on as a batch job\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting batch logon rights to administrators so that scheduled tasks cannot be used by lower-privileged accounts to run code with stored credentials.","Impact":"Scheduled tasks or batch jobs that run under non-administrator accounts will fail unless those accounts are granted a documented exception."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0039","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Log on as a service' is set to 'No One'","Expected":"Set equals: (No One)","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Log on as a service\n\nCheck that 'Log on as a service' has no assigned accounts or groups (No One).\nNT VIRTUAL MACHINE\\Virtual Machines is accepted when Hyper-V is installed, and WDAGUtilityAccount is accepted when Microsoft Defender Application Guard is installed.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Log on as a service\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends that no account is granted the right to log on as a service on a workstation, because service accounts keep credentials on the device where they can be extracted.","Impact":"Software that installs a service under a named account will not start until that account is granted the right; virtual service accounts are unaffected."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0040","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Manage auditing and security log' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Manage auditing and security log\n\nCheck that 'Manage auditing and security log' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Manage auditing and security log\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"The ability to manage the Security event log is a powerful user right and it should be closely guarded.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0041","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Modify an object label' is set to 'No One'","Expected":"Set equals: (No One)","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Modify an object label\n\nCheck that 'Modify an object label' has no assigned accounts or groups (No One).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Modify an object label\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"By modifying the integrity label of an object owned by another user a threat actor may cause them to execute code at a higher level of privilege than intended.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0042","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Modify firmware environment values' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Modify firmware environment values\n\nCheck that 'Modify firmware environment values' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Modify firmware environment values\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Anyone who is assigned the Modify firmware environment values user right could configure the settings of a hardware component to cause it to fail, which could lead to data corruption or a DoS condition.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0043","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Perform volume maintenance tasks' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Perform volume maintenance tasks\n\nCheck that 'Perform volume maintenance tasks' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Perform volume maintenance tasks\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"A user who is assigned the Perform volume maintenance tasks user right could delete a volume, which could result in the loss of data or a DoS condition.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0044","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Profile single process' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Profile single process\n\nCheck that 'Profile single process' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Profile single process\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"The Profile single process user right presents a moderate vulnerability.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0045","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Profile system performance' is set to 'Administrators, NT SERVICE\\WdiServiceHost'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators | NT SERVICE\\WdiServiceHost","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Profile system performance\n\nCheck that 'Profile system performance' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n- NT SERVICE\\WdiServiceHost\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Profile system performance\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Threat actors with this user right could monitor a computer's performance to help identify critical processes that they might wish to attack directly.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0046","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Replace a process level token' is set to 'LOCAL SERVICE, NETWORK SERVICE'","Expected":"Set equals: LOCAL SERVICE | NETWORK SERVICE","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Replace a process level token\n\nCheck that 'Replace a process level token' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- LOCAL SERVICE\n- NETWORK SERVICE\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Replace a process level token\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting process-token replacement to required service identities to reduce misuse of another account's execution context.","Impact":"Removing required service or IIS application-pool assignments can prevent processes starting; validate legitimate application dependencies."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0047","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Restore files and directories' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Restore files and directories\n\nCheck that 'Restore files and directories' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Restore files and directories\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends restricting restore rights to prevent unauthorised data overwrites or replacement of trusted files with malicious code.","Impact":"Backup operators or recovery software may lose restoration capability; preserve approved recovery procedures and required service-account permissions."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0048","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Shut down the system' is set to 'Administrators, Users'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators | Users","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Shut down the system\n\nCheck that 'Shut down the system' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n- Users\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Shut down the system\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends limiting shutdown rights to administrators and signed-in users so that other accounts cannot interrupt work or force a restart.","Impact":"Accounts outside Administrators and Users cannot shut down or restart the device."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0049","Area":"User rights assignments","AuditCheck":"'Take ownership of files or other objects' is set to 'Administrators'","Expected":"Set equals: Administrators","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Take ownership of files or other objects\n\nCheck that 'Take ownership of files or other objects' is assigned to exactly these entries, with no additional entries:\n- Administrators\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\User Rights Assignment\\Take ownership of files or other objects\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"Any users with the Take ownership of files or other objects user right can take control of any object, regardless of the permissions on that object, and then make any changes they wish to that object.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0050","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Accounts: Guest account status' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Disabled: ","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Accounts: Guest account status\n\nCheck that 'Accounts: Guest account status' is set to Disabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Accounts: Guest account status\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"The default Guest account allows unauthenticated network users to log on as Guest with no password.","Impact":"All network users will need to authenticate before they can access shared resources."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0051","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Accounts: Limit local account use of blank passwords to console logon only' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Accounts: Limit local account use of blank passwords to console logon only\n\nCheck that 'Accounts: Limit local account use of blank passwords to console logon only' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Accounts: Limit local account use of blank passwords to console logon only\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: LimitBlankPasswordUse\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Blank passwords are a serious threat to computer security and should be forbidden through both organizational policy and suitable technical measures.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0052","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Accounts: Rename administrator account'","Expected":"Organisation-approved configuration; manual confirmation required","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Accounts: Rename administrator account\n\nCheck that 'Accounts: Rename administrator account' matches the approved account naming policy and differs from the original built-in account name for this OS language.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Accounts: Rename administrator account\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"The built-in Administrator account exists on all systems that run the Windows 2000 or newer operating systems.","Impact":"Users who are authorized to use this account will need to be aware of the new account name. (The guidance for this setting assumes that the Administrator account was not disabled, which was recommended earlier in this chapter.)"},
{"Id":"CHECK_0053","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Accounts: Rename guest account'","Expected":"Organisation-approved configuration; manual confirmation required","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Accounts: Rename guest account\n\nCheck that 'Accounts: Rename guest account' matches the approved account naming policy and differs from the original built-in account name for this OS language.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Accounts: Rename guest account\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"The Guest account exists on all computers that run Windows 2000 or newer operating systems.","Impact":"There should be little impact, because the Guest account is disabled by default."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0054","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Audit: Force audit policy subcategory settings (Windows Vista or later) to override audit policy category settings' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Audit: Force audit policy subcategory settings (Windows Vista or later) to override audit policy category settings\n\nCheck that 'Audit: Force audit policy subcategory settings (Windows Vista or later) to override audit policy category settings' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Audit: Force audit policy subcategory settings (Windows Vista or later) to override audit policy category settings\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: SCENoApplyLegacyAuditPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Prior to the introduction of auditing subcategories in Windows Vista, it was difficult to track events at a per-system or per-user level.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0055","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Audit: Shut down system immediately if unable to log security audits' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Audit: Shut down system immediately if unable to log security audits\n\nCheck that 'Audit: Shut down system immediately if unable to log security audits' is set to Disabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Audit: Shut down system immediately if unable to log security audits\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: CrashOnAuditFail\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If the","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0056","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Devices: Prevent users from installing printer drivers' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Devices: Prevent users from installing printer drivers\n\nCheck that 'Devices: Prevent users from installing printer drivers' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Devices: Prevent users from installing printer drivers\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Print\\Providers\\LanMan Print Services\\Servers\nValue name: AddPrinterDrivers\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"It may be appropriate in some organizations to allow users to install printer drivers on their own workstations.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0057","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Domain member: Digitally encrypt or sign secure channel data (always)' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Digitally encrypt or sign secure channel data (always)\n\nCheck that 'Domain member: Digitally encrypt or sign secure channel data (always)' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Digitally encrypt or sign secure channel data (always)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Netlogon\\Parameters\nValue name: RequireSignOrSeal\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"When a computer joins a domain, a computer account is created.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. However, only Windows NT 4.0 with Service Pack 6a (SP6a) and subsequent versions of the Windows operating system support digital encryption and signing of the secure channel."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0058","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Domain member: Digitally encrypt secure channel data (when possible)' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Digitally encrypt secure channel data (when possible)\n\nCheck that 'Domain member: Digitally encrypt secure channel data (when possible)' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Digitally encrypt secure channel data (when possible)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Netlogon\\Parameters\nValue name: SealSecureChannel\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"When a computer joins a domain, a computer account is created.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. However, only Windows NT 4.0 Service Pack 6a (SP6a) and subsequent versions of the Windows operating system support digital encryption and signing of the secure channel."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0059","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Domain member: Digitally sign secure channel data (when possible)' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Digitally sign secure channel data (when possible)\n\nCheck that 'Domain member: Digitally sign secure channel data (when possible)' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Digitally sign secure channel data (when possible)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Netlogon\\Parameters\nValue name: SignSecureChannel\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"When a computer joins a domain, a computer account is created.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. However, only Windows NT 4.0 with Service Pack 6a (SP6a) and subsequent versions of the Windows operating system support digital encryption and signing of the secure channel."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0060","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Domain member: Disable machine account password changes' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Disable machine account password changes\n\nCheck that 'Domain member: Disable machine account password changes' is set to Disabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Disable machine account password changes\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Netlogon\\Parameters\nValue name: DisablePasswordChange\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If a system does not change their password, there are security risks because the security channel is used for pass-through authentication.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. Note: Some problems can occur as a result of machine account password expiration, particularly if a machine is reverted to a previous point-in-time state, as is common with virtual machines."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0061","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Domain member: Maximum machine account password age' is set to '30 or fewer days, but not 0'","Expected":"Range: 1 | 30","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Maximum machine account password age\n\nCheck that 'Domain member: Maximum machine account password age' is set to 30 or fewer days, but not 0.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Maximum machine account password age\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\System\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Netlogon\\Parameters\nValue name: MaximumPasswordAge\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 through 30, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In Active Directory-based domains, each computer has an account and password just like every user.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. Note: Some problems can occur as a result of machine account password expiration, particularly if a machine is reverted to a previous point-in-time state, as is common with virtual machines."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0062","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Domain member: Require strong (Windows 2000 or later) session key' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Require strong (Windows 2000 or later) session key\n\nCheck that 'Domain member: Require strong (Windows 2000 or later) session key' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Domain member: Require strong (Windows 2000 or later) session key\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Netlogon\\Parameters\nValue name: RequireStrongKey\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Session keys that are used to establish secure channel communications between Domain Controllers and member computers are much stronger in Windows 2000 than they were in previous Microsoft operating systems.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. However, computers will not be able to join Windows NT 4.0 domains, and trusts between Active Directory domains and Windows NT-style domains may not work properly."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0063","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Interactive logon: Do not require CTRL+ALT+DEL' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Do not require CTRL+ALT+DEL\n\nCheck that 'Interactive logon: Do not require CTRL+ALT+DEL' is set to Disabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Do not require CTRL+ALT+DEL\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: DisableCAD\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If users are not required to press CTRL+ALT+DEL, they are susceptible to attacks that attempt to intercept their passwords.","Impact":"Users must press CTRL+ALT+DEL before they log on to Windows unless they use a smart card for Windows logon."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0064","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Interactive logon: Don't display last signed-in' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Don't display last signed-in\n\nCheck that 'Interactive logon: Don't display last signed-in' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Don't display last signed-in\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: DontDisplayLastUserName\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends hiding the last signed-in username to reduce disclosure of valid account names to someone accessing the logon screen.","Impact":"Users must enter their username as well as their password rather than relying on the last signed-in account being displayed."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0065","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Interactive logon: Machine inactivity limit' is set to '900 or fewer second(s), but not 0'","Expected":"Range: 1 | 900","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Machine inactivity limit\n\nCheck that 'Interactive logon: Machine inactivity limit' is set to 900 or fewer second(s), but not 0.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Machine inactivity limit\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: InactivityTimeoutSecs\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 through 900, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If a user forgets to lock their computer when they walk away it's possible that a passerby will hijack it.","Impact":"The screen saver will automatically activate when the computer has been unattended for the amount of time specified."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0066","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Interactive logon: Message text for users attempting to log on'","Expected":"Organisation-approved configuration; manual confirmation required","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Message text for users attempting to log on\n\nCompare 'Interactive logon: Message text for users attempting to log on' with the organisation-approved legal notice wording.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Message text for users attempting to log on\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: LegalNoticeText\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: the approved wording or access list described in the verification steps; manual review required\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Displaying a warning message before logon may help prevent malicious logins by a threat actor warning of the consequences of their misconduct.","Impact":"Users will have to acknowledge a dialog box containing the configured text before they can log on to the computer."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0067","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Interactive logon: Message title for users attempting to log on'","Expected":"Organisation-approved configuration; manual confirmation required","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Message title for users attempting to log on\n\nCompare 'Interactive logon: Message title for users attempting to log on' with the organisation-approved legal notice wording.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Message title for users attempting to log on\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: LegalNoticeCaption\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: the approved wording or access list described in the verification steps; manual review required\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Displaying a warning message before logon may help prevent malicious logins by a threat actor warning of the consequences of their misconduct.","Impact":"Users will have to acknowledge a dialog box with the configured title before they can log on to the computer."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0068","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Interactive logon: Number of previous logons to cache (in case domain controller is not available)' is set to '4 or fewer logon(s)'","Expected":"Range: 0 | 4","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Number of previous logons to cache (in case domain controller is not available)\n\nCheck that 'Interactive logon: Number of previous logons to cache (in case domain controller is not available)' is an integer from 0 through 4 logons, inclusive.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Number of previous logons to cache (in case domain controller is not available)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon\nValue name: CachedLogonsCount\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: a numeric string representing an integer from 0 through 4, inclusive\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends limiting cached domain logons to reduce locally stored credential material exposed to offline password attacks.","Impact":{"Workgroup":"No domain-logon impact on this workgroup host; on domain members, reducing the cache can limit sign-in during domain controller outages.","Shared":"Fewer domain users may be able to sign in during domain controller outages; a cache value of zero prevents cached domain logons."}},
{"Id":"CHECK_0069","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Interactive logon: Prompt user to change password before expiration' is set to 'between 5 and 14 days'","Expected":"Range: 5 | 14","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Prompt user to change password before expiration\n\nCheck that 'Interactive logon: Prompt user to change password before expiration' is set to between 5 and 14 days.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Prompt user to change password before expiration\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon\nValue name: PasswordExpiryWarning\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 5 through 14, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"It is recommended that user passwords be configured to expire periodically.","Impact":"Users will see a dialog box prompt to change their password each time that they log on to the domain when their password is configured to expire between 5 and 14 days."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0070","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Interactive logon: Smart card removal behavior' is set to 'Lock Workstation' or higher","Expected":"OneOf: 1 | 2 | 3","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Smart card removal behavior\n\nCheck that 'Interactive logon: Smart card removal behavior' is one of: Lock Workstation; Force Logoff; Disconnect if a Remote Desktop Services session.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Interactive logon: Smart card removal behavior\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon\nValue name: ScRemoveOption\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: one of \"1\", \"2\", \"3\"\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends protecting sessions on smart-card removal to prevent unattended access using the authenticated user's identity.","Impact":"Smart-card removal will lock, disconnect or log off the session, depending on the selected option; forced logoff may lose unsaved work."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0071","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Microsoft network client: Digitally sign communications (always)' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network client: Digitally sign communications (always)\n\nCheck that 'Microsoft network client: Digitally sign communications (always)' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network client: Digitally sign communications (always)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanmanWorkstation\\Parameters\nValue name: RequireSecuritySignature\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Session hijacking uses tools that allow threat actors who have access to the same network as the client or server to interrupt, end, or steal a session in progress. threat actors can potentially intercept and modify unsigned SMB packets and then modify the traffic and forward it so that the server might perform undesirable actions.","Impact":"The Microsoft network client will not communicate with a Microsoft network server unless that server agrees to perform SMB packet signing."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0072","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Microsoft network client: Send unencrypted password to third-party SMB servers' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network client: Send unencrypted password to third-party SMB servers\n\nCheck that 'Microsoft network client: Send unencrypted password to third-party SMB servers' is set to Disabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network client: Send unencrypted password to third-party SMB servers\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanmanWorkstation\\Parameters\nValue name: EnablePlainTextPassword\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If you enable this policy setting, the server can transmit passwords in plaintext across the network to other computers that offer SMB services, which is a significant security risk.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. Some very old applications and operating systems such as MS-DOS, Windows for Workgroups 3.11, and Windows 95a may not be able to communicate with the servers in your organization by means of the SMB protocol."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0073","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Microsoft network server: Amount of idle time required before suspending session' is set to '15 or fewer minute(s)'","Expected":"Range: 0 | 15","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network server: Amount of idle time required before suspending session\n\nCheck that 'Microsoft network server: Amount of idle time required before suspending session' is set to 15 or fewer minute(s).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network server: Amount of idle time required before suspending session\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanManServer\\Parameters\nValue name: AutoDisconnect\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 through 15, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Each SMB session consumes server resources, and numerous null sessions will slow the server or possibly cause it to fail.","Impact":"There will be little impact because SMB sessions will be re-established automatically if the client resumes activity."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0074","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Microsoft network server: Digitally sign communications (always)' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network server: Digitally sign communications (always)\n\nCheck that 'Microsoft network server: Digitally sign communications (always)' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network server: Digitally sign communications (always)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanManServer\\Parameters\nValue name: RequireSecuritySignature\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Session hijacking uses tools that allow threat actors who have access to the same network as the client or server to interrupt, end, or steal a session in progress. threat actors can potentially intercept and modify unsigned SMB packets and then modify the traffic and forward it so that the server might perform undesirable actions.","Impact":"The Microsoft network client will not communicate with a Microsoft network server unless that server agrees to perform SMB packet signing."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0075","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Microsoft network server: Disconnect clients when logon hours expire' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network server: Disconnect clients when logon hours expire\n\nCheck that 'Microsoft network server: Disconnect clients when logon hours expire' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network server: Disconnect clients when logon hours expire\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanManServer\\Parameters\nValue name: enableforcedlogoff\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If your organization configures logon hours for users, then it makes sense to enable this policy setting.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. If logon hours are not used in your organization, this policy setting will have no impact."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0076","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Microsoft network server: Server SPN target name validation level' is set to 'Accept if provided by client' or higher","Expected":"One of: 1 | 2","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network server: Server SPN target name validation level\n\nCheck that 'Microsoft network server: Server SPN target name validation level' is set to Accept if provided by client.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Microsoft network server: Server SPN target name validation level\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanManServer\\Parameters\nValue name: SMBServerNameHardeningLevel\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The identity of a computer can be spoofed to gain unauthorized access to network resources.","Impact":"All Windows operating systems support both a client-side SMB component and a server-side SMB component."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0077","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Allow anonymous SID/Name translation' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Disabled","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Allow anonymous SID/Name translation\n\nCheck that 'Network access: Allow anonymous SID/Name translation' is set to Disabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Allow anonymous SID/Name translation\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"If this policy setting is enabled, a user with local access could use the well-known Administrator's SID to learn the real name of the built-in Administrator account, even if it has been renamed.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0078","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Do not allow anonymous enumeration of SAM accounts' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Do not allow anonymous enumeration of SAM accounts\n\nCheck that 'Network access: Do not allow anonymous enumeration of SAM accounts' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Do not allow anonymous enumeration of SAM accounts\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: RestrictAnonymousSAM\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"An unauthorized user could anonymously list account names and use the information to attempt to guess passwords or perform social engineering attacks. (Social engineering attacks try to deceive users in some way to obtain passwords or some form of security information.)","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. It will be impossible to establish trusts with Windows NT 4.0-based domains."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0079","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Do not allow anonymous enumeration of SAM accounts and shares' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Do not allow anonymous enumeration of SAM accounts and shares\n\nCheck that 'Network access: Do not allow anonymous enumeration of SAM accounts and shares' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Do not allow anonymous enumeration of SAM accounts and shares\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: RestrictAnonymous\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends blocking anonymous account and share enumeration to reduce information available for password guessing and social engineering.","Impact":"Clients must authenticate before protected enumeration; legacy or anonymous-access workflows may fail."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0080","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Do not allow storage of passwords and credentials for network authentication' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Do not allow storage of passwords and credentials for network authentication\n\nCheck that 'Network access: Do not allow storage of passwords and credentials for network authentication' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Do not allow storage of passwords and credentials for network authentication\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: DisableDomainCreds\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing unnecessary storage of network credentials to reduce passwords available to malicious software running under a user's account.","Impact":"Users may need to re-enter passwords; tasks, backups or connections relying on saved alternate credentials may fail."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0081","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Let Everyone permissions apply to anonymous users' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Let Everyone permissions apply to anonymous users\n\nCheck that 'Network access: Let Everyone permissions apply to anonymous users' is set to Disabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Let Everyone permissions apply to anonymous users\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: EveryoneIncludesAnonymous\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"An unauthorized user could anonymously list account names and shared resources and use the information to attempt to guess passwords, perform social engineering attacks, or launch DoS attacks.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0082","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Named Pipes that can be accessed anonymously' is set to 'None'","Expected":"No entries","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Named Pipes that can be accessed anonymously\n\nCheck that 'Network access: Named Pipes that can be accessed anonymously' is set to None (no entries).\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Named Pipes that can be accessed anonymously\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanManServer\\Parameters\nValue name: NullSessionPipes\nExpected type: REG_MULTI_SZ\nExpected data: no non-empty entries\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Limiting named pipes that can be accessed anonymously will reduce the attack surface of the system.","Impact":"Null session access over named pipes will be disabled unless they are included, and applications that rely on this feature or on unauthenticated access to named pipes will no longer function."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0083","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Remotely accessible registry paths' is configured","Expected":"Set equals: System\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\ProductOptions | System\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Server Applications | Software\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Remotely accessible registry paths\n\nConfirm the displayed setting against the recommended configuration stated above.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Remotely accessible registry paths\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\SecurePipeServers\\Winreg\\AllowedExactPaths\nValue name: Machine\nExpected type: REG_MULTI_SZ\nExpected data: exactly the following entries, without regard to ordering: System\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\ProductOptions | System\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Server Applications | Software\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The registry is a database that contains computer configuration information, and much of the information is sensitive.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. However, if the default registry paths from the list of accessible ones is removed, remote management tools such as the Microsoft Baseline Security Analyzer and Microsoft Systems Management Server could fail, as they require remote access to the registry to properly monitor and manage computers."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0084","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Remotely accessible registry paths and sub-paths' is configured","Expected":"Organisation-approved configuration; manual confirmation required","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Remotely accessible registry paths and sub-paths\n\nCompare 'Network access: Remotely accessible registry paths and sub-paths' with the approved remote registry access list. The suggested base entries are:\nSystem\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Print\\Printers\nSystem\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Eventlog\nSoftware\\Microsoft\\OLAP Server\nSoftware\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Print\nSoftware\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Windows\nSystem\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\ContentIndex\nSystem\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Terminal Server\nSystem\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Terminal Server\\UserConfig\nSystem\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Terminal Server\\DefaultUserConfiguration\nSoftware\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Perflib\nSystem\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\SysmonLog\nAdd System\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\CertSvc only for a Certification Authority; add System\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\WINS only when WINS is installed. This check requires manual policy review.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Remotely accessible registry paths and sub-paths\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\SecurePipeServers\\Winreg\\AllowedPaths\nValue name: Machine\nExpected type: REG_MULTI_SZ\nExpected data: the approved wording or access list described in the verification steps; manual review required\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The registry contains sensitive computer configuration information that could be used by a threat actor to facilitate unauthorized activities.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. However, if the default registry paths from the list of accessible ones is removed, remote management tools such as the Microsoft Baseline Security Analyzer and Microsoft Systems Management Server could fail, as they require remote access to the registry to properly monitor and manage computers."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0085","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Restrict anonymous access to Named Pipes and Shares' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Restrict anonymous access to Named Pipes and Shares\n\nCheck that 'Network access: Restrict anonymous access to Named Pipes and Shares' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Restrict anonymous access to Named Pipes and Shares\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanManServer\\Parameters\nValue name: RestrictNullSessAccess\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Null sessions are a weakness that can be exploited through shares (including the default shares) on computers in your environment.","Impact":"Normally no impact; this is the default behaviour. Legacy domain trusts or applications requiring anonymous named-pipe access may need reviewed exceptions."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0086","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Restrict clients allowed to make remote calls to SAM' is set to 'Administrators: Remote Access: Allow'","Expected":"Equals: O:BAG:BAD:(A;;RC;;;BA)","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Restrict clients allowed to make remote calls to SAM\n\nCheck that 'Network access: Restrict clients allowed to make remote calls to SAM' is set to Administrators: Remote Access: Allow.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Restrict clients allowed to make remote calls to SAM\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: restrictremotesam\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: \"O:BAG:BAD:(A;;RC;;;BA)\"\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"To ensure that an unauthorized user cannot anonymously list local account names or groups and use the information to attempt to guess passwords or perform social engineering attacks. (Social engineering attacks try to deceive users in some way to obtain passwords or some form of security information.)","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0087","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Shares that can be accessed anonymously' is set to 'None'","Expected":"No entries","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Shares that can be accessed anonymously\n\nCheck that 'Network access: Shares that can be accessed anonymously' is set to None.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Shares that can be accessed anonymously\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanManServer\\Parameters\nValue name: NullSessionShares\nExpected type: REG_MULTI_SZ\nExpected data: no non-empty entries\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"It is very dangerous to allow any values in this setting.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0088","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network access: Sharing and security model for local accounts' is set to 'Classic - local users authenticate as themselves'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Sharing and security model for local accounts\n\nCheck that 'Network access: Sharing and security model for local accounts' is set to Classic - local users authenticate as themselves.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network access: Sharing and security model for local accounts\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: ForceGuest\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"With the Guest only model, any user who can authenticate to your computer over the network does so with guest privileges, which probably means that they will not have write access to shared resources on that computer.","Impact":"None - this is the default configuration for domain-joined computers."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0089","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: Allow Local System to use computer identity for NTLM' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Allow Local System to use computer identity for NTLM\n\nCheck that 'Network security: Allow Local System to use computer identity for NTLM' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Allow Local System to use computer identity for NTLM\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: UseMachineId\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"When connecting to computers running versions of Windows earlier than Windows Vista or Windows Server 2008 (non-R2), services running as Local System and using SPNEGO (Negotiate) that revert to NTLM use the computer identity.","Impact":"Services running as Local System that use Negotiate when reverting to NTLM authentication will use the computer identity."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0090","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: Allow LocalSystem NULL session fallback' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Allow LocalSystem NULL session fallback\n\nCheck that 'Network security: Allow LocalSystem NULL session fallback' is set to Disabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Allow LocalSystem NULL session fallback\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\MSV1_0\nValue name: AllowNullSessionFallback\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"NULL sessions are less secure because by definition they are unauthenticated.","Impact":"Any applications that require NULL sessions for LocalSystem will not work as designed."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0091","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network Security: Allow PKU2U authentication requests to this computer to use online identities' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network Security: Allow PKU2U authentication requests to this computer to use online identities\n\nCheck that 'Network Security: Allow PKU2U authentication requests to this computer to use online identities' is set to Disabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network Security: Allow PKU2U authentication requests to this computer to use online identities\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\pku2u\nValue name: AllowOnlineID\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The PKU2U protocol is a peer-to-peer authentication protocol - authentication should be managed centrally in most managed networks.","Impact":"None - this is the default configuration for domain-joined computers."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0092","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: Configure encryption types allowed for Kerberos' is set to 'AES128_HMAC_SHA1, AES256_HMAC_SHA1, Future encryption types'","Expected":"Equals: 2147483640","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Configure encryption types allowed for Kerberos\n\nCheck that 'Network security: Configure encryption types allowed for Kerberos' is set to AES128_HMAC_SHA1, AES256_HMAC_SHA1, Future encryption types.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Configure encryption types allowed for Kerberos\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\Kerberos\\Parameters\nValue name: SupportedEncryptionTypes\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2147483640 (decimal; 0x7FFFFFF8)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The strength of each encryption algorithm varies from one to the next, choosing stronger algorithms will reduce the risk of compromise however doing so may cause issues when the computer attempts to authenticate with systems that do not support them.","Impact":"If not selected, the encryption type will not be allowed."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0093","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: Force logoff when logon hours expire' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Enabled","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Force logoff when logon hours expire\n\nCheck that 'Network security: Force logoff when logon hours expire' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Force logoff when logon hours expire\n\nRead the displayed setting.","CheckType":"GUI verification","Rationale":"If this setting is disabled, a user could remain connected to the computer outside of their allotted logon hours.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0094","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: LAN Manager authentication level' is set to 'Send NTLMv2 response only. Refuse LM & NTLM'","Expected":"Equals: 5","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: LAN Manager authentication level\n\nCheck that 'Network security: LAN Manager authentication level' is set to Send NTLMv2 response only. Refuse LM & NTLM.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: LAN Manager authentication level\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\nValue name: LmCompatibilityLevel\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 5 (decimal; 0x00000005)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Windows 2000 and Windows XP clients were configured by default to send LM and NTLM authentication responses (Windows 95-based and Windows 98-based clients only send LM).","Impact":"Clients use NTLMv2 authentication only and use NTLMv2 session security if the server supports it; Domain Controllers refuse LM and NTLM (accept only NTLMv2 authentication)."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0095","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: LDAP client encryption requirements' is set to 'Negotiate sealing' or higher","Expected":"One of: 1 | 2","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: LDAP client encryption requirements\n\nCheck that 'Network security: LDAP client encryption requirements' is set to Negotiate sealing.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: LDAP client encryption requirements\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LDAP\nValue name: LDAPClientConfidentiality\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Unencrypted network traffic is susceptible to man-in-the-middle attacks in which an intruder captures the packets between the client and server, modifies them, and then forwards them to the server.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. However, if this setting is configured to require LDAP encryption on the server, then it must also be configured to require on the client."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0096","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: LDAP client signing requirements' is set to 'Negotiate signing' or higher","Expected":"One of: 1 | 2","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: LDAP client signing requirements\n\nCheck that 'Network security: LDAP client signing requirements' is set to Negotiate signing.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: LDAP client signing requirements\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LDAP\nValue name: LDAPClientIntegrity\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Unsigned network traffic is susceptible to man-in-the-middle attacks in which an intruder captures the packets between the client and server, modifies them, and then forwards them to the server.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. However, if the option to require LDAP signatures tis selected, then the client must also be configured."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0097","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: Minimum session security for NTLM SSP based (including secure RPC) clients' is set to 'Require NTLMv2 session security, Require 128-bit encryption'","Expected":"Equals: 537395200","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Minimum session security for NTLM SSP based (including secure RPC) clients\n\nCheck that both options are selected for 'Network security: Minimum session security for NTLM SSP based (including secure RPC) clients': Require NTLMv2 session security and Require 128-bit encryption.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Minimum session security for NTLM SSP based (including secure RPC) clients\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\MSV1_0\nValue name: NTLMMinClientSec\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 537395200 (decimal; 0x20080000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends NTLMv2 session security and 128-bit encryption to reduce interception and tampering of NTLM-protected client traffic.","Impact":"NTLM connections lacking the required session security or encryption will fail, potentially affecting legacy servers and applications."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0098","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: Minimum session security for NTLM SSP based (including secure RPC) servers' is set to 'Require NTLMv2 session security, Require 128-bit encryption'","Expected":"Equals: 537395200","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Minimum session security for NTLM SSP based (including secure RPC) servers\n\nCheck that both options are selected for 'Network security: Minimum session security for NTLM SSP based (including secure RPC) servers': Require NTLMv2 session security and Require 128-bit encryption.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Minimum session security for NTLM SSP based (including secure RPC) servers\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\MSV1_0\nValue name: NTLMMinServerSec\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 537395200 (decimal; 0x20080000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends NTLMv2 session security and 128-bit encryption to reduce interception and tampering of NTLM-protected traffic.","Impact":"NTLM connections lacking the required session security or encryption will fail, potentially affecting legacy clients and applications."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0099","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: Restrict NTLM: Audit Incoming NTLM Traffic' is set to 'Enable auditing for all accounts'","Expected":"Equals: 2","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Restrict NTLM: Audit Incoming NTLM Traffic\n\nCheck that 'Network security: Restrict NTLM: Audit Incoming NTLM Traffic' is set to Enable auditing for all accounts.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Restrict NTLM: Audit Incoming NTLM Traffic\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\MSV1_0\nValue name: AuditReceivingNTLMTraffic\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Auditing and monitoring NTLM traffic can assist in identifying systems using this outdated authentication protocol, so they can be remediated to using a more secure protocol, such as Kerberos.","Impact":"The event log will contain information on incoming NTLM authentication traffic."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0100","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'Network security: Restrict NTLM: Outgoing NTLM traffic to remote servers' is set to 'Audit all' or higher","Expected":"One of: 1 | 2","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Restrict NTLM: Outgoing NTLM traffic to remote servers\n\nCheck that 'Network security: Restrict NTLM: Outgoing NTLM traffic to remote servers' is set to Audit all.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\Network security: Restrict NTLM: Outgoing NTLM traffic to remote servers\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Lsa\\MSV1_0\nValue name: RestrictSendingNTLMTraffic\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Auditing and monitoring NTLM traffic can assist in identifying systems using this outdated authentication protocol, so they can be remediated to using a more secure protocol, such as Kerberos.","Impact":"The event log will contain information on outgoing NTLM authentication traffic."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0101","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'System cryptography: Force strong key protection for user keys stored on the computer' is set to 'User is prompted when the key is first used' or higher","Expected":"One of: 1 | 2","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\System cryptography: Force strong key protection for user keys stored on the computer\n\nCheck that 'System cryptography: Force strong key protection for user keys stored on the computer' is set to User is prompted when the key is first used, or User must enter a password each time they use a key.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\System cryptography: Force strong key protection for user keys stored on the computer\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Cryptography\nValue name: ForceKeyProtection\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends prompting users when their stored private keys are used, so that malware running in the user session cannot silently use certificates and keys.","Impact":"Users are prompted, or must enter a password, each time a stored private key is used; some unattended certificate-based processes may stop working."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0102","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'System objects: Require case insensitivity for non-Windows subsystems' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\System objects: Require case insensitivity for nonWindows subsystems\n\nCheck that 'System objects: Require case insensitivity for non-Windows subsystems' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\System objects: Require case insensitivity for nonWindows subsystems\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\Kernel\nValue name: ObCaseInsensitive\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Because Windows is case-insensitive but the POSIX subsystem will support case sensitivity, failure to enable this policy setting would make it possible for a user of that subsystem to create a file with the same name as another file but with a different mix of upper and lower case letters.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0103","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'System objects: Strengthen default permissions of internal system objects (e.g. Symbolic Links)' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\System objects: Strengthen default permissions of internal system objects (e.g. Symbolic Links)\n\nCheck that 'System objects: Strengthen default permissions of internal system objects (e.g. Symbolic Links)' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\System objects: Strengthen default permissions of internal system objects (e.g. Symbolic Links)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\nValue name: ProtectionMode\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting determines the strength of the default DACL for objects.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0104","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'User Account Control: Admin Approval Mode for the Built-in Administrator account' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Admin Approval Mode for the Built-in Administrator account\n\nCheck that 'User Account Control: Admin Approval Mode for the Built-in Administrator account' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Admin Approval Mode for the Built-in Administrator account\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: FilterAdministratorToken\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends Admin Approval Mode for the built-in Administrator account to reduce silent execution of malicious software with full administrative privileges.","Impact":"Use of the built-in Administrator account introduces elevation prompts; administrative workflows may need adjustment."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0105","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'User Account Control: Behavior of the elevation prompt for administrators in Admin Approval Mode' is set to 'Prompt for consent on the secure desktop' or higher","Expected":"OneOf: 1 | 2","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Behavior of the elevation prompt for administrators in Admin Approval Mode\n\nCheck that 'User Account Control: Behavior of the elevation prompt for administrators in Admin Approval Mode' is one of: Prompt for consent on the secure desktop; Prompt for credentials on the secure desktop.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Behavior of the elevation prompt for administrators in Admin Approval Mode\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: ConsentPromptBehaviorAdmin\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends secure desktop elevation prompts so administrators can review and reject requests for elevated privileges.","Impact":"Administrators receive secure desktop consent or credential prompts; workflows requiring elevation may need adjustment."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0106","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'User Account Control: Behavior of the elevation prompt for standard users' is set to 'Automatically deny elevation requests'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Behavior of the elevation prompt for standard users\n\nCheck that 'User Account Control: Behavior of the elevation prompt for standard users' is set to Automatically deny elevation requests.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Behavior of the elevation prompt for standard users\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: ConsentPromptBehaviorUser\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends denying standard-user elevation requests to keep privileged operations within approved administrative workflows and reduce malicious elevation attempts.","Impact":"Standard users cannot elevate through a prompt and need an approved administrative route; this may increase support requests."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0107","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'User Account Control: Detect application installations and prompt for elevation' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Detect application installations and prompt for elevation\n\nCheck that 'User Account Control: Detect application installations and prompt for elevation' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Detect application installations and prompt for elevation\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: EnableInstallerDetection\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Some malicious software will attempt to install itself after being given permission to run.","Impact":"When an application installation package is detected that requires elevation of privilege, the user is prompted to enter an administrative user name and password."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0108","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'User Account Control: Only elevate UIAccess applications that are installed in secure locations' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Only elevate UIAccess applications that are installed in secure locations\n\nCheck that 'User Account Control: Only elevate UIAccess applications that are installed in secure locations' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Only elevate UIAccess applications that are installed in secure locations\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: EnableSecureUIAPaths\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"UIAccess Integrity allows an application to bypass User Interface Privilege Isolation (UIPI) restrictions when an application is elevated in privilege from a standard user to an administrator.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0109","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'User Account Control: Run all administrators in Admin Approval Mode' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Run all administrators in Admin Approval Mode\n\nCheck that 'User Account Control: Run all administrators in Admin Approval Mode' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Run all administrators in Admin Approval Mode\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: EnableLUA\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This is the setting that turns on or off UAC.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. Users and administrators will need to learn to work with UAC prompts and adjust their work habits to use least privilege operations."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0110","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'User Account Control: Switch to the secure desktop when prompting for elevation' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Switch to the secure desktop when prompting for elevation\n\nCheck that 'User Account Control: Switch to the secure desktop when prompting for elevation' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Switch to the secure desktop when prompting for elevation\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: PromptOnSecureDesktop\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Standard elevation prompt dialog boxes can be spoofed, which may cause users to disclose their passwords to malicious software.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0111","Area":"Security options","AuditCheck":"'User Account Control: Virtualize file and registry write failures to per-user locations' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Local Security Policy (secpol.msc). Navigate to:\n\nSecurity Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Virtualize file and registry write failures to per-user locations\n\nCheck that 'User Account Control: Virtualize file and registry write failures to per-user locations' is set to Enabled.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Local Policies\\Security Options\\User Account Control: Virtualize file and registry write failures to per-user locations\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: EnableVirtualization\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting reduces vulnerabilities by ensuring that legacy applications only write data to permitted locations.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0112","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Bluetooth Audio Gateway Service (BTAGService)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Bluetooth Audio Gateway Service (service name: BTAGService). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Bluetooth Audio Gateway Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\BTAGService\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Bluetooth Audio Gateway service on workstations that do not need it, to reduce the Bluetooth attack surface.","Impact":"Bluetooth hands-free and headset call audio will not work."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0113","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Bluetooth Support Service (bthserv)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Bluetooth Support Service (service name: bthserv). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Bluetooth Support Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\bthserv\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Bluetooth Support service on workstations that do not need it, to prevent discovery and pairing of Bluetooth devices that could be used to attack or extract data from the device.","Impact":"Bluetooth devices, including keyboards, mice and headsets, cannot be discovered or used."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0114","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Computer Browser (Browser)' is set to 'Disabled' or 'Not Installed'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Computer Browser (service name: Browser). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Computer Browser\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Browser\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Computer Browser service on workstations that do not need it, to remove a legacy NetBIOS browsing protocol that depends on SMBv1.","Impact":"Legacy network browsing of the Network Neighbourhood list will not work; no impact where SMBv1 is already removed."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0115","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Downloaded Maps Manager (MapsBroker)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Downloaded Maps Manager (service name: MapsBroker). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Downloaded Maps Manager\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\MapsBroker\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Downloaded Maps Manager service on workstations that do not need it, to stop unnecessary background downloads from the internet.","Impact":"Applications cannot access downloaded offline maps."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0116","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'GameInput Service (GameInputSvc)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of GameInput Service (service name: GameInputSvc). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\GameInput Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\GameInputSvc\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the GameInput service on workstations that do not need it, to reduce the risk of keystrokes and other input being captured through a low-level gaming input interface.","Impact":"Game controllers and other peripherals that rely on the GameInput API may not work in games."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0117","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Geolocation Service (lfsvc)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Geolocation Service (service name: lfsvc). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Geolocation Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\lfsvc\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Geolocation service on workstations that do not need it, to prevent applications from tracking the location of the device.","Impact":"Applications cannot obtain the device location, and geofencing features will not work."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0118","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'IIS Admin Service (IISADMIN)' is set to 'Disabled' or 'Not Installed'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of IIS Admin Service (service name: IISADMIN). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\IIS Admin Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\IISADMIN\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the IIS Admin service on workstations that do not need it, to ensure workstations are not hosting web content outside managed server platforms.","Impact":"IIS cannot be administered on this device; web development that relies on local IIS needs an exception."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0119","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Infrared monitor service (irmon)' is set to 'Disabled' or 'Not Installed'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Infrared monitor service (service name: irmon). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Infrared monitor service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\irmon\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Infrared monitor service on workstations that do not need it, to prevent file transfer over infrared links.","Impact":"Files cannot be exchanged with infrared devices."},
