{"Id":"CHECK_0141","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'SSDP Discovery (SSDPSRV)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of SSDP Discovery (service name: SSDPSRV). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\SSDP Discovery\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\SSDPSRV\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the SSDP Discovery service on workstations that do not need it, to stop discovery and announcement of UPnP devices on the network.","Impact":"UPnP devices are not discovered and this device does not announce its own services."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0142","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'UPnP Device Host (upnphost)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of UPnP Device Host (service name: upnphost). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\UPnP Device Host\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\upnphost\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the UPnP Device Host service on workstations that do not need it, to prevent the device from hosting UPnP devices that other systems can connect to.","Impact":"Hosted UPnP devices stop working and no further hosted devices can be added."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0143","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Web Management Service (WMSvc)' is set to 'Disabled' or 'Not Installed'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Web Management Service (service name: WMSvc). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Web Management Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\WMSvc\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Web Management service on workstations that do not need it, to prevent remote administration of a local web server.","Impact":"Remote IIS management of this device will not work."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0144","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Windows Error Reporting Service (WerSvc)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Windows Error Reporting Service (service name: WerSvc). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Windows Error Reporting Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\WerSvc\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Windows Error Reporting service on workstations that do not need it, to limit the amount of crash and diagnostic data, which may include memory contents, sent to Microsoft.","Impact":"Error reports are not sent, and diagnostic and repair services may be less effective."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0145","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Windows Event Collector (Wecsvc)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Windows Event Collector (service name: Wecsvc). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Windows Event Collector\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Wecsvc\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Windows Event Collector service on workstations that do not need it, to ensure workstations are not used to collect and store event logs from other systems.","Impact":"The device cannot act as an event collector; forwarding its own events to a central collector is unaffected."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0146","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Windows Media Player Network Sharing Service (WMPNetworkSvc)' is set to 'Disabled' or 'Not Installed'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Windows Media Player Network Sharing Service (service name: WMPNetworkSvc). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Windows Media Player Network Sharing Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\WMPNetworkSvc\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Windows Media Player Network Sharing service on workstations that do not need it, to prevent media libraries being shared with other devices on the network.","Impact":"Windows Media Player libraries cannot be shared to network players and media devices."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0147","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Windows Mobile Hotspot Service (icssvc)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Windows Mobile Hotspot Service (service name: icssvc). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Windows Mobile Hotspot Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\icssvc\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Windows Mobile Hotspot service on workstations that do not need it, to prevent the device sharing its network connection and bridging other devices onto the corporate network.","Impact":"The device cannot be used as a mobile hotspot."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0148","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Windows Push Notifications System Service (WpnService)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Windows Push Notifications System Service (service name: WpnService). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Windows Push Notifications System Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\WpnService\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Windows Push Notifications System service on workstations that do not need it, to stop unsolicited content being pushed to the device from the internet.","Impact":"Live tiles, toast notifications and applications that depend on push notifications, including some messaging and collaboration clients, will not receive updates."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0149","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Windows PushToInstall Service (PushToInstall)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Windows PushToInstall Service (service name: PushToInstall). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Windows PushToInstall Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\PushToInstall\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Windows PushToInstall service on workstations that do not need it, to prevent applications being installed remotely from the Microsoft Store.","Impact":"Applications cannot be pushed to the device from the Microsoft Store website or another device."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0150","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Windows Remote Management (WS-Management) (WinRM)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Windows Remote Management (WS-Management) (service name: WinRM). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Windows Remote Management (WS-Management)\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\WinRM\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Windows Remote Management (WS-Management) service on workstations that do not need it, to prevent remote command execution through WinRM and PowerShell remoting.","Impact":"Inbound PowerShell remoting and WinRM-based management of this device will not work."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0151","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'World Wide Web Publishing Service (W3SVC)' is set to 'Disabled' or 'Not Installed'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of World Wide Web Publishing Service (service name: W3SVC). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\World Wide Web Publishing Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\W3SVC\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the World Wide Web Publishing service on workstations that do not need it, to ensure workstations are not hosting web content outside managed server platforms.","Impact":"The device cannot host IIS websites; local web development needs an exception."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0152","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Xbox Accessory Management Service (XboxGipSvc)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Xbox Accessory Management Service (service name: XboxGipSvc). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Xbox Accessory Management Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\XboxGipSvc\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Xbox Accessory Management service on workstations that do not need it, to remove consumer gaming components that have no business purpose.","Impact":"Xbox accessories cannot be managed or updated from this device."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0153","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Xbox Live Auth Manager (XblAuthManager)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Xbox Live Auth Manager (service name: XblAuthManager). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Xbox Live Auth Manager\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\XblAuthManager\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Xbox Live Auth Manager service on workstations that do not need it, to remove consumer gaming components that have no business purpose.","Impact":"Applications and games cannot sign in to Xbox Live."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0154","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Xbox Live Game Save (XblGameSave)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Xbox Live Game Save (service name: XblGameSave). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Xbox Live Game Save\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\XblGameSave\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Xbox Live Game Save service on workstations that do not need it, to remove consumer gaming components that sync data to a personal cloud service.","Impact":"Game save data is not synchronised with Xbox Live."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0155","Area":"Services","AuditCheck":"'Xbox Live Networking Service (XboxNetApiSvc)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Startup type Disabled (Start = 4), or service not installed","Steps":"On {HOST}, open Services (services.msc). Open the properties of Xbox Live Networking Service (service name: XboxNetApiSvc). Check that Startup type is Disabled; close with Cancel. If the service is not listed, it is not installed and the check is met.\n\nEquivalent GP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\System Services\\Xbox Live Networking Service\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\XboxNetApiSvc\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. If the service key does not exist, the service is not installed.","CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the Xbox Live Networking service on workstations that do not need it, to remove consumer gaming components that have no business purpose.","Impact":"Xbox Live networking features are unavailable to applications and games."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0156","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Domain: Firewall state' is set to 'On (recommended)'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Firewall state\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Domain: Firewall state' is set to On (recommended).\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Firewall state\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\DomainProfile\nValue name: EnableFirewall\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Firewall state\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Domain: Firewall state' is set to On (recommended).\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\DomainProfile\nValue name: EnableFirewall\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If the firewall is turned off all traffic will be able to access the system, and a threat actor may be able to remotely exploit a weakness in a network service.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0157","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Domain: Inbound connections' is set to 'Block (default)'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Inbound connections\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Domain: Inbound connections' is set to Block (default).\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Inbound connections\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\DomainProfile\nValue name: DefaultInboundAction\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Inbound connections\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Domain: Inbound connections' is set to Block (default).\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\DomainProfile\nValue name: DefaultInboundAction\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If the firewall allows all traffic to access the system, then a threat actor may be able to remotely exploit a weakness in a network service.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0158","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Domain: Settings: Display a notification' is set to 'No'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Settings Customize\\Display a notification\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Domain: Settings: Display a notification' is set to No.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Settings Customize\\Display a notification\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\DomainProfile\nValue name: DisableNotifications\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Settings Customize\\Display a notification\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Domain: Settings: Display a notification' is set to No.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\DomainProfile\nValue name: DisableNotifications\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Firewall notifications can be complex and may confuse the end users, who would not be able to address the alert.","Impact":"Windows Firewall will not display a notification when a program is blocked from receiving inbound connections."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0159","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Domain: Logging: Name' is configured","Expected":"Non-empty configured path ending in \\<filename>.log","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Logging Customize\\Name\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Domain: Logging: Name' is a non-empty path ending in \\<filename>.log. Also compare the location with the organisation-approved logging policy.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Logging Customize\\Name\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\DomainProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogFilePath\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: a non-empty configured path ending in \\<filename>.log; confirm the approved location separately\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Logging Customize\\Name\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Domain: Logging: Name' is a non-empty path ending in \\<filename>.log. Also compare the location with the organisation-approved logging policy.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\DomainProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogFilePath\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: a non-empty configured path ending in \\<filename>.log; confirm the approved location separately\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If Windows Firewall events are not recorded it may be difficult or impossible for Administrators to analyze system issues or unauthorized activities of threat actors.","Impact":"The log file will be stored in the location and file specified by the organization."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0160","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Domain: Logging: Size limit (KB)' is set to '16,384 KB or greater'","Expected":"At least: 16384","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Logging Customize\\Size limit (KB)\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Domain: Logging: Size limit (KB)' is set to 16,384 KB or greater.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Logging Customize\\Size limit (KB)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\DomainProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogFileSize\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: at least 16384 (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Domain Profile\\Logging Customize\\Size limit (KB)\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Domain: Logging: Size limit (KB)' is set to 16,384 KB or greater.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\DomainProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogFileSize\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: at least 16384 (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If events are not recorded it may be difficult or impossible to determine the root cause of system problems or the unauthorized activities of threat actors.","Impact":"The log file size will be limited to the specified size, old events will be overwritten by newer ones when the limit is reached."},
