ication' is set to No.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PrivateProfile\nValue name: DisableNotifications\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Firewall notifications can be complex and may confuse the end users, who would not be able to address the alert.","Impact":"Windows Firewall will not display a notification when a program is blocked from receiving inbound connections."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0166","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Private: Logging: Name' is configured","Expected":"Non-empty configured path ending in \\<filename>.log","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Private Profile\\Logging Customize\\Name\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Private: Logging: Name' is a non-empty path ending in \\<filename>.log. Also compare the location with the organisation-approved logging policy.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Private Profile\\Logging Customize\\Name\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PrivateProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogFilePath\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: a non-empty configured path ending in \\<filename>.log; confirm the approved location separately\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Private Profile\\Logging Customize\\Name\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Private: Logging: Name' is a non-empty path ending in \\<filename>.log. Also compare the location with the organisation-approved logging policy.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PrivateProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogFilePath\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: a non-empty configured path ending in \\<filename>.log; confirm the approved location separately\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If Windows Firewall events are not recorded it may be difficult or impossible for Administrators to analyze system issues or unauthorized activities of threat actors.","Impact":"The log file will be stored in the location and file specified by the organization."},
