{"Id":"CHECK_0172","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Public: Settings: Display a notification' is set to 'No'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Settings Customize\\Display a notification\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Settings: Display a notification' is set to No.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Settings Customize\\Display a notification\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\nValue name: DisableNotifications\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Settings Customize\\Display a notification\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Settings: Display a notification' is set to No.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\nValue name: DisableNotifications\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Some organizations may prefer to avoid alarming users when firewall rules block certain types of network activity.","Impact":"Windows Firewall will not display a notification when a program is blocked from receiving inbound connections."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0173","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Public: Settings: Apply local firewall rules' is set to 'No'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Settings Customize\\Apply local firewall rules\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Settings: Apply local firewall rules' is set to No.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Settings Customize\\Apply local firewall rules\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\nValue name: AllowLocalPolicyMerge\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Settings Customize\\Apply local firewall rules\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Settings: Apply local firewall rules' is set to No.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\nValue name: AllowLocalPolicyMerge\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"When in the Public profile, there should be no special local firewall exceptions per computer.","Impact":"Administrators can still create firewall rules, but the rules will not be applied."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0174","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Public: Settings: Apply local connection security rules' is set to 'No'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Settings Customize\\Apply local connection security rules\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Settings: Apply local connection security rules' is set to No.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Settings Customize\\Apply local connection security rules\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\nValue name: AllowLocalIPsecPolicyMerge\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Settings Customize\\Apply local connection security rules\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Settings: Apply local connection security rules' is set to No.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\nValue name: AllowLocalIPsecPolicyMerge\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Users with administrative privileges might create firewall rules that expose the system to remote attack.","Impact":"Administrators can still create local connection security rules, but the rules will not be applied."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0175","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Name' is configured","Expected":"Non-empty configured path ending in \\<filename>.log","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Name\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Name' is a non-empty path ending in \\<filename>.log. Also compare the location with the organisation-approved logging policy.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Name\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogFilePath\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: a non-empty configured path ending in \\<filename>.log; confirm the approved location separately\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Name\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Name' is a non-empty path ending in \\<filename>.log. Also compare the location with the organisation-approved logging policy.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogFilePath\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: a non-empty configured path ending in \\<filename>.log; confirm the approved location separately\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If Windows Firewall events are not recorded it may be difficult or impossible for Administrators to analyze system issues or unauthorized activities of threat actors.","Impact":"The log file will be stored in the location and file specified by the organization."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0176","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Size limit (KB)' is set to '16,384 KB or greater'","Expected":"At least: 16384","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Size limit (KB)\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Size limit (KB)' is set to 16,384 KB or greater.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Size limit (KB)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogFileSize\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: at least 16384 (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Size limit (KB)\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Size limit (KB)' is set to 16,384 KB or greater.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogFileSize\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: at least 16384 (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If events are not recorded it may be difficult or impossible to determine the root cause of system problems or the unauthorized activities of threat actors.","Impact":"The log file size will be limited to the specified size, old events will be overwritten by newer ones when the limit is reached."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0177","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Log dropped packets' is set to 'Yes'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Log dropped packets\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Log dropped packets' is set to Yes.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Log dropped packets\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogDroppedPackets\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Log dropped packets\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Log dropped packets' is set to Yes.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogDroppedPackets\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If events are not recorded it may be difficult or impossible to determine the root cause of system problems or the unauthorized activities of threat actors.","Impact":"Information about dropped packets will be recorded in the firewall log file."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0178","Area":"Windows Defender Firewall","AuditCheck":"'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Log successful connections' is set to 'Yes'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Log successful connections\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Log successful connections' is set to Yes.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Log successful connections\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogSuccessfulConnections\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Windows Settings\\Security Settings\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security\\Windows Defender Firewall Properties\\Public Profile\\Logging Customize\\Log successful connections\n\nCheck that 'Windows Firewall: Public: Logging: Log successful connections' is set to Yes.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsFirewall\\PublicProfile\\Logging\nValue name: LogSuccessfulConnections\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If events are not recorded it may be difficult or impossible to determine the root cause of system problems or the unauthorized activities of threat actors.","Impact":"Information about successful connections will be recorded in the firewall log file."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0179","Area":"Audit policy","AuditCheck":"'Audit Credential Validation' is set to 'Success and Failure'","Expected":"AuditEquals: 3","Steps":"On {HOST}, run the following command in an elevated Command Prompt:\n\nauditpol /get /subcategory:\"{0cce923f-69ae-11d9-bed3-505054503030}\"\n\nCheck the Setting returned for 'Credential Validation'. Expected result: Success and Failure.","CheckType":"Command (auditpol)","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends auditing credential validation to help detect suspicious activity and preserve evidence for incident investigation.","Impact":"Additional audit events increase Security log volume and may require more storage, retention and collection capacity."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0180","Area":"Audit policy","AuditCheck":"'Audit Application Group Management' is set to 'Success and Failure'","Expected":"AuditEquals: 3","Steps":"On {HOST}, run the following command in an elevated Command Prompt:\n\nauditpol /get /subcategory:\"{0cce9239-69ae-11d9-bed3-505054503030}\"\n\nCheck the Setting returned for 'Application Group Management'. Expected result: Success and Failure.","CheckType":"Command (auditpol)","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends auditing application group management to help detect suspicious activity and preserve evidence for incident investigation.","Impact":"Additional audit events increase Security log volume and may require more storage, retention and collection capacity."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0181","Area":"Audit policy","AuditCheck":"'Audit Security Group Management' is set to include 'Success'","Expected":"AuditIncludes: 1","Steps":"On {HOST}, run the following command in an elevated Command Prompt:\n\nauditpol /get /subcategory:\"{0cce9237-69ae-11d9-bed3-505054503030}\"\n\nCheck the Setting returned for 'Security Group Management'. Expected result: Success or Success and Failure.","CheckType":"Command (auditpol)","Rationale":"If no audit settings are configured, or if audit settings are too lax on the computers in your organization, security incidents might not be detected, or not enough evidence will be available for network forensic analysis after security incidents occur.","Impact":"Advanced audit logging will generate additional events when this feature is enabled."},
