{"Id":"CHECK_0205","Area":"Audit policy","AuditCheck":"'Audit System Integrity' is set to 'Success and Failure'","Expected":"AuditEquals: 3","Steps":"On {HOST}, run the following command in an elevated Command Prompt:\n\nauditpol /get /subcategory:\"{0cce9212-69ae-11d9-bed3-505054503030}\"\n\nCheck the Setting returned for 'System Integrity'. Expected result: Success and Failure.","CheckType":"Command (auditpol)","Rationale":"If no audit settings are configured, or if audit settings are too lax on the computers in your organization, security incidents might not be detected, or not enough evidence will be available for network forensic analysis after security incidents occur.","Impact":"Advanced audit logging will generate additional events when this feature is enabled."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0206","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prevent enabling lock screen camera' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Personalization\\Prevent enabling lock screen camera\n\nCheck that 'Prevent enabling lock screen camera' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Personalization\\Prevent enabling lock screen camera\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Personalization\nValue name: NoLockScreenCamera\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Personalization\\Prevent enabling lock screen camera\n\nCheck that 'Prevent enabling lock screen camera' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Personalization\nValue name: NoLockScreenCamera\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Disabling the lock screen camera extends the protection afforded by the lock screen to camera features.","Impact":"If you enable this setting, users will no longer be able to enable or disable lock screen camera access in PC Settings, and the camera cannot be invoked on the lock screen."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0207","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prevent enabling lock screen slide show' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Personalization\\Prevent enabling lock screen slide show\n\nCheck that 'Prevent enabling lock screen slide show' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Personalization\\Prevent enabling lock screen slide show\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Personalization\nValue name: NoLockScreenSlideshow\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Personalization\\Prevent enabling lock screen slide show\n\nCheck that 'Prevent enabling lock screen slide show' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Personalization\nValue name: NoLockScreenSlideshow\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Disabling the lock screen slide show extends the protection afforded by the lock screen to slide show contents.","Impact":"If you enable this setting, users will no longer be able to modify slide show settings in PC Settings, and no slide show will ever start."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0208","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow users to enable online speech recognition services' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Regional and Language Options\\Allow users to enable online speech recognition services\n\nCheck that 'Allow users to enable online speech recognition services' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Regional and Language Options\\Allow users to enable online speech recognition services\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\InputPersonalization\nValue name: AllowInputPersonalization\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Regional and Language Options\\Allow users to enable online speech recognition services\n\nCheck that 'Allow users to enable online speech recognition services' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\InputPersonalization\nValue name: AllowInputPersonalization\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If this setting is Enabled sensitive information could be stored in the cloud or sent to Microsoft.","Impact":"Automatic learning of speech, inking, and typing stops and users cannot change its value via PC Settings."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0209","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Online Tips' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Allow Online Tips\n\nCheck that 'Allow Online Tips' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Allow Online Tips\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: AllowOnlineTips\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Control Panel\\Allow Online Tips\n\nCheck that 'Allow Online Tips' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: AllowOnlineTips\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In high-security environments, data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"Settings will not contact Microsoft content services to retrieve tips and help content."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0210","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Apply UAC restrictions to local accounts on network logons' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Apply UAC restrictions to local accounts on network logons\n\nCheck that 'Apply UAC restrictions to local accounts on network logons' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Apply UAC restrictions to local accounts on network logons\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: LocalAccountTokenFilterPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Apply UAC restrictions to local accounts on network logons\n\nCheck that 'Apply UAC restrictions to local accounts on network logons' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: LocalAccountTokenFilterPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Local accounts are at high risk for credential theft when the same account and password is configured on multiple systems.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0211","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure SMB v1 client driver' is set to 'Enabled: Disable driver (recommended)'","Expected":"Equals: 4","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Configure SMB v1 client driver\n\nCheck that 'Configure SMB v1 client driver' is set to Enabled: Disable driver (recommended).\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Configure SMB v1 client driver\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\mrxsmb10\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Configure SMB v1 client driver\n\nCheck that 'Configure SMB v1 client driver' is set to Enabled: Disable driver (recommended).\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\mrxsmb10\nValue name: Start\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Since September 2016, Microsoft has strongly encouraged that SMBv1 be disabled and no longer used on modern networks, as it is a 30 year old design that is much more vulnerable to attacks then much newer designs such as SMBv2 and SMBv3.","Impact":"Some legacy OSes (e.g. Windows XP, Server 2003 or older), applications and appliances may no longer be able to communicate with the system once SMBv1 is disabled."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0212","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure SMB v1 server' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Configure SMB v1 server\n\nCheck that 'Configure SMB v1 server' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Configure SMB v1 server\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanmanServer\\Parameters\nValue name: SMB1\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Configure SMB v1 server\n\nCheck that 'Configure SMB v1 server' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\LanmanServer\\Parameters\nValue name: SMB1\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Since September 2016, Microsoft has strongly encouraged that SMBv1 be disabled and no longer used on modern networks, as it is a 30 year old design that is much more vulnerable to attacks then much newer designs such as SMBv2 and SMBv3.","Impact":"Some legacy OSes (e.g. Windows XP, Server 2003 or older), applications and appliances may no longer be able to communicate with the system once SMBv1 is disabled."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0213","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable Certificate Padding' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Enable Certificate Padding\n\nCheck that 'Enable Certificate Padding' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Enable Certificate Padding\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Cryptography\\Wintrust\\Config\nValue name: EnableCertPaddingCheck\nExpected type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Wow6432Node\\Microsoft\\Cryptography\\Wintrust\\Config\nValue name: EnableCertPaddingCheck\nExpected type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Enable Certificate Padding\n\nCheck that 'Enable Certificate Padding' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Cryptography\\Wintrust\\Config\nValue name: EnableCertPaddingCheck\nExpected type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Wow6432Node\\Microsoft\\Cryptography\\Wintrust\\Config\nValue name: EnableCertPaddingCheck\nExpected type: REG_DWORD or REG_SZ\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A remote code execution vulnerability exists in the way that the WinVerifyTrust function handles Windows Authenticode signature verification for portable executable (PE) files.","Impact":"Microsoft recommends that installers are built to only extract content from validated portions of signed files."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0214","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable Structured Exception Handling Overwrite Protection (SEHOP)' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Enable Structured Exception Handling Overwrite Protection (SEHOP)\n\nCheck that 'Enable Structured Exception Handling Overwrite Protection (SEHOP)' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Enable Structured Exception Handling Overwrite Protection (SEHOP)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\kernel\nValue name: DisableExceptionChainValidation\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\Enable Structured Exception Handling Overwrite Protection (SEHOP)\n\nCheck that 'Enable Structured Exception Handling Overwrite Protection (SEHOP)' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\\kernel\nValue name: DisableExceptionChainValidation\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This feature is designed to block exploits that use the Structured Exception Handler (SEH) overwrite technique.","Impact":"After you enable SEHOP, existing versions of Cygwin, Skype, and Armadillo-protected applications may not work correctly."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0215","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'NetBT NodeType configuration' is set to 'Enabled: P-node (recommended)'","Expected":"Equals: 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\NetBT NodeType configuration\n\nCheck that 'NetBT NodeType configuration' is set to Enabled: P-node (recommended).\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\NetBT NodeType configuration\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\NetBT\\Parameters\nValue name: NodeType\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\NetBT NodeType configuration\n\nCheck that 'NetBT NodeType configuration' is set to Enabled: P-node (recommended).\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\NetBT\\Parameters\nValue name: NodeType\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In order to help mitigate the risk of NetBIOS Name Service (NBT-NS) poisoning attacks, setting the node type to P-node (point-to-point) will prevent the system from sending out NetBIOS broadcasts.","Impact":"NetBIOS name resolution queries will require a defined and available WINS server for external NetBIOS name resolution."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0216","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'WDigest Authentication' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\WDigest Authentication (disabling may require KB2871997)\n\nCheck that 'WDigest Authentication' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\WDigest Authentication (disabling may require KB2871997)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\SecurityProviders\\WDigest\nValue name: UseLogonCredential\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MS Security Guide\\WDigest Authentication (disabling may require KB2871997)\n\nCheck that 'WDigest Authentication' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\SecurityProviders\\WDigest\nValue name: UseLogonCredential\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling WDigest credential caching so that plaintext passwords are not held in LSASS memory, where credential-theft tools can read them.","Impact":"None expected - this is the default behaviour on supported versions of Windows; applications that still depend on Digest authentication would need review."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0217","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (AutoAdminLogon) Enable Automatic Logon' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (AutoAdminLogon) Enable Automatic Logon\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (AutoAdminLogon) Enable Automatic Logon' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (AutoAdminLogon) Enable Automatic Logon\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon\nValue name: AutoAdminLogon\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (AutoAdminLogon) Enable Automatic Logon\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (AutoAdminLogon) Enable Automatic Logon' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Winlogon\nValue name: AutoAdminLogon\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If you configure a computer for automatic logon, anyone who can physically gain access to the computer can also gain access to everything that is on the computer, including any network or networks that the computer is connected to.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0218","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting IPv6) IP source routing protection level' is set to 'Enabled: Highest protection, source routing is completely disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting IPv6) IP source routing protection level\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting IPv6) IP source routing protection level' is set to Enabled: Highest protection, source routing is completely disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting IPv6) IP source routing protection level\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip6\\Parameters\nValue name: DisableIPSourceRouting\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting IPv6) IP source routing protection level\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting IPv6) IP source routing protection level' is set to Enabled: Highest protection, source routing is completely disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip6\\Parameters\nValue name: DisableIPSourceRouting\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor could use source routed packets to obscure their identity and location.","Impact":"All incoming source routed packets will be dropped."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0219","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting) IP source routing protection level' is set to 'Enabled: Highest protection, source routing is completely disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting) IP source routing protection level\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting) IP source routing protection level' is set to Enabled: Highest protection, source routing is completely disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting) IP source routing protection level\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\nValue name: DisableIPSourceRouting\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting) IP source routing protection level\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (DisableIPSourceRouting) IP source routing protection level' is set to Enabled: Highest protection, source routing is completely disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\nValue name: DisableIPSourceRouting\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor could use source routed packets to obscure their identity and location.","Impact":"All incoming source routed packets will be dropped."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0220","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (DisableSavePassword) Prevent the dial-up password from being saved' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (DisableSavePassword) Prevent the dial-up password from being saved\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (DisableSavePassword) Prevent the dial-up password from being saved' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (DisableSavePassword) Prevent the dial-up password from being saved\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\RasMan\\Parameters\nValue name: DisableSavePassword\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (DisableSavePassword) Prevent the dial-up password from being saved\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (DisableSavePassword) Prevent the dial-up password from being saved' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\RasMan\\Parameters\nValue name: DisableSavePassword\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing dial-up and VPN passwords being saved, so that a lost or compromised device does not give automatic access to remote networks.","Impact":"Users must enter their password each time they start a dial-up or VPN connection."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0221","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (EnableICMPRedirect) Allow ICMP redirects to override OSPF generated routes' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (EnableICMPRedirect) Allow ICMP redirects to override OSPF generated routes\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (EnableICMPRedirect) Allow ICMP redirects to override OSPF generated routes' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (EnableICMPRedirect) Allow ICMP redirects to override OSPF generated routes\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\nValue name: EnableICMPRedirect\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Under Computer Configuration, inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (EnableICMPRedirect) Allow ICMP redirects to override OSPF generated routes\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (EnableICMPRedirect) Allow ICMP redirects to override OSPF generated routes' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\nValue name: EnableICMPRedirect\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends ignoring ICMP redirects to reduce malicious routing changes and disruption of normal network connectivity.","Impact":"Networks relying on ICMP route redirection may require routing changes; validate RRAS/OSPF routing and connectivity before applying."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0222","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (KeepAliveTime) How often keep-alive packets are sent in milliseconds' is set to 'Enabled: 300,000 or 5 minutes'","Expected":"Equals: 300000","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (KeepAliveTime) How often keep-alive packets are sent in milliseconds\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (KeepAliveTime) How often keep-alive packets are sent in milliseconds' is set to Enabled: 300,000 or 5 minutes.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (KeepAliveTime) How often keep-alive packets are sent in milliseconds\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\nValue name: KeepAliveTime\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 300000 (decimal; 0x000493E0)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (KeepAliveTime) How often keep-alive packets are sent in milliseconds\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (KeepAliveTime) How often keep-alive packets are sent in milliseconds' is set to Enabled: 300,000 or 5 minutes.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\nValue name: KeepAliveTime\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 300000 (decimal; 0x000493E0)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor who is able to connect to network applications could establish numerous connections to cause a DoS condition.","Impact":"Keep-alive packets are not sent by default by Windows. However, some applications may configure the TCP stack flag that requests keep-alive packets."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0223","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (NoNameReleaseOnDemand) Allow the computer to ignore NetBIOS name release requests except from WINS servers' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (NoNameReleaseOnDemand) Allow the computer to ignore NetBIOS name release requests except from WINS servers\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (NoNameReleaseOnDemand) Allow the computer to ignore NetBIOS name release requests except from WINS servers' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (NoNameReleaseOnDemand) Allow the computer to ignore NetBIOS name release requests except from WINS servers\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\NetBT\\Parameters\nValue name: NoNameReleaseOnDemand\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (NoNameReleaseOnDemand) Allow the computer to ignore NetBIOS name release requests except from WINS servers\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (NoNameReleaseOnDemand) Allow the computer to ignore NetBIOS name release requests except from WINS servers' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\NetBT\\Parameters\nValue name: NoNameReleaseOnDemand\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The NetBT protocol is designed not to use authentication, and is therefore vulnerable to spoofing.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0224","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (PerformRouterDiscovery) Allow IRDP to detect and configure Default Gateway addresses' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (PerformRouterDiscovery) Allow IRDP to detect and configure Default Gateway addresses\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (PerformRouterDiscovery) Allow IRDP to detect and configure Default Gateway addresses' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (PerformRouterDiscovery) Allow IRDP to detect and configure Default Gateway addresses\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\nValue name: PerformRouterDiscovery\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (PerformRouterDiscovery) Allow IRDP to detect and configure Default Gateway addresses\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (PerformRouterDiscovery) Allow IRDP to detect and configure Default Gateway addresses' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\nValue name: PerformRouterDiscovery\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor who has gained control of a computer on the same network segment could configure a computer on the network to impersonate a router.","Impact":"Windows will not automatically detect and configure default gateway addresses on the computer."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0225","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (SafeDllSearchMode) Enable Safe DLL search mode' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (SafeDllSearchMode) Enable Safe DLL search mode\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (SafeDllSearchMode) Enable Safe DLL search mode' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (SafeDllSearchMode) Enable Safe DLL search mode\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\nValue name: SafeDllSearchMode\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (SafeDllSearchMode) Enable Safe DLL search mode\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (SafeDllSearchMode) Enable Safe DLL search mode' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Control\\Session Manager\nValue name: SafeDllSearchMode\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If a user unknowingly executes hostile code that was packaged with additional files that include modified versions of system DLLs, the hostile code could load its own versions of those DLLs and potentially increase the type and degree of damage the code can render.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0226","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (TcpMaxDataRetransmissions IPv6) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted' is set to 'Enabled: 3'","Expected":"Equals: 3","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS:(TcpMaxDataRetransmissions IPv6) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (TcpMaxDataRetransmissions IPv6) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted' is set to Enabled: 3.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS:(TcpMaxDataRetransmissions IPv6) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\TCPIP6\\Parameters\nValue name: TcpMaxDataRetransmissions\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS:(TcpMaxDataRetransmissions IPv6) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (TcpMaxDataRetransmissions IPv6) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted' is set to Enabled: 3.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\TCPIP6\\Parameters\nValue name: TcpMaxDataRetransmissions\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor could exhaust a target computer's resources if it never sent any acknowledgment messages for data that was transmitted by the target computer.","Impact":"TCP starts a retransmission timer when each outbound segment is passed to the IP."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0227","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (TcpMaxDataRetransmissions) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted' is set to 'Enabled: 3'","Expected":"Equals: 3","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS:(TcpMaxDataRetransmissions) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (TcpMaxDataRetransmissions) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted' is set to Enabled: 3.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS:(TcpMaxDataRetransmissions) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\nValue name: TcpMaxDataRetransmissions\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS:(TcpMaxDataRetransmissions) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (TcpMaxDataRetransmissions) How many times unacknowledged data is retransmitted' is set to Enabled: 3.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Tcpip\\Parameters\nValue name: TcpMaxDataRetransmissions\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor could exhaust a target computer's resources if it never sent any acknowledgment messages for data that was transmitted by the target computer.","Impact":"TCP starts a retransmission timer when each outbound segment is passed to the IP."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0228","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'MSS: (WarningLevel) Percentage threshold for the security event log at which the system will generate a warning' is set to 'Enabled: 90% or less'","Expected":"Range: 0 | 90","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (WarningLevel) Percentage threshold for the security event log at which the system will generate a warning\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (WarningLevel) Percentage threshold for the security event log at which the system will generate a warning' is set to Enabled: 90% or less.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (WarningLevel) Percentage threshold for the security event log at which the system will generate a warning\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Eventlog\\Security\nValue name: WarningLevel\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 through 90, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\MSS (Legacy)\\MSS: (WarningLevel) Percentage threshold for the security event log at which the system will generate a warning\n\nCheck that 'MSS: (WarningLevel) Percentage threshold for the security event log at which the system will generate a warning' is set to Enabled: 90% or less.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\Eventlog\\Security\nValue name: WarningLevel\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 through 90, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If the Security log reaches 90 percent of its capacity and the computer has not been configured to overwrite events as needed, more recent events will not be written to the log.","Impact":"An audit event will be generated when the Security log reaches the 90% percent full threshold (or whatever lower value may be set) unless the log is configured to overwrite events as needed."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0229","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure multicast DNS (mDNS) protocol' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Configure multicast DNS (mDNS) protocol\n\nCheck that 'Configure multicast DNS (mDNS) protocol' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Configure multicast DNS (mDNS) protocol\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient\nValue name: EnableMDNS\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Configure multicast DNS (mDNS) protocol\n\nCheck that 'Configure multicast DNS (mDNS) protocol' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient\nValue name: EnableMDNS\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor can listen on a network over UDP port 5353 and respond to them, tricking the host into thinking that it knows the location of the requested system.","Impact":"In the event DNS is unavailable a system will be unable to request it from other systems on the same subnet."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0230","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure NetBIOS settings' is set to 'Enabled: Disable NetBIOS name resolution on public networks'","Expected":"One of: 0 | 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Configure NetBIOS settings\n\nCheck that 'Configure NetBIOS settings' is set to Enabled: Disable NetBIOS name resolution on public networks.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Configure NetBIOS settings\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient\nValue name: EnableNetbios\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 0 (decimal; 0x00000000), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Configure NetBIOS settings\n\nCheck that 'Configure NetBIOS settings' is set to Enabled: Disable NetBIOS name resolution on public networks.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient\nValue name: EnableNetbios\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 0 (decimal; 0x00000000), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"NetBIOS does not perform authentication and can allow remote threat actors to cause a denial-of-service by sending spoofed Name Conflicts or Name Release datagrams.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0231","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off default IPv6 DNS Servers' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Turn off default IPv6 DNS Servers\n\nCheck that 'Turn off default IPv6 DNS Servers' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Turn off default IPv6 DNS Servers\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient\nValue name: DisableIPv6DefaultDnsServers\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Turn off default IPv6 DNS Servers\n\nCheck that 'Turn off default IPv6 DNS Servers' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient\nValue name: DisableIPv6DefaultDnsServers\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Since the vast majority of private enterprise managed networks have no need to utilize IPv6 (because they have access to private IPv4 addressing), disabling the use of IPv6 DNS server addresses removes a possible attack surface that is also harder to monitor the traffic on.","Impact":"Default IPv6 DNS server addresses will not be utilized by Windows."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0232","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off multicast name resolution' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Turn off multicast name resolution\n\nCheck that 'Turn off multicast name resolution' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Turn off multicast name resolution\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient\nValue name: EnableMulticast\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\DNS Client\\Turn off multicast name resolution\n\nCheck that 'Turn off multicast name resolution' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient\nValue name: EnableMulticast\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actors can listen on a network for these LLMNR (UDP/5355) or NBT-NS (UDP/137) broadcasts and respond to them, tricking the host into thinking that it knows the location of the requested system.","Impact":"In the event DNS is unavailable a system will be unable to request it from other systems on the same subnet."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0233","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable Font Providers' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Fonts\\Enable Font Providers\n\nCheck that 'Enable Font Providers' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Fonts\\Enable Font Providers\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: EnableFontProviders\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Fonts\\Enable Font Providers\n\nCheck that 'Enable Font Providers' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: EnableFontProviders\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In an enterprise managed environment the IT department should be managing the changes to the system configuration, to ensure all changes are tested and approved.","Impact":"Windows will not connect to an online font provider and will only enumerate locallyinstalled fonts."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0234","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Audit client does not support encryption' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Audit client does not support encryption\n\nCheck that 'Audit client does not support encryption' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Audit client does not support encryption\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanServer\nValue name: AuditClientDoesNotSupportEncryption\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Audit client does not support encryption\n\nCheck that 'Audit client does not support encryption' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanServer\nValue name: AuditClientDoesNotSupportEncryption\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Organizations should be aware of all unencrypted SMB traffic in their environment.","Impact":"All SMB traffic that is unencrypted will be logged as an event."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0235","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Audit client does not support signing' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Audit client does not support signing\n\nCheck that 'Audit client does not support signing' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Audit client does not support signing\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanServer\nValue name: AuditClientDoesNotSupportSigning\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Audit client does not support signing\n\nCheck that 'Audit client does not support signing' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanServer\nValue name: AuditClientDoesNotSupportSigning\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"Ch