{"Id":"CHECK_0236","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Audit insecure guest logon' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Audit insecure guest logon\n\nCheck that 'Audit insecure guest logon' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Audit insecure guest logon\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanServer\nValue name: AuditInsecureGuestLogon\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Audit insecure guest logon\n\nCheck that 'Audit insecure guest logon' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanServer\nValue name: AuditInsecureGuestLogon\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Insecure guest logons can be used by file servers to allow unauthenticated access to shared folders.","Impact":"All insecure guest logons will be logged as an event."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0237","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable authentication rate limiter' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Enable authentication rate limiter\n\nCheck that 'Enable authentication rate limiter' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Enable authentication rate limiter\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanServer\nValue name: EnableAuthRateLimiter\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Enable authentication rate limiter\n\nCheck that 'Enable authentication rate limiter' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanServer\nValue name: EnableAuthRateLimiter\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Authentication rate limiter considerably reduces the risk of brute force attacks by implementing a 2-second delay (default) between each failed NTLM or PKU2U-based authentication attempt.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0238","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable remote mailslots' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Enable remote mailslots\n\nCheck that 'Enable remote mailslots' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Enable remote mailslots\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Bowser\nValue name: EnableMailslots\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Enable remote mailslots\n\nCheck that 'Enable remote mailslots' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Bowser\nValue name: EnableMailslots\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Remote mailslots is a legacy protocol that uses SMBv1 to function.","Impact":"If the remote mailslots feature was in operation, it will no longer function."},
