{"Id":"CHECK_0240","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Set authentication rate limiter delay (milliseconds)' is set to 'Enabled: 2000' or more","Expected":"At least: 2000","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Set authentication rate limiter delay (milliseconds)\n\nCheck that 'Set authentication rate limiter delay (milliseconds)' is set to Enabled: 2000.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Set authentication rate limiter delay (milliseconds)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanServer\nValue name: InvalidAuthenticationDelayTimeInMs\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: at least 2000 (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Server\\Set authentication rate limiter delay (milliseconds)\n\nCheck that 'Set authentication rate limiter delay (milliseconds)' is set to Enabled: 2000.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanServer\nValue name: InvalidAuthenticationDelayTimeInMs\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: at least 2000 (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Authentication rate limiter considerably reduces the risk of brute force attacks by implementing a 2-second delay (default) between each failed NTLM or PKU2U-based authentication attempt.","Impact":"None - 2,000 milliseconds (2 seconds) is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0241","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Audit insecure guest logon' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Workstation\\Audit insecure guest logon\n\nCheck that 'Audit insecure guest logon' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Workstation\\Audit insecure guest logon\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanWorkstation\nValue name: AuditInsecureGuestLogon\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Lanman Workstation\\Audit insecure guest logon\n\nCheck that 'Audit insecure guest logon' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LanmanWorkstation\nValue name: AuditInsecureGuestLogon\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Insecure guest logons can be used by file servers to allow unauthenticated access to shared folders.","Impact":"All insecure guest logons will be logged as an event."},
