{"Id":"CHECK_0248","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn on Mapper I/O (LLTDIO) driver' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Link-Layer Topology Discovery\\Turn on Mapper I/O (LLTDIO) driver\n\nCheck that 'Turn on Mapper I/O (LLTDIO) driver' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Link-Layer Topology Discovery\\Turn on Mapper I/O (LLTDIO) driver\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: AllowLLTDIOOnDomain\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: AllowLLTDIOOnPublicNet\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: EnableLLTDIO\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: ProhibitLLTDIOOnPrivateNet\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nEvery listed value must match its expected type and data.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Link-Layer Topology Discovery\\Turn on Mapper I/O (LLTDIO) driver\n\nCheck that 'Turn on Mapper I/O (LLTDIO) driver' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: AllowLLTDIOOnDomain\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: AllowLLTDIOOnPublicNet\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: EnableLLTDIO\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: ProhibitLLTDIOOnPrivateNet\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling topology mapping to reduce unnecessary network discovery and interaction with unauthorised devices.","Impact":"Policy application may briefly disrupt network connectivity and real-time applications; network topology mapping will no longer be available."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0249","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn on Responder (RSPNDR) driver' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Link-Layer Topology Discovery\\Turn on Responder (RSPNDR) driver\n\nCheck that 'Turn on Responder (RSPNDR) driver' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Link-Layer Topology Discovery\\Turn on Responder (RSPNDR) driver\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: AllowRspndrOnDomain\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: AllowRspndrOnPublicNet\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: EnableRspndr\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: ProhibitRspndrOnPrivateNet\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nEvery listed value must match its expected type and data.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Link-Layer Topology Discovery\\Turn on Responder (RSPNDR) driver\n\nCheck that 'Turn on Responder (RSPNDR) driver' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: AllowRspndrOnDomain\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: AllowRspndrOnPublicNet\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: EnableRspndr\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LLTD\nValue name: ProhibitRspndrOnPrivateNet\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling topology-discovery responses to reduce unnecessary network discovery and interaction with unauthorised devices.","Impact":"Policy application may briefly disrupt network connectivity and real-time applications; the host will no longer respond to topology-discovery requests."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0250","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Microsoft Peer-to-Peer Networking Services' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Microsoft Peer-to-Peer Networking Services\\Turn off Microsoft Peer-to-Peer Networking Services\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Microsoft Peer-to-Peer Networking Services' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Microsoft Peer-to-Peer Networking Services\\Turn off Microsoft Peer-to-Peer Networking Services\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Peernet\nValue name: Disabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Microsoft Peer-to-Peer Networking Services\\Turn off Microsoft Peer-to-Peer Networking Services\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Microsoft Peer-to-Peer Networking Services' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Peernet\nValue name: Disabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting enhances the security of the environment and reduces the overall risk exposure related to peer-to-peer networking.","Impact":"Microsoft Peer-to-Peer Networking Services are turned off in their entirety, and all applications dependent on them will stop working."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0251","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prohibit installation and configuration of Network Bridge on your DNS domain network' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Connections\\Prohibit installation and configuration of Network Bridge on your DNS domain network\n\nCheck that 'Prohibit installation and configuration of Network Bridge on your DNS domain network' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Connections\\Prohibit installation and configuration of Network Bridge on your DNS domain network\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Network Connections\nValue name: NC_AllowNetBridge_NLA\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Connections\\Prohibit installation and configuration of Network Bridge on your DNS domain network\n\nCheck that 'Prohibit installation and configuration of Network Bridge on your DNS domain network' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Network Connections\nValue name: NC_AllowNetBridge_NLA\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The Network Bridge setting, if enabled, allows users to create a Layer 2 Media Access Control (MAC) bridge, enabling them to connect two or more physical network segments together.","Impact":"Users cannot create or configure a Network Bridge."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0252","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prohibit use of Internet Connection Sharing on your DNS domain network' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Connections\\Prohibit use of Internet Connection Sharing on your DNS domain network\n\nCheck that 'Prohibit use of Internet Connection Sharing on your DNS domain network' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Connections\\Prohibit use of Internet Connection Sharing on your DNS domain network\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Network Connections\nValue name: NC_ShowSharedAccessUI\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Connections\\Prohibit use of Internet Connection Sharing on your DNS domain network\n\nCheck that 'Prohibit use of Internet Connection Sharing on your DNS domain network' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Network Connections\nValue name: NC_ShowSharedAccessUI\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Non-administrators should not be able to turn on the Mobile Hotspot feature and open their Internet connectivity up to nearby mobile devices.","Impact":"Mobile Hotspot cannot be enabled or configured by Administrators and nonAdministrators alike."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0253","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Require domain users to elevate when setting a network's location' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Connections\\Require domain users to elevate when setting a network's location\n\nCheck that 'Require domain users to elevate when setting a network's location' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Connections\\Require domain users to elevate when setting a network's location\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Network Connections\nValue name: NC_StdDomainUserSetLocation\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Connections\\Require domain users to elevate when setting a network's location\n\nCheck that 'Require domain users to elevate when setting a network's location' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Network Connections\nValue name: NC_StdDomainUserSetLocation\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Allowing regular users to set a network location increases the risk and attack surface.","Impact":"Domain users must elevate when setting a network's location."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0254","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Hardened UNC Paths' is set to 'Enabled, with \"Require Mutual Authentication\", \"Require Integrity\", and \"Require Privacy\" set for all NETLOGON and SYSVOL shares'","Expected":"RequireMutualAuthentication=1, RequireIntegrity=1, RequirePrivacy=1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Provider\\Hardened UNC Paths\n\nCheck that 'Hardened UNC Paths' is set to Enabled, with \"Require Mutual Authentication\", \"Require Integrity\", and \"Require Privacy\" set for all NETLOGON and SYSVOL shares.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Provider\\Hardened UNC Paths\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\NetworkProvider\\HardenedPaths\nValue name: \\\\*\\NETLOGON\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: RequireMutualAuthentication=1, RequireIntegrity=1, RequirePrivacy=1; exactly one occurrence of each required key, with no duplicate keys\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\NetworkProvider\\HardenedPaths\nValue name: \\\\*\\SYSVOL\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: RequireMutualAuthentication=1, RequireIntegrity=1, RequirePrivacy=1; exactly one occurrence of each required key, with no duplicate keys\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Network Provider\\Hardened UNC Paths\n\nCheck that 'Hardened UNC Paths' is set to Enabled, with \"Require Mutual Authentication\", \"Require Integrity\", and \"Require Privacy\" set for all NETLOGON and SYSVOL shares.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\NetworkProvider\\HardenedPaths\nValue name: \\\\*\\NETLOGON\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: RequireMutualAuthentication=1, RequireIntegrity=1, RequirePrivacy=1; exactly one occurrence of each required key, with no duplicate keys\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\NetworkProvider\\HardenedPaths\nValue name: \\\\*\\SYSVOL\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: RequireMutualAuthentication=1, RequireIntegrity=1, RequirePrivacy=1; exactly one occurrence of each required key, with no duplicate keys\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In February 2015, Microsoft released a new control mechanism to mitigate a remote code execution vulnerability (MS15-011 / MSKB 3000483) in how Group Policy receives and applies connection data when a domain-joined system connects to a domain controller.","Impact":"Windows only allows access to the specified UNC paths after fulfilling additional security requirements."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0255","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"Disable IPv6 (Ensure TCPIP6 Parameter 'DisabledComponents' is set to '0xff (255)')","Expected":"Equals: 255","Steps":"Registry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Services\\TCPIP6\\Parameters\nValue name: DisabledComponents\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 255 (decimal; 0x000000FF)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"Registry verification","Rationale":"Since the vast majority of private enterprise managed networks have no need to utilize IPv6 (because they have access to private IPv4 addressing), disabling IPv6 components removes a possible attack surface that is also harder to monitor the traffic on.","Impact":"Connectivity to other systems using IPv6 will no longer operate, and software that depends on IPv6 will cease to function."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0256","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configuration of wireless settings using Windows Connect Now' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connect Now\\Configuration of wireless settings using Windows Connect Now\n\nCheck that 'Configuration of wireless settings using Windows Connect Now' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connect Now\\Configuration of wireless settings using Windows Connect Now\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\Registrars\nValue name: EnableRegistrars\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\Registrars\nValue name: DisableUPnPRegistrar\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\Registrars\nValue name: DisableInBand802DOT11Registrar\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\Registrars\nValue name: DisableFlashConfigRegistrar\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\Registrars\nValue name: DisableWPDRegistrar\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connect Now\\Configuration of wireless settings using Windows Connect Now\n\nCheck that 'Configuration of wireless settings using Windows Connect Now' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\Registrars\nValue name: EnableRegistrars\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\Registrars\nValue name: DisableUPnPRegistrar\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\Registrars\nValue name: DisableInBand802DOT11Registrar\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\Registrars\nValue name: DisableFlashConfigRegistrar\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\Registrars\nValue name: DisableWPDRegistrar\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting enhances the security of the environment and reduces the overall risk exposure related to user configuration of wireless settings.","Impact":"WCN operations are disabled over all media."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0257","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prohibit access of the Windows Connect Now wizards' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connect Now\\Prohibit access of the Windows Connect Now wizards\n\nCheck that 'Prohibit access of the Windows Connect Now wizards' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connect Now\\Prohibit access of the Windows Connect Now wizards\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\UI\nValue name: DisableWcnUi\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connect Now\\Prohibit access of the Windows Connect Now wizards\n\nCheck that 'Prohibit access of the Windows Connect Now wizards' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WCN\\UI\nValue name: DisableWcnUi\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Allowing standard users to access the Windows Connect Now wizard increases the risk and attack surface.","Impact":"The WCN wizards are turned off and users have no access to any of the wizard tasks."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0258","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Minimize the number of simultaneous connections to the Internet or a Windows Domain' is set to 'Enabled: 3 = Prevent Wi-Fi when on Ethernet'","Expected":"Equals: 3","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connection Manager\\Minimize the number of simultaneous connections to the Internet or a Windows Domain\n\nCheck that 'Minimize the number of simultaneous connections to the Internet or a Windows Domain' is set to Enabled: 3 = Prevent Wi-Fi when on Ethernet.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connection Manager\\Minimize the number of simultaneous connections to the Internet or a Windows Domain\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WcmSvc\\GroupPolicy\nValue name: fMinimizeConnections\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connection Manager\\Minimize the number of simultaneous connections to the Internet or a Windows Domain\n\nCheck that 'Minimize the number of simultaneous connections to the Internet or a Windows Domain' is set to Enabled: 3 = Prevent Wi-Fi when on Ethernet.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WcmSvc\\GroupPolicy\nValue name: fMinimizeConnections\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Preventing bridged network connections can help prevent a user unknowingly allowing traffic to route between internal and external networks, which risks exposure to sensitive internal data.","Impact":"While connected to an Ethernet connection, Windows won't allow use of a WLAN (automatically or manually) until Ethernet is disconnected."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0259","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prohibit connection to non-domain networks when connected to domain authenticated network' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connection Manager\\Prohibit connection to non-domain networks when connected to domain authenticated network\n\nCheck that 'Prohibit connection to non-domain networks when connected to domain authenticated network' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connection Manager\\Prohibit connection to non-domain networks when connected to domain authenticated network\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WcmSvc\\GroupPolicy\nValue name: fBlockNonDomain\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\Windows Connection Manager\\Prohibit connection to non-domain networks when connected to domain authenticated network\n\nCheck that 'Prohibit connection to non-domain networks when connected to domain authenticated network' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WcmSvc\\GroupPolicy\nValue name: fBlockNonDomain\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The potential concern is that a user would unknowingly allow network traffic to flow between the insecure public network and the enterprise managed network.","Impact":"The computer responds to automatic and manual network connection attempts based on the following circumstances: Automatic connection attempts - When the computer is already connected to a domain based network, all automatic connection attempts to non-domain networks are blocked. - When the computer is already connected to a non-domain based network, automatic connection attempts to domain based networks are blocked."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0260","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Windows to automatically connect to suggested open hotspots, to networks shared by contacts, and to hotspots offering paid services' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\WLAN Service\\WLAN Settings\\Allow Windows to automatically connect to suggested open hotspots, to networks shared by contacts, and to hotspots offering paid services\n\nCheck that 'Allow Windows to automatically connect to suggested open hotspots, to networks shared by contacts, and to hotspots offering paid services' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\WLAN Service\\WLAN Settings\\Allow Windows to automatically connect to suggested open hotspots, to networks shared by contacts, and to hotspots offering paid services\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\WcmSvc\\wifinetworkmanager\\config\nValue name: AutoConnectAllowedOEM\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Network\\WLAN Service\\WLAN Settings\\Allow Windows to automatically connect to suggested open hotspots, to networks shared by contacts, and to hotspots offering paid services\n\nCheck that 'Allow Windows to automatically connect to suggested open hotspots, to networks shared by contacts, and to hotspots offering paid services' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\WcmSvc\\wifinetworkmanager\\config\nValue name: AutoConnectAllowedOEM\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing automatic connection to suggested open hotspots and shared networks, which could place the device on an untrusted or attacker-controlled network without the user noticing.","Impact":"The device will not automatically join suggested open hotspots, networks shared by contacts or paid hotspot services; users can still connect manually."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0261","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Print Spooler to accept client connections' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Allow Print Spooler to accept client connections\n\nCheck that 'Allow Print Spooler to accept client connections' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Allow Print Spooler to accept client connections\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\nValue name: RegisterSpoolerRemoteRpcEndPoint\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Under Computer Configuration, inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Allow Print Spooler to accept client connections\n\nCheck that 'Allow Print Spooler to accept client connections' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\nValue name: RegisterSpoolerRemoteRpcEndPoint\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling unnecessary Spooler client connections to reduce exposure to remote PrintNightmare and other print-service attacks.","Impact":"Other computers cannot print through this workstation once the policy takes effect; local printing remains possible if Spooler stays enabled. Workstations that share printers require an exception."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0262","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure Redirection Guard' is set to 'Enabled: Redirection Guard Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Configure Redirection Guard\n\nCheck that 'Configure Redirection Guard' is set to Enabled: Redirection Guard Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Configure Redirection Guard\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\nValue name: RedirectionguardPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Configure Redirection Guard\n\nCheck that 'Configure Redirection Guard' is set to Enabled: Redirection Guard Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\nValue name: RedirectionguardPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting prevents non-administrators from redirecting files within the print spooler process.","Impact":"None - this is default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0263","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure RPC connection settings: Protocol to use for outgoing RPC connections' is set to 'Enabled: RPC over TCP'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Configure RPC connection settings: Protocol to use for outgoing RPC connections\n\nCheck that 'Configure RPC connection settings: Protocol to use for outgoing RPC connections' is set to Enabled: RPC over TCP.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Configure RPC connection settings: Protocol to use for outgoing RPC connections\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\\RPC\nValue name: RpcUseNamedPipeProtocol\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Configure RPC connection settings: Protocol to use for outgoing RPC connections\n\nCheck that 'Configure RPC connection settings: Protocol to use for outgoing RPC connections' is set to Enabled: RPC over TCP.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\\RPC\nValue name: RpcUseNamedPipeProtocol\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting prevents the use of named pipes for RPC connections to the print spooler and forces the use of TCP which is a more secure communication method.","Impact":"Warning: Many existing print configurations may be using the older named pipes protocol and therefore will cease to function."},
