{"Id":"CHECK_0272","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Point and Print Restrictions: When installing drivers for a new connection' is set to 'Enabled: Show warning and elevation prompt'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Point and Print Restrictions: When installing drivers for a new connection\n\nCheck that 'Point and Print Restrictions: When installing drivers for a new connection' is set to Enabled: Show warning and elevation prompt.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Point and Print Restrictions: When installing drivers for a new connection\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\\PointAndPrint\nValue name: NoWarningNoElevationOnInstall\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Point and Print Restrictions: When installing drivers for a new connection\n\nCheck that 'Point and Print Restrictions: When installing drivers for a new connection' is set to Enabled: Show warning and elevation prompt.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\\PointAndPrint\nValue name: NoWarningNoElevationOnInstall\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Enabling Windows User Account Control (UAC) for the installation of new print drivers can help mitigate the PrintNightmare vulnerability (CVE-2021-34527) and other Print Spooler attacks.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0273","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Point and Print Restrictions: When updating drivers for an existing connection' is set to 'Enabled: Show warning and elevation prompt'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Point and Print Restrictions: When updating drivers for an existing connection\n\nCheck that 'Point and Print Restrictions: When updating drivers for an existing connection' is set to Enabled: Show warning and elevation prompt.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Point and Print Restrictions: When updating drivers for an existing connection\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\\PointAndPrint\nValue name: UpdatePromptSettings\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Point and Print Restrictions: When updating drivers for an existing connection\n\nCheck that 'Point and Print Restrictions: When updating drivers for an existing connection' is set to Enabled: Show warning and elevation prompt.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\\PointAndPrint\nValue name: UpdatePromptSettings\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Enabling Windows User Account Control (UAC) for updating existing print drivers can help mitigate the PrintNightmare vulnerability (CVE-2021-34527) and other Print Spooler attacks.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0274","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Require IPPS for IPP printers' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Require IPPS for IPP printers\n\nCheck that 'Require IPPS for IPP printers' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Require IPPS for IPP printers\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\\IPP\nValue name: RequireIpps\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Require IPPS for IPP printers\n\nCheck that 'Require IPPS for IPP printers' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\\IPP\nValue name: RequireIpps\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"To prevent interception or tampering with printer data, IPPS encrypts all communication between the client and the printer.","Impact":"IPP printers which use self-signed or locally issued certificates will be affected and may not function properly."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0275","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Set TLS/SSL security policy for IPP printers: Disallow invalid certificate authority' is set to 'Enabled: Checked'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Set TLS/SSL security policy for IPP printers: Disallow invalid certificate authority\n\nCheck that 'Set TLS/SSL security policy for IPP printers: Disallow invalid certificate authority' is set to Enabled: Checked.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Set TLS/SSL security policy for IPP printers: Disallow invalid certificate authority\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\\IPP\nValue name: SecurityFlagsBlockUnknownCA\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Printers\\Set TLS/SSL security policy for IPP printers: Disallow invalid certificate authority\n\nCheck that 'Set TLS/SSL security policy for IPP printers: Disallow invalid certificate authority' is set to Enabled: Checked.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\\IPP\nValue name: SecurityFlagsBlockUnknownCA\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Certificate validation helps prevent spoofed or unauthorized printers, reduces the risk of credential theft, and protects sensitive print jobs from being redirected.","Impact":"The system enforces certificate validation and blocks printing whenever certificate errors are detected."},
