{"Id":"CHECK_0279","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off notifications network usage' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Start Menu and Taskbar\\Turn off notifications network usage\n\nCheck that 'Turn off notifications network usage' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Start Menu and Taskbar\\Turn off notifications network usage\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\PushNotifications\nValue name: NoCloudApplicationNotification\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Start Menu and Taskbar\\Turn off notifications network usage\n\nCheck that 'Turn off notifications network usage' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\PushNotifications\nValue name: NoCloudApplicationNotification\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Windows Push Notification Services (WNS) is a mechanism to receive third-party notifications and updates from the cloud/Internet.","Impact":"Applications and system features will not be able receive notifications from the network from WNS or via notification polling APIs."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0280","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Remove Personalized Website Recommendations from the Recommended section in the Start Menu' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Start Menu and Taskbar\\Remove Personalized Website Recommendations from the Recommended section in the Start Menu\n\nCheck that 'Remove Personalized Website Recommendations from the Recommended section in the Start Menu' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Start Menu and Taskbar\\Remove Personalized Website Recommendations from the Recommended section in the Start Menu\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: HideRecommendedPersonalizedSites\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Start Menu and Taskbar\\Remove Personalized Website Recommendations from the Recommended section in the Start Menu\n\nCheck that 'Remove Personalized Website Recommendations from the Recommended section in the Start Menu' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: HideRecommendedPersonalizedSites\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends removing personalised website recommendations from the Start menu to limit use of browsing history for suggestions and reduce distraction with externally sourced content.","Impact":"Personalised website recommendations are no longer shown in the Recommended section of the Start menu."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0281","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Include command line in process creation events' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Audit Process Creation\\Include command line in process creation events\n\nCheck that 'Include command line in process creation events' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Audit Process Creation\\Include command line in process creation events\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\Audit\nValue name: ProcessCreationIncludeCmdLine_Enabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Audit Process Creation\\Include command line in process creation events\n\nCheck that 'Include command line in process creation events' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\Audit\nValue name: ProcessCreationIncludeCmdLine_Enabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Capturing process command line information in event logs can be very valuable when performing forensic investigations of attack incidents.","Impact":"Process command line information will be included in the event logs, which can contain sensitive or private information such as passwords or user data."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0282","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Encryption Oracle Remediation' is set to 'Enabled: Force Updated Clients'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Credentials Delegation\\Encryption Oracle Remediation\n\nCheck that 'Encryption Oracle Remediation' is set to Enabled: Force Updated Clients.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Credentials Delegation\\Encryption Oracle Remediation\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\CredSSP\\Parameters\nValue name: AllowEncryptionOracle\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Credentials Delegation\\Encryption Oracle Remediation\n\nCheck that 'Encryption Oracle Remediation' is set to Enabled: Force Updated Clients.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\CredSSP\\Parameters\nValue name: AllowEncryptionOracle\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting is important to mitigate the CredSSP encryption oracle vulnerability, for which information was published by Microsoft on 03/13/2018 in CVE-2018-0886 | CredSSP Remote Code Execution Vulnerability.","Impact":"Client applications which use CredSSP will not be able to fall back to the insecure versions and services using CredSSP will not accept unpatched clients."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0283","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Remote host allows delegation of non-exportable credentials' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Credentials Delegation\\Remote host allows delegation of non-exportable credentials\n\nCheck that 'Remote host allows delegation of non-exportable credentials' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Credentials Delegation\\Remote host allows delegation of non-exportable credentials\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CredentialsDelegation\nValue name: AllowProtectedCreds\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Credentials Delegation\\Remote host allows delegation of non-exportable credentials\n\nCheck that 'Remote host allows delegation of non-exportable credentials' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CredentialsDelegation\nValue name: AllowProtectedCreds\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Restricted Admin Mode was designed to help protect administrator accounts by ensuring that reusable credentials are not stored in memory on remote devices that could potentially be compromised.","Impact":"The host will support the Restricted Admin Mode and Windows Defender Remote Credential Guard features."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0284","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn On Virtualization Based Security' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: EnableVirtualizationBasedSecurity\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Kerberos, NTLM, and Credential manager isolate secrets by using virtualization-based security.","Impact":"Warning: All drivers on the system must be compatible with this feature or the system may crash."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0285","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Select Platform Security Level' is set to 'Secure Boot' or higher","Expected":"One of: 1 | 3","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Select Platform Security Level\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Select Platform Security Level' is set to Secure Boot.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Select Platform Security Level\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: RequirePlatformSecurityFeatures\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Select Platform Security Level\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Select Platform Security Level' is set to Secure Boot.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: RequirePlatformSecurityFeatures\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Secure Boot can help reduce the risk of bootloader attacks and in conjunction with DMA protections to help protect data from being scraped from memory.","Impact":"Choosing the Secure Boot option provides the system with as much protection as is supported by the computer's hardware."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0286","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Virtualization Based Protection of Code Integrity' is set to 'Enabled with UEFI lock'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Virtualization Based Protection of Code Integrity\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Virtualization Based Protection of Code Integrity' is set to Enabled with UEFI lock.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Virtualization Based Protection of Code Integrity\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Virtualization Based Protection of Code Integrity\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Virtualization Based Protection of Code Integrity' is set to Enabled with UEFI lock.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: HypervisorEnforcedCodeIntegrity\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The Enabled with UEFI lock option ensures that Virtualization Based Protection of Code Integrity cannot be disabled remotely.","Impact":"Warning: All drivers on the system must be compatible with this feature or the system may crash."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0287","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Require UEFI Memory Attributes Table' is set to 'True (checked)'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Require UEFI Memory Attributes Table\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Require UEFI Memory Attributes Table' is set to True (checked).\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Require UEFI Memory Attributes Table\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: HVCIMATRequired\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Require UEFI Memory Attributes Table\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Require UEFI Memory Attributes Table' is set to True (checked).\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: HVCIMATRequired\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting will help protect this control from being enabled on a system that is not compatible which could lead to a crash or data loss.","Impact":"Warning: All drivers on the system must be compatible with this feature or the system may crash."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0288","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Credential Guard Configuration' is set to 'Enabled with UEFI lock'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Credential Guard Configuration\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Credential Guard Configuration' is set to Enabled with UEFI lock.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Credential Guard Configuration\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: LsaCfgFlags\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Credential Guard Configuration\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Credential Guard Configuration' is set to Enabled with UEFI lock.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: LsaCfgFlags\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The Enabled with UEFI lock option ensures that Credential Guard cannot be disabled remotely.","Impact":"Warning: All drivers on the system must be compatible with this feature or the system may crash."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0289","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Secure Launch Configuration' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Secure Launch Configuration\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Secure Launch Configuration' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Secure Launch Configuration\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: ConfigureSystemGuardLaunch\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Secure Launch Configuration\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Secure Launch Configuration' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: ConfigureSystemGuardLaunch\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Secure Launch changes the way windows boots to use Intel Trusted Execution Technology (TXT) and Runtime BIOS Resilience features to prevent firmware exploits from being able to impact the security of the Windows Virtualization Based Security environment.","Impact":"Warning: All drivers on the system must be compatible with this feature or the system may crash."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0290","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Kernel-mode Hardware-enforced Stack Protection' is set to 'Enabled: Enabled in enforcement mode'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Kernel-mode Hardware-enforced Stack Protection\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Kernel-mode Hardware-enforced Stack Protection' is set to Enabled: Enabled in enforcement mode.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Kernel-mode Hardware-enforced Stack Protection\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: ConfigureKernelShadowStacksLaunch\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Guard\\Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Kernel-mode Hardware-enforced Stack Protection\n\nCheck that 'Turn On Virtualization Based Security: Kernel-mode Hardware-enforced Stack Protection' is set to Enabled: Enabled in enforcement mode.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceGuard\nValue name: ConfigureKernelShadowStacksLaunch\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends enforcing kernel-mode hardware-enforced stack protection so that return-oriented programming attacks against kernel code are blocked on capable hardware.","Impact":"Requires compatible processors with virtualisation-based security and HVCI enabled; incompatible kernel drivers will be blocked from loading and may cause device or application failures."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0291","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Installation\\Device Installation Restrictions\\Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes\n\nCheck that 'Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Installation\\Device Installation Restrictions\\Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceInstall\\Restrictions\nValue name: DenyDeviceClasses\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Installation\\Device Installation Restrictions\\Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes\n\nCheck that 'Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceInstall\\Restrictions\nValue name: DenyDeviceClasses\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends blocking installation of IEEE 1394 (FireWire) class devices on BitLocker-protected systems, because these interfaces allow direct memory access that can be used to recover encryption keys.","Impact":"IEEE 1394 (FireWire) devices cannot be installed or used; devices already installed are removed where the retroactive option is set."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0292","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes: Prevent installation of devices using drivers for these device setup' is set to 'IEEE 1394 device setup classes'","Expected":"Contains: {d48179be-ec20-11d1-b6b8-00c04fa372a7} | {7ebefbc0-3200-11d2-b4c2-00a0C9697d07} | {c06ff265-ae09-48f0-812c-16753d7cba83} | {6bdd1fc1-810f-11d0-bec7-08002be2092f}","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Installation\\Device Installation Restrictions\\Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes\n\nCheck that 'Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes: Prevent installation of devices using drivers for these device setup' is set to IEEE 1394 device setup classes.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Installation\\Device Installation Restrictions\\Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceInstall\\Restrictions\\DenyDeviceClasses\nValue names: numbered entries (1, 2, 3, ...)\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: the entries together include every one of the following:\n- {d48179be-ec20-11d1-b6b8-00c04fa372a7}\n- {7ebefbc0-3200-11d2-b4c2-00a0C9697d07}\n- {c06ff265-ae09-48f0-812c-16753d7cba83}\n- {6bdd1fc1-810f-11d0-bec7-08002be2092f}\n\nRead the entries without changing them. A missing key remains unresolved.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Installation\\Device Installation Restrictions\\Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes\n\nCheck that 'Prevent installation of devices using drivers that match these device setup classes: Prevent installation of devices using drivers for these device setup' is set to IEEE 1394 device setup classes.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceInstall\\Restrictions\\DenyDeviceClasses\nValue names: numbered entries (1, 2, 3, ...)\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: the entries together include every one of the following:\n- {d48179be-ec20-11d1-b6b8-00c04fa372a7}\n- {7ebefbc0-3200-11d2-b4c2-00a0C9697d07}\n- {c06ff265-ae09-48f0-812c-16753d7cba83}\n- {6bdd1fc1-810f-11d0-bec7-08002be2092f}\n\nRead the entries without changing them. A missing key remains unresolved."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends blocking installation of IEEE 1394 (FireWire) class devices on BitLocker-protected systems, because these interfaces allow direct memory access that can be used to recover encryption keys.","Impact":"IEEE 1394 (FireWire) devices cannot be installed or used; devices already installed are removed where the retroactive option is set."},
