g drivers that match these device setup classes: Also apply to matching devices that are already installed.' is set to True (checked).\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeviceInstall\\Restrictions\nValue name: DenyDeviceClassesRetroactive\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends blocking installation of IEEE 1394 (FireWire) class devices on BitLocker-protected systems, because these interfaces allow direct memory access that can be used to recover encryption keys.","Impact":"IEEE 1394 (FireWire) devices cannot be installed or used; devices already installed are removed where the retroactive option is set."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0294","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prevent automatic download of applications associated with device metadata' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Installation\\Prevent automatic download of applications associated with device metadata\n\nCheck that 'Prevent automatic download of applications associated with device metadata' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Installation\\Prevent automatic download of applications associated with device metadata\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Device Metadata\nValue name: PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Device Installation\\Prevent automatic download of applications associated with device metadata\n\nCheck that 'Prevent automatic download of applications associated with device metadata' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Device Metadata\nValue name: PreventDeviceMetadataFromNetwork\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Installation of software should be conducted by an authorized system administrator and not a standard user.","Impact":"Standard users without administrator privileges will not be able to install associated third-party utility software for peripheral devices."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0295","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Boot-Start Driver Initialization Policy' is set to 'Enabled: Good, unknown and bad but critical'","Expected":"Equals: 3","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Early Launch Antimalware\\Boot-Start Driver Initialization Policy\n\nCheck that 'Boot-Start Driver Initialization Policy' is set to Enabled: Good, unknown and bad but critical.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Early Launch Antimalware\\Boot-Start Driver Initialization Policy\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Policies\\EarlyLaunch\nValue name: DriverLoadPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Early Launch Antimalware\\Boot-Start Driver Initialization Policy\n\nCheck that 'Boot-Start Driver Initialization Policy' is set to Enabled: Good, unknown and bad but critical.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Policies\\EarlyLaunch\nValue name: DriverLoadPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This policy setting helps reduce the impact of malware that has already infected your system.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0296","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable / disable CLFS logfile authentication' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Filesystem\\Enable / disable CLFS logfile authentication\n\nCheck that 'Enable / disable CLFS logfile authentication' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Filesystem\\Enable / disable CLFS logfile authentication\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Policies\nValue name: ClfsAuthenticationChecking\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Filesystem\\Enable / disable CLFS logfile authentication\n\nCheck that 'Enable / disable CLFS logfile authentication' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Policies\nValue name: ClfsAuthenticationChecking\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"CLFS is a security feature which hardens logfile parsing. If modifications to logfiles are detected, CLFS will consider the logfile unsafe for parsing and return an error to the caller.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. Warning: Logfiles that were created on other systems will fail to open as these contain authentication codes created using a system-unique cryptographic key."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0297","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure security policy processing: Do not apply during periodic background processing' is set to 'Enabled: FALSE'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Configure security policy processing\n\nCheck that 'Configure security policy processing: Do not apply during periodic background processing' is set to Enabled: FALSE.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Configure security policy processing\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Group Policy\\{827D319E-6EAC-11D2-A4EA-00C04F79F83A}\nValue name: NoBackgroundPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Configure security policy processing\n\nCheck that 'Configure security policy processing: Do not apply during periodic background processing' is set to Enabled: FALSE.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Group Policy\\{827D319E-6EAC-11D2-A4EA-00C04F79F83A}\nValue name: NoBackgroundPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Setting this option to false (unchecked) will ensure that domain security policy changes are applied more quickly, as compared to waiting until the next user logon or system restart.","Impact":"Built-in security template settings will be reapplied by Group Policy even when the system is in use, which may have a slight impact on performance."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0298","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure security policy processing: Process even if the Group Policy objects have not changed' is set to 'Enabled: TRUE'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Configure security policy processing\n\nCheck that 'Configure security policy processing: Process even if the Group Policy objects have not changed' is set to Enabled: TRUE.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Configure security policy processing\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Group Policy\\{827D319E-6EAC-11D2-A4EA-00C04F79F83A}\nValue name: NoGPOListChanges\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Configure security policy processing\n\nCheck that 'Configure security policy processing: Process even if the Group Policy objects have not changed' is set to Enabled: TRUE.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Group Policy\\{827D319E-6EAC-11D2-A4EA-00C04F79F83A}\nValue name: NoGPOListChanges\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Setting this option to true (checked) will ensure unauthorized local changes are reverted to match the domain-based Group Policy settings.","Impact":"Built-in security template settings will be reapplied even if they have not been changed, which may cause Group Policy refreshes to take longer."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0299","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Continue experiences on this device' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Continue experiences on this device\n\nCheck that 'Continue experiences on this device' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Continue experiences on this device\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: EnableCdp\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Continue experiences on this device\n\nCheck that 'Continue experiences on this device' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: EnableCdp\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A cross-device experience is when a system can access app and send messages to other devices.","Impact":"The Windows device will not be discoverable by other devices, and cannot participate in cross-device experiences."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0300","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off background refresh of Group Policy' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Value must be absent","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Turn off background refresh of Group Policy\n\nCheck that 'Turn off background refresh of Group Policy' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Turn off background refresh of Group Policy\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: DisableBkGndGroupPolicy\nExpected type: \nExpected data: the named value must not exist\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Group Policy\\Turn off background refresh of Group Policy\n\nCheck that 'Turn off background refresh of Group Policy' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: DisableBkGndGroupPolicy\nExpected type: \nExpected data: the named value must not exist\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting ensures that group policy changes take effect more quickly, as compared to waiting until the next user logon or system restart.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0301","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off access to the Store' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off access to the Store\n\nCheck that 'Turn off access to the Store' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off access to the Store\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: NoUseStoreOpenWith\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off access to the Store\n\nCheck that 'Turn off access to the Store' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: NoUseStoreOpenWith\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends removing the Microsoft Store option from the Open With dialog so that users are not directed to install unapproved applications for unknown file types.","Impact":"The \"Look for an app in the Store\" option is removed from the Open With dialog."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0302","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off downloading of print drivers over HTTP' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off downloading of print drivers over HTTP\n\nCheck that 'Turn off downloading of print drivers over HTTP' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off downloading of print drivers over HTTP\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\nValue name: DisableWebPnPDownload\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off downloading of print drivers over HTTP\n\nCheck that 'Turn off downloading of print drivers over HTTP' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\nValue name: DisableWebPnPDownload\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Users might download drivers that include malicious code.","Impact":"Print drivers cannot be downloaded over HTTP. Note: This policy setting does not prevent the client computer from printing to printers on the intranet or the Internet over HTTP."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0303","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off handwriting personalization data sharing' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off handwriting personalization data sharing\n\nCheck that 'Turn off handwriting personalization data sharing' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off handwriting personalization data sharing\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\TabletPC\nValue name: PreventHandwritingDataSharing\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off handwriting personalization data sharing\n\nCheck that 'Turn off handwriting personalization data sharing' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\TabletPC\nValue name: PreventHandwritingDataSharing\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A person's handwriting is Personally Identifiable Information (PII), especially when it comes to your signature.","Impact":"Tablet PC users cannot choose to share writing samples from the handwriting recognition personalization tool with Microsoft."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0304","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off handwriting recognition error reporting' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off handwriting recognition error reporting\n\nCheck that 'Turn off handwriting recognition error reporting' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off handwriting recognition error reporting\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\HandwritingErrorReports\nValue name: PreventHandwritingErrorReports\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off handwriting recognition error reporting\n\nCheck that 'Turn off handwriting recognition error reporting' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\HandwritingErrorReports\nValue name: PreventHandwritingErrorReports\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A person's handwriting is Personally Identifiable Information (PII), especially when it comes to your signature.","Impact":"Users cannot start the handwriting recognition error reporting tool or send error reports to Microsoft."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0305","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Internet Connection Wizard if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Internet Connection Wizard if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Internet Connection Wizard if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Internet Connection Wizard if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Internet Connection Wizard\nValue name: ExitOnMSICW\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Internet Connection Wizard if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Internet Connection Wizard if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Internet Connection Wizard\nValue name: ExitOnMSICW\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In an enterprise managed environment we want to lower the risk of a user unknowingly exposing sensitive data.","Impact":"The \"Choose a list of Internet Service Providers\" path in the Internet Connection Wizard causes the wizard to exit."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0306","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Internet download for Web publishing and online ordering wizards' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Internet download for Web publishing and online ordering wizards\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Internet download for Web publishing and online ordering wizards' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Internet download for Web publishing and online ordering wizards\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: NoWebServices\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Internet download for Web publishing and online ordering wizards\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Internet download for Web publishing and online ordering wizards' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: NoWebServices\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Although the risk is minimal, enabling this setting will reduce the possibility of a user unknowingly downloading malicious content through this feature.","Impact":"Windows is prevented from downloading providers; only the service providers cached in the local registry are displayed."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0307","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off printing over HTTP' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off printing over HTTP\n\nCheck that 'Turn off printing over HTTP' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off printing over HTTP\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\nValue name: DisableHTTPPrinting\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off printing over HTTP\n\nCheck that 'Turn off printing over HTTP' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Printers\nValue name: DisableHTTPPrinting\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Information that is transmitted over HTTP through this capability is not protected and can be intercepted by threat actors.","Impact":"The client computer will not be able to print to Internet printers over HTTP or HTTPS."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0308","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Registration if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Registration if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Registration if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Registration if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Registration Wizard Control\nValue name: NoRegistration\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Registration if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Registration if URL connection is referring to Microsoft.com' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Registration Wizard Control\nValue name: NoRegistration\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Users in an enterprise managed environment should not be registering their own copies of Windows, providing their own PII in the process.","Impact":"Users are blocked from connecting to Microsoft.com for online registration and they cannot register their copy of Windows online."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0309","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Search Companion content file updates' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Search Companion content file updates\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Search Companion content file updates' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Search Companion content file updates\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\SearchCompanion\nValue name: DisableContentFileUpdates\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Search Companion content file updates\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Search Companion content file updates' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\SearchCompanion\nValue name: DisableContentFileUpdates\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"There is a small risk that users will unknowingly reveal sensitive information because of the topics they are searching for.","Impact":"Search Companion does not download content updates during searches. Note: Internet searches will still send the search text and information about the search to Microsoft and the chosen search provider."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0310","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off the \"Order Prints\" picture task' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off the \"Order Prints\" picture task\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the \"Order Prints\" picture task' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off the \"Order Prints\" picture task\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: NoOnlinePrintsWizard\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off the \"Order Prints\" picture task\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the \"Order Prints\" picture task' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: NoOnlinePrintsWizard\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In an enterprise managed environment we want to lower the risk of a user unknowingly exposing sensitive data.","Impact":"The task \"Order Prints Online\" is removed from Picture Tasks in File Explorer folders."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0311","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off the \"Publish to Web\" task for files and folders' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off the \"Publish to Web\" task for files and folders\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the \"Publish to Web\" task for files and folders' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off the \"Publish to Web\" task for files and folders\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: NoPublishingWizard\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off the \"Publish to Web\" task for files and folders\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the \"Publish to Web\" task for files and folders' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: NoPublishingWizard\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Users may publish confidential or sensitive information to a public service outside of the control of the organization.","Impact":"The \"Publish to Web\" task is removed from File and Folder tasks in Windows folders."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0312","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off the Windows Messenger Customer Experience Improvement Program' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off the Windows Messenger Customer Experience Improvement Program\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the Windows Messenger Customer Experience Improvement Program' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off the Windows Messenger Customer Experience Improvement Program\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Messenger\\Client\nValue name: CEIP\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off the Windows Messenger Customer Experience Improvement Program\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the Windows Messenger Customer Experience Improvement Program' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Messenger\\Client\nValue name: CEIP\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Large enterprise managed environments may not want to have information collected by Microsoft from managed client computers.","Impact":"Windows Messenger will not collect usage information, and the user settings to enable the collection of usage information will not be shown."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0313","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Windows Customer Experience Improvement Program' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Windows Customer Experience Improvement Program\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Windows Customer Experience Improvement Program' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Windows Customer Experience Improvement Program\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\SQMClient\\Windows\nValue name: CEIPEnable\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Windows Customer Experience Improvement Program\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Windows Customer Experience Improvement Program' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\SQMClient\\Windows\nValue name: CEIPEnable\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Large enterprise managed environments may not want to have information collected by Microsoft from managed client computers.","Impact":"All users are opted out of the Windows Customer Experience Improvement Program."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0314","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Windows Error Reporting' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Disabled: 1; DoReport: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Windows Error Reporting\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Windows Error Reporting' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Windows Error Reporting\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Error Reporting\nValue name: Disabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\PCHealth\\ErrorReporting\nValue name: DoReport\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication settings\\Turn off Windows Error Reporting\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Windows Error Reporting' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Error Reporting\nValue name: Disabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\PCHealth\\ErrorReporting\nValue name: DoReport\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If a Windows Error occurs in a secure, enterprise managed environment, the error should be reported directly to IT staff for troubleshooting and remediation.","Impact":"Users are not given the option to report errors to Microsoft."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0315","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Support device authentication using certificate' is set to 'Enabled: Automatic'","Expected":"DevicePKInitBehavior: 0; DevicePKInitEnabled: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Kerberos\\Support device authentication using certificate\n\nCheck that 'Support device authentication using certificate' is set to Enabled: Automatic.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Kerberos\\Support device authentication using certificate\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\kerberos\\parameters\nValue name: DevicePKInitBehavior\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\kerberos\\parameters\nValue name: DevicePKInitEnabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Kerberos\\Support device authentication using certificate\n\nCheck that 'Support device authentication using certificate' is set to Enabled: Automatic.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\kerberos\\parameters\nValue name: DevicePKInitBehavior\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\kerberos\\parameters\nValue name: DevicePKInitEnabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Having stronger device authentication with the use of certificates is strongly encouraged over standard username and password authentication.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0316","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Enumeration policy for external devices incompatible with Kernel DMA Protection' is set to 'Enabled: Block All'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Kernel DMA Protection\\Enumeration policy for external devices incompatible with Kernel DMA Protection\n\nCheck that 'Enumeration policy for external devices incompatible with Kernel DMA Protection' is set to Enabled: Block All.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Kernel DMA Protection\\Enumeration policy for external devices incompatible with Kernel DMA Protection\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Kernel DMA Protection\nValue name: DeviceEnumerationPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Kernel DMA Protection\\Enumeration policy for external devices incompatible with Kernel DMA Protection\n\nCheck that 'Enumeration policy for external devices incompatible with Kernel DMA Protection' is set to Enabled: Block All.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Kernel DMA Protection\nValue name: DeviceEnumerationPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Device memory sandboxing allows the OS to leverage the I/O Memory Management Unit (IOMMU) of a device to block unpermitted I/O, or memory access, by the peripheral.","Impact":"External devices that are not compatible with DMA-remapping will not be enumerated and will not function unless/until the user has logged in successfully and has an unlocked user session."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0317","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure password backup directory' is set to 'Enabled: Active Directory' or 'Enabled: Azure Active Directory'","Expected":"One of: 1 | 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Configure password backup directory\n\nCheck that 'Configure password backup directory' is set to Enabled: Active Directory' or 'Enabled: Azure Active Directory.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Configure password backup directory\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: BackupDirectory\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Configure password backup directory\n\nCheck that 'Configure password backup directory' is set to Enabled: Active Directory' or 'Enabled: Azure Active Directory.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: BackupDirectory\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Due to the difficulty in managing local Administrator passwords, many organizations choose to use the same password on all workstations and/or Member Servers when deploying them.","Impact":"The passwords managed by Windows LAPS will only be retrievable from the configured directory type."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0318","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Do not allow password expiration time longer than required by policy' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Do not allow password expiration time longer than required by policy\n\nCheck that 'Do not allow password expiration time longer than required by policy' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Do not allow password expiration time longer than required by policy\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PasswordExpirationProtectionEnabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Do not allow password expiration time longer than required by policy\n\nCheck that 'Do not allow password expiration time longer than required by policy' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PasswordExpirationProtectionEnabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Due to the difficulty in managing local Administrator passwords, many organizations choose to use the same password on all workstations and/or Member Servers when deploying them.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. Planned password expiration longer than password age dictated by \"Password Settings\" policy is NOT allowed."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0319","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable password encryption' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Enable password encryption\n\nCheck that 'Enable password encryption' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Enable password encryption\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: ADPasswordEncryptionEnabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Enable password encryption\n\nCheck that 'Enable password encryption' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: ADPasswordEncryptionEnabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Due to the difficulty in managing local Administrator passwords, many organizations choose to use the same password on all workstations and/or Member Servers when deploying them.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior. If the domain functional level is set at or above Windows Server 2016, the Windows LAPS managed account password is encrypted automatically, if it is set at a lower domain functional level, the Windows LAPS managed account password will not be backed up to the directory."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0320","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Password Settings: Password Complexity' is set to 'Enabled: Large letters + small letters + numbers + specials' or higher","Expected":"One of: 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 | 8","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Password Settings\n\nCheck that 'Password Settings: Password Complexity' is set to Enabled: Large letters + small letters + numbers + specials.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Password Settings\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PasswordComplexity\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 4 (decimal; 0x00000004), 5 (decimal; 0x00000005), 6 (decimal; 0x00000006), 7 (decimal; 0x00000007), 8 (decimal; 0x00000008)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Password Settings\n\nCheck that 'Password Settings: Password Complexity' is set to Enabled: Large letters + small letters + numbers + specials.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PasswordComplexity\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 4 (decimal; 0x00000004), 5 (decimal; 0x00000005), 6 (decimal; 0x00000006), 7 (decimal; 0x00000007), 8 (decimal; 0x00000008)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Due to the difficulty in managing local Administrator passwords, many organizations choose to use the same password on all workstations and/or Member Servers when deploying them.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0321","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Password Settings: Password Length' is set to 'Enabled: 15 or more'","Expected":"At least: 15","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Password Settings\n\nCheck that 'Password Settings: Password Length' is set to Enabled: 15 or more.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Password Settings\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PasswordLength\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: at least 15 (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Password Settings\n\nCheck that 'Password Settings: Password Length' is set to Enabled: 15 or more.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PasswordLength\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: at least 15 (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Due to the difficulty in managing local Administrator passwords, many organizations choose to use the same password on all workstations and/or Member Servers when deploying them.","Impact":"Windows LAPS-generated passwords will be required to have a length of 15 characters (or more, if selected)."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0322","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Password Settings: Password Age (Days)' is set to 'Enabled: 30 or fewer'","Expected":"Range: 1 | 30","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Password Settings\n\nCheck that 'Password Settings: Password Age (Days)' is set to Enabled: 30 or fewer.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Password Settings\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PasswordAgeDays\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 through 30, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Password Settings\n\nCheck that 'Password Settings: Password Age (Days)' is set to Enabled: 30 or fewer.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PasswordAgeDays\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 through 30, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Due to the difficulty in managing local Administrator passwords, many organizations choose to use the same password on all workstations and/or Member Servers when deploying them.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior, unless set to fewer than 30 days."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0323","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Post-authentication actions: Grace period (hours)' is set to 'Enabled: 8 or fewer hours, but not 0'","Expected":"Range: 1 | 8","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Postauthentication actions: Grace period (hours)\n\nCheck that 'Post-authentication actions: Grace period (hours)' is set to Enabled: 8 or fewer hours, but not 0.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Postauthentication actions: Grace period (hours)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PostAuthenticationResetDelay\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 through 8, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Postauthentication actions: Grace period (hours)\n\nCheck that 'Post-authentication actions: Grace period (hours)' is set to Enabled: 8 or fewer hours, but not 0.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PostAuthenticationResetDelay\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 through 8, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Due to the difficulty in managing local Administrator passwords, many organizations choose to use the same password on all workstations and/or Member Servers when deploying them.","Impact":"After 8 hours, the Windows LAPS managed account password will be reset and log off the system."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0324","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Post-authentication actions: Actions' is set to 'Enabled: Reset the password and logoff the managed account' or higher","Expected":"One of: 3 | 5 | 11","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Postauthentication actions: Actions\n\nCheck that 'Post-authentication actions: Actions' is set to Enabled: Reset the password and logoff the managed account.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Postauthentication actions: Actions\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PostAuthenticationActions\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 3 (decimal; 0x00000003), 5 (decimal; 0x00000005), 11 (decimal; 0x0000000B)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\LAPS\\Postauthentication actions: Actions\n\nCheck that 'Post-authentication actions: Actions' is set to Enabled: Reset the password and logoff the managed account.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\LAPS\nValue name: PostAuthenticationActions\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 3 (decimal; 0x00000003), 5 (decimal; 0x00000005), 11 (decimal; 0x0000000B)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Due to the difficulty in managing local Administrator passwords, many organizations choose to use the same password on all workstations and/or Member Servers when deploying them.","Impact":"After the grace period expires, the Windows LAPS managed account password will be reset and logged off the system or the OS will be restarted."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0325","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Custom SSPs and APs to be loaded into LSASS' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Local Security Authority\\Allow Custom SSPs and APs to be loaded into LSASS\n\nCheck that 'Allow Custom SSPs and APs to be loaded into LSASS' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Local Security Authority\\Allow Custom SSPs and APs to be loaded into LSASS\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: AllowCustomSSPsAPs\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Local Security Authority\\Allow Custom SSPs and APs to be loaded into LSASS\n\nCheck that 'Allow Custom SSPs and APs to be loaded into LSASS' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: AllowCustomSSPsAPs\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing custom security support providers and authentication packages from loading into LSASS, a technique used to capture credentials and maintain persistence.","Impact":"Custom SSPs and APs, including some third-party smart card, biometric or single sign-on components, will not load into LSASS; test such software before applying."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0326","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configures LSASS to run as a protected process' is set to 'Enabled: Enabled with UEFI Lock'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Local Security Authority\\Configures LSASS to run as a protected process\n\nCheck that 'Configures LSASS to run as a protected process' is set to Enabled: Enabled with UEFI Lock.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Local Security Authority\\Configures LSASS to run as a protected process\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: RunAsPPL\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Local Security Authority\\Configures LSASS to run as a protected process\n\nCheck that 'Configures LSASS to run as a protected process' is set to Enabled: Enabled with UEFI Lock.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: RunAsPPL\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Provides added security for the credentials that LSA stores and manages.","Impact":"Once this setting has been applied (Enabled), removing the group policy setting (set to Not Configured) will not reverse the impact."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0327","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Disallow copying of user input methods to the system account for sign-in' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Locale Services\\Disallow copying of user input methods to the system account for sign-in\n\nCheck that 'Disallow copying of user input methods to the system account for sign-in' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Locale Services\\Disallow copying of user input methods to the system account for sign-in\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Control Panel\\International\nValue name: BlockUserInputMethodsForSignIn\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Locale Services\\Disallow copying of user input methods to the system account for sign-in\n\nCheck that 'Disallow copying of user input methods to the system account for sign-in' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Control Panel\\International\nValue name: BlockUserInputMethodsForSignIn\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This is a way to increase the security of the system account.","Impact":"Users will have input methods enabled for the system account on the sign-in page."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0328","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Block user from showing account details on sign-in' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Block user from showing account details on sign-in\n\nCheck that 'Block user from showing account details on sign-in' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Block user from showing account details on sign-in\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: BlockUserFromShowingAccountDetailsOnSignin\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Block user from showing account details on sign-in\n\nCheck that 'Block user from showing account details on sign-in' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: BlockUserFromShowingAccountDetailsOnSignin\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor with access to the console (for example, someone with physical access or someone who is able to connect to the computer through Remote Desktop Services) could view the name of the last user who logged on to the computer.","Impact":"The user cannot choose to show account details on the sign-in screen."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0329","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Do not display network selection UI' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Do not display network selection UI\n\nCheck that 'Do not display network selection UI' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Do not display network selection UI\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: DontDisplayNetworkSelectionUI\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Do not display network selection UI\n\nCheck that 'Do not display network selection UI' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: DontDisplayNetworkSelectionUI\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"An unauthorized user could disconnect the PC from the network or can connect the PC to other available networks without signing into Windows.","Impact":"The PC's network connectivity state cannot be changed without signing into Windows."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0330","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Do not enumerate connected users on domain-joined computers' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Do not enumerate connected users on domain-joined computers\n\nCheck that 'Do not enumerate connected users on domain- joined computers' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Do not enumerate connected users on domain-joined computers\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: DontEnumerateConnectedUsers\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Do not enumerate connected users on domain-joined computers\n\nCheck that 'Do not enumerate connected users on domain- joined computers' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: DontEnumerateConnectedUsers\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor could use this feature to gather account names of other users, that information could then be used in conjunction with other types of attacks such as guessing passwords or social engineering.","Impact":"The Logon UI will not enumerate any connected users on domain-joined computers."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0331","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enumerate local users on domain-joined computers' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Enumerate local users on domain-joined computers\n\nCheck that 'Enumerate local users on domain-joined computers' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Enumerate local users on domain-joined computers\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: EnumerateLocalUsers\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Enumerate local users on domain-joined computers\n\nCheck that 'Enumerate local users on domain-joined computers' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: EnumerateLocalUsers\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor could use this feature to gather account names of other users, that information could then be used in conjunction with other types of attacks such as guessing passwords or social engineering.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0332","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off app notifications on the lock screen' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Turn off app notifications on the lock screen\n\nCheck that 'Turn off app notifications on the lock screen' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Turn off app notifications on the lock screen\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: DisableLockScreenAppNotifications\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Turn off app notifications on the lock screen\n\nCheck that 'Turn off app notifications on the lock screen' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: DisableLockScreenAppNotifications\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"App notifications might display sensitive business or personal data.","Impact":"No app notifications are displayed on the lock screen."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0333","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off picture password sign-in' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Turn off picture password sign-in\n\nCheck that 'Turn off picture password sign-in' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Turn off picture password sign-in\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: BlockDomainPicturePassword\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Turn off picture password sign-in\n\nCheck that 'Turn off picture password sign-in' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: BlockDomainPicturePassword\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling picture password sign-in for domain accounts, because gesture-based sign-in is easier to observe and guess than other credentials.","Impact":"Domain users cannot set up or sign in with a picture password."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0334","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn on convenience PIN sign-in' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Turn on convenience PIN sign-in\n\nCheck that 'Turn on convenience PIN sign-in' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Turn on convenience PIN sign-in\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: AllowDomainPINLogon\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Logon\\Turn on convenience PIN sign-in\n\nCheck that 'Turn on convenience PIN sign-in' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: AllowDomainPINLogon\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A PIN is created from a much smaller selection of characters than a password, so in most cases a PIN will be much less robust than a password.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0335","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Block NetBIOS-based discovery for domain controller location' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Net Logon\\DC Locator DNS Records\\Block NetBIOS-based discovery for domain controller location\n\nCheck that 'Block NetBIOS-based discovery for domain controller location' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Net Logon\\DC Locator DNS Records\\Block NetBIOS-based discovery for domain controller location\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Netlogon\\Parameters\nValue name: BlockNetbiosDiscovery\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Net Logon\\DC Locator DNS Records\\Block NetBIOS-based discovery for domain controller location\n\nCheck that 'Block NetBIOS-based discovery for domain controller location' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Netlogon\\Parameters\nValue name: BlockNetbiosDiscovery\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"NetBIOS is considered insecure because it doesn't perform authentication and can allow remote threat actors to trigger a denial-of-service by sending spoofed Name Conflicts or Name Release datagrams.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0336","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Clipboard synchronization across devices' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\OS Policies\\Allow Clipboard synchronization across devices\n\nCheck that 'Allow Clipboard synchronization across devices' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\OS Policies\\Allow Clipboard synchronization across devices\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: AllowCrossDeviceClipboard\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\OS Policies\\Allow Clipboard synchronization across devices\n\nCheck that 'Allow Clipboard synchronization across devices' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: AllowCrossDeviceClipboard\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Due to privacy concerns, clipboard data should stay local to the system and not synced across devices.","Impact":"If you disable this policy setting, Clipboard contents cannot be shared to other devices."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0337","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow upload of User Activities' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\OS Policies\\Allow upload of User Activities\n\nCheck that 'Allow upload of User Activities' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\OS Policies\\Allow upload of User Activities\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: UploadUserActivities\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\OS Policies\\Allow upload of User Activities\n\nCheck that 'Allow upload of User Activities' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: UploadUserActivities\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In high-security environments, data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"Activities of type User Activity are not allowed to be uploaded to the cloud."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0338","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow network connectivity during connected-standby (on battery)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow network connectivity during connected-standby (on battery)\n\nCheck that 'Allow network connectivity during connected- standby (on battery)' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow network connectivity during connected-standby (on battery)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\f15576e8-98b7-4186-b944-eafa664402d9\nValue name: DCSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow network connectivity during connected-standby (on battery)\n\nCheck that 'Allow network connectivity during connected- standby (on battery)' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\f15576e8-98b7-4186-b944-eafa664402d9\nValue name: DCSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Disabling this setting ensures that the computer will not be accessible to threat actors over a WLAN network while left unattended, on battery and in a sleep state.","Impact":"Network connectivity in standby (while on battery) is not guaranteed."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0339","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow network connectivity during connected-standby (plugged in)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow network connectivity during connected-standby (plugged in)\n\nCheck that 'Allow network connectivity during connected- standby (plugged in)' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow network connectivity during connected-standby (plugged in)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\f15576e8-98b7-4186-b944-eafa664402d9\nValue name: ACSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow network connectivity during connected-standby (plugged in)\n\nCheck that 'Allow network connectivity during connected- standby (plugged in)' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\f15576e8-98b7-4186-b944-eafa664402d9\nValue name: ACSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Disabling this setting ensures that the computer will not be accessible to threat actors over a WLAN network while left unattended, plugged in and in a sleep state.","Impact":"Network connectivity in standby (while plugged in) is not guaranteed."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0340","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (on battery)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (on battery)\n\nCheck that 'Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (on battery)' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (on battery)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\abfc2519-3608-4c2a-94ea-171b0ed546ab\nValue name: DCSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (on battery)\n\nCheck that 'Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (on battery)' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\abfc2519-3608-4c2a-94ea-171b0ed546ab\nValue name: DCSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling standby states S1-S3 on BitLocker-protected systems, because encryption keys remain in memory during sleep and can be recovered by an attacker with physical access.","Impact":"The device cannot enter legacy sleep (S1-S3); it hibernates or shuts down instead. Devices that use Modern Standby (S0 low-power idle) are not affected."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0341","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (plugged in)' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (plugged in)\n\nCheck that 'Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (plugged in)' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (plugged in)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\abfc2519-3608-4c2a-94ea-171b0ed546ab\nValue name: ACSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (plugged in)\n\nCheck that 'Allow standby states (S1-S3) when sleeping (plugged in)' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\abfc2519-3608-4c2a-94ea-171b0ed546ab\nValue name: ACSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling standby states S1-S3 on BitLocker-protected systems, because encryption keys remain in memory during sleep and can be recovered by an attacker with physical access.","Impact":"The device cannot enter legacy sleep (S1-S3); it hibernates or shuts down instead. Devices that use Modern Standby (S0 low-power idle) are not affected."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0342","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Require a password when a computer wakes (on battery)' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Require a password when a computer wakes (on battery)\n\nCheck that 'Require a password when a computer wakes (on battery)' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Require a password when a computer wakes (on battery)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\0e796bdb-100d-47d6-a2d5-f7d2daa51f51\nValue name: DCSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Require a password when a computer wakes (on battery)\n\nCheck that 'Require a password when a computer wakes (on battery)' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\0e796bdb-100d-47d6-a2d5-f7d2daa51f51\nValue name: DCSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Enabling this setting ensures that anyone who wakes an unattended computer from sleep state will have to provide logon credentials before they can access the system.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0343","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Require a password when a computer wakes (plugged in)' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Require a password when a computer wakes (plugged in)\n\nCheck that 'Require a password when a computer wakes (plugged in)' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Require a password when a computer wakes (plugged in)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\0e796bdb-100d-47d6-a2d5-f7d2daa51f51\nValue name: ACSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Power Management\\Sleep Settings\\Require a password when a computer wakes (plugged in)\n\nCheck that 'Require a password when a computer wakes (plugged in)' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Power\\PowerSettings\\0e796bdb-100d-47d6-a2d5-f7d2daa51f51\nValue name: ACSettingIndex\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Enabling this setting ensures that anyone who wakes an unattended computer from sleep state will have to provide logon credentials before they can access the system.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0344","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure Offer Remote Assistance' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Assistance\\Configure Offer Remote Assistance\n\nCheck that 'Configure Offer Remote Assistance' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Assistance\\Configure Offer Remote Assistance\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: fAllowUnsolicited\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Assistance\\Configure Offer Remote Assistance\n\nCheck that 'Configure Offer Remote Assistance' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: fAllowUnsolicited\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A user might be tricked and accept an unsolicited Remote Assistance offer from a threat actor.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0345","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure Solicited Remote Assistance' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Assistance\\Configure Solicited Remote Assistance\n\nCheck that 'Configure Solicited Remote Assistance' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Assistance\\Configure Solicited Remote Assistance\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: fAllowToGetHelp\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Assistance\\Configure Solicited Remote Assistance\n\nCheck that 'Configure Solicited Remote Assistance' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: fAllowToGetHelp\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"There is slight risk that a rogue administrator will gain access to another user's desktop session, however, they cannot connect to a user's computer unannounced or control it without permission from the user.","Impact":"Users on this computer cannot use e-mail or file transfer to ask someone for help."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0346","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable RPC Endpoint Mapper Client Authentication' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Procedure Call\\Enable RPC Endpoint Mapper Client Authentication\n\nCheck that 'Enable RPC Endpoint Mapper Client Authentication' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Procedure Call\\Enable RPC Endpoint Mapper Client Authentication\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Rpc\nValue name: EnableAuthEpResolution\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Procedure Call\\Enable RPC Endpoint Mapper Client Authentication\n\nCheck that 'Enable RPC Endpoint Mapper Client Authentication' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Rpc\nValue name: EnableAuthEpResolution\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Anonymous access to RPC services could result in accidental disclosure of information to unauthenticated users.","Impact":"RPC clients will authenticate to the Endpoint Mapper Service for calls that contain authentication information."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0347","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Restrict Unauthenticated RPC clients' is set to 'Enabled: Authenticated'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Procedure Call\\Restrict Unauthenticated RPC clients\n\nCheck that 'Restrict Unauthenticated RPC clients' is set to Enabled: Authenticated.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Procedure Call\\Restrict Unauthenticated RPC clients\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Rpc\nValue name: RestrictRemoteClients\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Remote Procedure Call\\Restrict Unauthenticated RPC clients\n\nCheck that 'Restrict Unauthenticated RPC clients' is set to Enabled: Authenticated.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Rpc\nValue name: RestrictRemoteClients\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Unauthenticated RPC communication can create a security vulnerability.","Impact":"Only authenticated RPC Clients will be allowed to connect to RPC servers running on the machine on which the policy setting is applied."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0348","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure SAM change password RPC methods policy' is set to 'Enabled: Block all change password RPC methods'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Security Account Manager\\Configure SAM change password RPC methods policy\n\nCheck that 'Configure SAM change password RPC methods policy' is set to Enabled: Block all change password RPC methods.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Security Account Manager\\Configure SAM change password RPC methods policy\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\SAM\nValue name: SamrChangeUserPasswordApiPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Security Account Manager\\Configure SAM change password RPC methods policy\n\nCheck that 'Configure SAM change password RPC methods policy' is set to Enabled: Block all change password RPC methods.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\\SAM\nValue name: SamrChangeUserPasswordApiPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"User passwords stored in the SAM should only be changed from a Domain Controller using secure methods.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0349","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Microsoft Support Diagnostic Tool: Turn on MSDT interactive communication with support provider' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Troubleshooting and Diagnostics\\Microsoft Support Diagnostic Tool\\Microsoft Support Diagnostic Tool: Turn on MSDT interactive communication with support provider\n\nCheck that 'Microsoft Support Diagnostic Tool: Turn on MSDT interactive communication with support provider' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Troubleshooting and Diagnostics\\Microsoft Support Diagnostic Tool\\Microsoft Support Diagnostic Tool: Turn on MSDT interactive communication with support provider\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\ScriptedDiagnosticsProvider\\Policy\nValue name: DisableQueryRemoteServer\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Troubleshooting and Diagnostics\\Microsoft Support Diagnostic Tool\\Microsoft Support Diagnostic Tool: Turn on MSDT interactive communication with support provider\n\nCheck that 'Microsoft Support Diagnostic Tool: Turn on MSDT interactive communication with support provider' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\ScriptedDiagnosticsProvider\\Policy\nValue name: DisableQueryRemoteServer\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In high-security environments, data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"MSDT cannot run in support mode, and no data can be collected or sent to the support provider."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0350","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable/Disable PerfTrack' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Troubleshooting and Diagnostics\\Windows Performance PerfTrack\\Enable/Disable PerfTrack\n\nCheck that 'Enable/Disable PerfTrack' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Troubleshooting and Diagnostics\\Windows Performance PerfTrack\\Enable/Disable PerfTrack\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WDI\\{9c5a40da-b965-4fc3-8781-88dd50a6299d}\nValue name: ScenarioExecutionEnabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Troubleshooting and Diagnostics\\Windows Performance PerfTrack\\Enable/Disable PerfTrack\n\nCheck that 'Enable/Disable PerfTrack' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WDI\\{9c5a40da-b965-4fc3-8781-88dd50a6299d}\nValue name: ScenarioExecutionEnabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"When enabled the aggregated data of a given event will be transmitted to Microsoft.","Impact":"Responsiveness events are not processed."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0351","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off the advertising ID' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\User Profiles\\Turn off the advertising ID\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the advertising ID' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\User Profiles\\Turn off the advertising ID\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AdvertisingInfo\nValue name: DisabledByGroupPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\User Profiles\\Turn off the advertising ID\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the advertising ID' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AdvertisingInfo\nValue name: DisabledByGroupPolicy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In high-security environments, data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"The advertising ID is turned off. Apps can't use the ID for experiences across apps."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0352","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable Windows NTP Client' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Windows Time Service\\Time Providers\\Enable Windows NTP Client\n\nCheck that 'Enable Windows NTP Client' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Windows Time Service\\Time Providers\\Enable Windows NTP Client\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\W32Time\\TimeProviders\\NtpClient\nValue name: Enabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Windows Time Service\\Time Providers\\Enable Windows NTP Client\n\nCheck that 'Enable Windows NTP Client' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\W32Time\\TimeProviders\\NtpClient\nValue name: Enabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A reliable and accurate account of time is important for a number of services and security requirements, including but not limited to distributed applications, authentication services, multi-user databases and logging services.","Impact":"System time will be synced to the configured NTP server(s)."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0353","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable Windows NTP Server' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Windows Time Service\\Time Providers\\Enable Windows NTP Server\n\nCheck that 'Enable Windows NTP Server' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Windows Time Service\\Time Providers\\Enable Windows NTP Server\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\W32Time\\TimeProviders\\NtpServer\nValue name: Enabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Windows Time Service\\Time Providers\\Enable Windows NTP Server\n\nCheck that 'Enable Windows NTP Server' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\W32Time\\TimeProviders\\NtpServer\nValue name: Enabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The configuration of proper time synchronization is critically important in an enterprise managed environment both due to the sensitivity of Kerberos authentication timestamps and also to ensure accurate security logging.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0354","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure the behavior of the sudo command' is set to 'Enabled: Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Sudo\\Configure the behavior of the sudo command\n\nCheck that 'Configure the behavior of the sudo command' is set to Enabled: Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Sudo\\Configure the behavior of the sudo command\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Sudo\nValue name: Enabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Sudo\\Configure the behavior of the sudo command\n\nCheck that 'Configure the behavior of the sudo command' is set to Enabled: Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Sudo\nValue name: Enabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling the sudo command so that elevation from an unelevated console cannot be used to bypass established privilege-separation and elevation controls.","Impact":"Users cannot run the sudo command; elevation continues to work through the normal User Account Control mechanisms."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0355","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off API Sampling' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App and Device Inventory\\Turn off API Sampling\n\nCheck that 'Turn off API Sampling' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App and Device Inventory\\Turn off API Sampling\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppCompat\nValue name: DisableAPISamping\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App and Device Inventory\\Turn off API Sampling\n\nCheck that 'Turn off API Sampling' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppCompat\nValue name: DisableAPISamping\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends turning off API sampling to limit the application usage data that is collected and sent to Microsoft.","Impact":"Application API usage is not sampled for compatibility telemetry."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0356","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Application Footprint' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App and Device Inventory\\Turn off Application Footprint\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Application Footprint' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App and Device Inventory\\Turn off Application Footprint\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppCompat\nValue name: DisableApplicationFootprint\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App and Device Inventory\\Turn off Application Footprint\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Application Footprint' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppCompat\nValue name: DisableApplicationFootprint\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends turning off application footprint collection to limit the software inventory data that is collected and sent to Microsoft.","Impact":"Application footprint data is not collected for compatibility telemetry."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0357","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Install Tracing' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App and Device Inventory\\Turn off Install Tracing\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Install Tracing' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App and Device Inventory\\Turn off Install Tracing\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppCompat\nValue name: DisableInstallTracing\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App and Device Inventory\\Turn off Install Tracing\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Install Tracing' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppCompat\nValue name: DisableInstallTracing\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends turning off install tracing to limit the application installation data that is collected and sent to Microsoft.","Impact":"Application installation activity is not traced for compatibility telemetry."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0358","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow a Windows app to share application data between users' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Package Deployment\\Allow a Windows app to share application data between users\n\nCheck that 'Allow a Windows app to share application data between users' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Package Deployment\\Allow a Windows app to share application data between users\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\AppModel\\StateManager\nValue name: AllowSharedLocalAppData\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Package Deployment\\Allow a Windows app to share application data between users\n\nCheck that 'Allow a Windows app to share application data between users' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\AppModel\\StateManager\nValue name: AllowSharedLocalAppData\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Users of a system could accidentally share sensitive data with other users on the same system.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0359","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Not allow per-user unsigned packages to install by default (requires explicitly allow per install)' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Package Deployment\\Not allow per-user unsigned packages to install by default (requires explicitly allow per install)\n\nCheck that 'Not allow per-user unsigned packages to install by default (requires explicitly allow per install)' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Package Deployment\\Not allow per-user unsigned packages to install by default (requires explicitly allow per install)\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Appx\nValue name: DisablePerUserUnsignedPackagesByDefault\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Package Deployment\\Not allow per-user unsigned packages to install by default (requires explicitly allow per install)\n\nCheck that 'Not allow per-user unsigned packages to install by default (requires explicitly allow per install)' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Appx\nValue name: DisablePerUserUnsignedPackagesByDefault\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In a corporate managed environment, application installations should be managed centrally by IT staff, not by end users.","Impact":"Standard users will not be able to install unsigned packaged Microsoft Store Apps, unless they are explicitly permitted by other policies."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0360","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prevent non-admin users from installing packaged Windows apps' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Package Deployment\\Prevent non-admin users from installing packaged Windows apps\n\nCheck that 'Prevent non-admin users from installing packaged Windows apps' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Package Deployment\\Prevent non-admin users from installing packaged Windows apps\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Appx\nValue name: BlockNonAdminUserInstall\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Package Deployment\\Prevent non-admin users from installing packaged Windows apps\n\nCheck that 'Prevent non-admin users from installing packaged Windows apps' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Appx\nValue name: BlockNonAdminUserInstall\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing standard users from installing packaged Windows apps, so that only approved software is introduced to the device.","Impact":"Standard users cannot install packaged (MSIX/AppX) applications; installation requires an administrator or a managed deployment tool."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0361","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Let Windows apps activate with voice while the system is locked' is set to 'Enabled: Force Deny'","Expected":"Equals: 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Privacy\\Let Windows apps activate with voice while the system is locked\n\nCheck that 'Let Windows apps activate with voice while the system is locked' is set to Enabled: Force Deny.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Privacy\\Let Windows apps activate with voice while the system is locked\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppPrivacy\nValue name: LetAppsActivateWithVoiceAboveLock\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App Privacy\\Let Windows apps activate with voice while the system is locked\n\nCheck that 'Let Windows apps activate with voice while the system is locked' is set to Enabled: Force Deny.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppPrivacy\nValue name: LetAppsActivateWithVoiceAboveLock\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends denying voice activation of applications while the device is locked, so that someone near an unattended device cannot interact with applications or data.","Impact":"Applications cannot be started by voice while the device is locked."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0362","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Microsoft accounts to be optional' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App runtime\\Allow Microsoft accounts to be optional\n\nCheck that 'Allow Microsoft accounts to be optional' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App runtime\\Allow Microsoft accounts to be optional\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: MSAOptional\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App runtime\\Allow Microsoft accounts to be optional\n\nCheck that 'Allow Microsoft accounts to be optional' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: MSAOptional\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Enabling this setting allows an organization to use their enterprise user accounts instead of using their Microsoft accounts when accessing Windows store apps.","Impact":"Windows Store apps that typically require a Microsoft account to sign in will allow users to sign in with an enterprise account instead."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0363","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Block launching Universal Windows apps with Windows Runtime API access from hosted content.' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App runtime\\Block launching Universal Windows apps with Windows Runtime API access from hosted content.\n\nCheck that 'Block launching Universal Windows apps with Windows Runtime API access from hosted content.' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App runtime\\Block launching Universal Windows apps with Windows Runtime API access from hosted content.\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: BlockHostedAppAccessWinRT\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\App runtime\\Block launching Universal Windows apps with Windows Runtime API access from hosted content.\n\nCheck that 'Block launching Universal Windows apps with Windows Runtime API access from hosted content.' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\System\nValue name: BlockHostedAppAccessWinRT\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends blocking hosted web content from launching Universal Windows apps with Windows Runtime API access, to limit what untrusted web content can do on the device.","Impact":"Universal Windows apps that load hosted web content needing Windows Runtime API access will not launch; line-of-business apps built this way need review."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0364","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Disallow Autoplay for non-volume devices' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\AutoPlay Policies\\Disallow Autoplay for non-volume devices\n\nCheck that 'Disallow Autoplay for non-volume devices' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\AutoPlay Policies\\Disallow Autoplay for non-volume devices\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: NoAutoplayfornonVolume\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\AutoPlay Policies\\Disallow Autoplay for non-volume devices\n\nCheck that 'Disallow Autoplay for non-volume devices' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: NoAutoplayfornonVolume\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor could use this feature to launch a program to damage a client computer or data on the computer.","Impact":"AutoPlay will not be allowed for MTP devices like cameras or phones."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0365","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Set the default behavior for AutoRun' is set to 'Enabled: Do not execute any autorun commands'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\AutoPlay Policies\\Set the default behavior for AutoRun\n\nCheck that 'Set the default behavior for AutoRun' is set to Enabled: Do not execute any autorun commands.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\AutoPlay Policies\\Set the default behavior for AutoRun\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: NoAutorun\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\AutoPlay Policies\\Set the default behavior for AutoRun\n\nCheck that 'Set the default behavior for AutoRun' is set to Enabled: Do not execute any autorun commands.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: NoAutorun\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Prior to Windows Vista, when media containing an autorun command is inserted, the system will automatically execute the program without user intervention.","Impact":"AutoRun commands will be completely disabled."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0366","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Autoplay' is set to 'Enabled: All drives'","Expected":"Equals: 255","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\AutoPlay Policies\\Turn off Autoplay\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Autoplay' is set to Enabled: All drives.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\AutoPlay Policies\\Turn off Autoplay\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: NoDriveTypeAutoRun\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 255 (decimal; 0x000000FF)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\AutoPlay Policies\\Turn off Autoplay\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Autoplay' is set to Enabled: All drives.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: NoDriveTypeAutoRun\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 255 (decimal; 0x000000FF)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"A threat actor could use this feature to launch a program to damage a client computer or data on the computer.","Impact":"Autoplay will be disabled - users will have to manually launch setup or installation programs that are provided on removable media."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0367","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure enhanced anti-spoofing' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Biometrics\\Facial Features\\Configure enhanced anti-spoofing\n\nCheck that 'Configure enhanced anti-spoofing' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Biometrics\\Facial Features\\Configure enhanced anti-spoofing\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Biometrics\\FacialFeatures\nValue name: EnhancedAntiSpoofing\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Biometrics\\Facial Features\\Configure enhanced anti-spoofing\n\nCheck that 'Configure enhanced anti-spoofing' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Biometrics\\FacialFeatures\nValue name: EnhancedAntiSpoofing\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Enterprise managed environments are now supporting a wider range of mobile devices, increasing the security on these devices will help protect against unauthorized access on your network.","Impact":"Windows will require all users on the device to use anti-spoofing for facial features, on devices which support it."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0368","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Allow access to BitLocker-protected fixed data drives from earlier versions of Windows' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: <none>","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Allow access to BitLocker-protected fixed data drives from earlier versions of Windows\n\nCheck that 'Allow access to BitLocker-protected fixed data drives from earlier versions of Windows' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Allow access to BitLocker-protected fixed data drives from earlier versions of Windows\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: FDVDiscoveryVolumeType\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: \"<none>\"\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Allow access to BitLocker-protected fixed data drives from earlier versions of Windows\n\nCheck that 'Allow access to BitLocker-protected fixed data drives from earlier versions of Windows' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: FDVDiscoveryVolumeType\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: \"<none>\"\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing BitLocker-protected fixed drives from being unlocked on earlier versions of Windows, which do not enforce current protections.","Impact":"FAT-formatted fixed drives protected by BitLocker cannot be unlocked on Windows Vista, Windows XP SP2/SP3 or Windows Server 2008."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0369","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered\n\nCheck that 'Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: FDVRecovery\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered\n\nCheck that 'Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: FDVRecovery\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends defining how BitLocker-protected fixed data drives are recovered, so that data remains recoverable by the organisation without leaving weak or unmanaged recovery methods available.","Impact":"Recovery behaviour for fixed data drives follows the configured policy options instead of the defaults."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0370","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Allow data recovery agent' is set to 'Enabled: True'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Allow data recovery agent\n\nCheck that 'Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Allow data recovery agent' is set to Enabled: True.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Allow data recovery agent\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: FDVManageDRA\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Allow data recovery agent\n\nCheck that 'Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Allow data recovery agent' is set to Enabled: True.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: FDVManageDRA\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends defining how BitLocker-protected fixed data drives are recovered, so that data remains recoverable by the organisation without leaving weak or unmanaged recovery methods available.","Impact":"A data recovery agent certificate can be used to recover fixed data drives; a DRA must be published through Public Key Policies."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0371","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Recovery Password' is set to 'Enabled: Allow 48-digit recovery password' or higher","Expected":"One of: 1 | 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Recovery Password\n\nCheck that 'Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Recovery Password' is set to Enabled: Allow 48-digit recovery password, or Enabled: Require 48-digit recovery password.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Recovery Password\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: FDVRecoveryPassword\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Fixed Data Drives\\Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Recovery Password\n\nCheck that 'Choose how BitLocker-protected fixed drives can be recovered: Recovery Password' is set to Enabled: Allow 48-digit recovery password, or Enabled: Require 48-digit recovery password.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: FDVRecoveryPassword\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends defining how BitLocker-protected fixed data drives are recovered, so that data remains recoverable by the organisation without leaving weak or unmanaged recovery methods available.","Impact":"A 48-digit recovery password is allowed or required for fixed data drives."},
