{"Id":"CHECK_0401","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Configure use of hardware-based encryption for removable data drives' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of hardware-based encryption for removable data drives\n\nCheck that 'Configure use of hardware-based encryption for removable data drives' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of hardware-based encryption for removable data drives\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVHardwareEncryption\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of hardware-based encryption for removable data drives\n\nCheck that 'Configure use of hardware-based encryption for removable data drives' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVHardwareEncryption\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends software-based BitLocker encryption for removable data drives, because drive firmware encryption has had vulnerabilities that allow protection to be bypassed.","Impact":"BitLocker always uses software-based encryption; there may be a small performance cost on drives that support hardware encryption."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0402","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Configure use of passwords for removable data drives' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of passwords for removable data drives\n\nCheck that 'Configure use of passwords for removable data drives' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of passwords for removable data drives\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVPassphrase\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of passwords for removable data drives\n\nCheck that 'Configure use of passwords for removable data drives' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVPassphrase\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends that passwords are not used to unlock BitLocker-protected removable data drives, because passwords are easier to guess or capture than certificate or TPM-based protectors.","Impact":"Removable data drives cannot be unlocked with a password; smart cards must be used."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0403","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives\n\nCheck that 'Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVAllowUserCert\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives\n\nCheck that 'Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVAllowUserCert\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends smart card protection for BitLocker-protected removable data drives, so that unlocking the drive requires a physical token and its PIN.","Impact":"A smart card with a suitable certificate is needed to protect and unlock the drive; smart card infrastructure must be in place."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0404","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives: Require use of smart cards on removable data drives' is set to 'Enabled: True'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives: Require use of smart cards on removable data drives\n\nCheck that 'Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives: Require use of smart cards on removable data drives' is set to Enabled: True.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives: Require use of smart cards on removable data drives\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVEnforceUserCert\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives: Require use of smart cards on removable data drives\n\nCheck that 'Configure use of smart cards on removable data drives: Require use of smart cards on removable data drives' is set to Enabled: True.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVEnforceUserCert\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends smart card protection for BitLocker-protected removable data drives, so that unlocking the drive requires a physical token and its PIN.","Impact":"Removable data drives can only be protected with a smart card; a smart card with a suitable certificate is needed to protect and unlock the drive; smart card infrastructure must be in place."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0405","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker\n\nCheck that 'Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVDenyWriteAccess\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker\n\nCheck that 'Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SYSTEM\\CurrentControlSet\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVDenyWriteAccess\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends denying write access to removable drives that are not BitLocker-protected, so that data copied off the device is always encrypted.","Impact":"Removable drives without BitLocker are read-only; users must encrypt a removable drive before saving data to it."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0406","Area":"BitLocker profile (BL)","AuditCheck":"'Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker: Do not allow write access to devices configured in another organization' is set to 'Enabled: False'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker: Do not allow write access to devices configured in another organization\n\nCheck that 'Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker: Do not allow write access to devices configured in another organization' is set to Enabled: False.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker: Do not allow write access to devices configured in another organization\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVDenyCrossOrg\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\BitLocker Drive Encryption\\Removable Data Drives\\Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker: Do not allow write access to devices configured in another organization\n\nCheck that 'Deny write access to removable drives not protected by BitLocker: Do not allow write access to devices configured in another organization' is set to Enabled: False.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\FVE\nValue name: RDVDenyCrossOrg\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends allowing write access to BitLocker-protected removable drives regardless of the organisation identifier, so that encrypted drives remain usable without identifier management.","Impact":"BitLocker-protected removable drives from other organisations can be written to once unlocked."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0407","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Use of Camera' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Camera\\Allow Use of Camera\n\nCheck that 'Allow Use of Camera' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Camera\\Allow Use of Camera\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Camera\nValue name: AllowCamera\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Camera\\Allow Use of Camera\n\nCheck that 'Allow Use of Camera' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Camera\nValue name: AllowCamera\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cameras in a high security environment can pose serious privacy and data exfiltration risks - they should be disabled to help mitigate that risk.","Impact":"Users will not be able to utilize the camera on a system."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0408","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off cloud consumer account state content' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Cloud Content\\Turn off cloud consumer account state content\n\nCheck that 'Turn off cloud consumer account state content' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Cloud Content\\Turn off cloud consumer account state content\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent\nValue name: DisableConsumerAccountStateContent\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Cloud Content\\Turn off cloud consumer account state content\n\nCheck that 'Turn off cloud consumer account state content' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent\nValue name: DisableConsumerAccountStateContent\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The use of consumer accounts in an enterprise managed environment is not good security practice as it could lead to possible data leakage.","Impact":"Users will not be able to use Microsoft consumer accounts on the system, and associated Windows experiences will instead present default fallback content."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0409","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off cloud optimized content' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Cloud Content\\Turn off cloud optimized content\n\nCheck that 'Turn off cloud optimized content' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Cloud Content\\Turn off cloud optimized content\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent\nValue name: DisableCloudOptimizedContent\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Cloud Content\\Turn off cloud optimized content\n\nCheck that 'Turn off cloud optimized content' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent\nValue name: DisableCloudOptimizedContent\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In high-security environments, data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"Windows experiences that use the cloud optimized content client component, will present the default fallback content instead of customized content."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0410","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Microsoft consumer experiences' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Cloud Content\\Turn off Microsoft consumer experiences\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Microsoft consumer experiences' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Cloud Content\\Turn off Microsoft consumer experiences\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent\nValue name: DisableWindowsConsumerFeatures\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Cloud Content\\Turn off Microsoft consumer experiences\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Microsoft consumer experiences' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CloudContent\nValue name: DisableWindowsConsumerFeatures\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends turning off Microsoft consumer experiences so that third-party applications and suggestions are not installed or promoted on business devices.","Impact":"Users no longer see personalised recommendations from Microsoft, and suggested consumer applications are not installed automatically."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0411","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Require pin for pairing' is set to 'Enabled: First Time' OR 'Enabled: Always'","Expected":"One of: 1 | 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Connect\\Require pin for pairing\n\nCheck that 'Require pin for pairing' is set to Enabled: First Time' OR 'Enabled: Always.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Connect\\Require pin for pairing\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Connect\nValue name: RequirePinForPairing\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Connect\\Require pin for pairing\n\nCheck that 'Require pin for pairing' is set to Enabled: First Time' OR 'Enabled: Always.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Connect\nValue name: RequirePinForPairing\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 1 (decimal; 0x00000001), 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If this setting is not configured or disabled then a PIN would not be required when pairing wireless display devices to the system, increasing the risk of unauthorized use.","Impact":"The pairing ceremony for connecting to new wireless display devices will always require a PIN."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0412","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Do not display the password reveal button' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Credential User Interface\\Do not display the password reveal button\n\nCheck that 'Do not display the password reveal button' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Credential User Interface\\Do not display the password reveal button\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CredUI\nValue name: DisablePasswordReveal\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Credential User Interface\\Do not display the password reveal button\n\nCheck that 'Do not display the password reveal button' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CredUI\nValue name: DisablePasswordReveal\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This is a useful feature when entering a long and complex password, especially when using a touchscreen.","Impact":"The password reveal button will not be displayed after a user types a password in the password entry text box."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0413","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enumerate administrator accounts on elevation' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Credential User Interface\\Enumerate administrator accounts on elevation\n\nCheck that 'Enumerate administrator accounts on elevation' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Credential User Interface\\Enumerate administrator accounts on elevation\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\CredUI\nValue name: EnumerateAdministrators\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Credential User Interface\\Enumerate administrator accounts on elevation\n\nCheck that 'Enumerate administrator accounts on elevation' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\CredUI\nValue name: EnumerateAdministrators\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Users could see the list of administrator accounts, making it slightly easier for a threat actor who has logged onto a console session to try to crack the passwords of those accounts.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0414","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prevent the use of security questions for local accounts' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Credential User Interface\\Prevent the use of security questions for local accounts\n\nCheck that 'Prevent the use of security questions for local accounts' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Credential User Interface\\Prevent the use of security questions for local accounts\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: NoLocalPasswordResetQuestions\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Credential User Interface\\Prevent the use of security questions for local accounts\n\nCheck that 'Prevent the use of security questions for local accounts' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: NoLocalPasswordResetQuestions\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing security questions for local accounts, because answers are often easy to guess or research and allow a local account password to be reset.","Impact":"Local accounts cannot use security questions to reset a forgotten password; an administrator must reset it."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0415","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Diagnostic Data' is set to 'Enabled: Diagnostic data off (not recommended)' or 'Enabled: Send required diagnostic data'","Expected":"One of: 0 | 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Allow Diagnostic Data\n\nCheck that 'Allow Diagnostic Data' is set to Enabled: Diagnostic data off (not recommended)' or 'Enabled: Send required diagnostic data.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Allow Diagnostic Data\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: AllowTelemetry\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 0 (decimal; 0x00000000), 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Allow Diagnostic Data\n\nCheck that 'Allow Diagnostic Data' is set to Enabled: Diagnostic data off (not recommended)' or 'Enabled: Send required diagnostic data.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: AllowTelemetry\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 0 (decimal; 0x00000000), 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Certain aspects of Microsoft Anti-Virus must have the setting Allow Diagnostic Data on and sending data to function properly.","Impact":"Note that setting values of 0 or 1 will degrade certain experiences on the device."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0416","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure Authenticated Proxy usage for the Connected User Experience and Telemetry service' is set to 'Enabled: Disable Authenticated Proxy usage'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Configure Authenticated Proxy usage for the Connected User Experience and Telemetry service\n\nCheck that 'Configure Authenticated Proxy usage for the Connected User Experience and Telemetry service' is set to Enabled: Disable Authenticated Proxy usage.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Configure Authenticated Proxy usage for the Connected User Experience and Telemetry service\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: DisableEnterpriseAuthProxy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Configure Authenticated Proxy usage for the Connected User Experience and Telemetry service\n\nCheck that 'Configure Authenticated Proxy usage for the Connected User Experience and Telemetry service' is set to Enabled: Disable Authenticated Proxy usage.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: DisableEnterpriseAuthProxy\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In high-security environments, data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"The Connected User Experience and Telemetry service will be blocked from automatically using an authenticated proxy."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0417","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Do not show feedback notifications' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Do not show feedback notifications\n\nCheck that 'Do not show feedback notifications' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Do not show feedback notifications\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: DoNotShowFeedbackNotifications\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Do not show feedback notifications\n\nCheck that 'Do not show feedback notifications' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: DoNotShowFeedbackNotifications\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"Users will no longer see feedback notifications through the Windows Feedback app."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0418","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable OneSettings Auditing' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Enable OneSettings Auditing\n\nCheck that 'Enable OneSettings Auditing' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Enable OneSettings Auditing\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: EnableOneSettingsAuditing\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Enable OneSettings Auditing\n\nCheck that 'Enable OneSettings Auditing' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: EnableOneSettingsAuditing\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If events are not recorded it may be difficult or impossible to determine the root cause of system problems or the unauthorized activities of threat actors.","Impact":"Windows will record attempts to connect with the OneSettings service to the Applications and Services Logs\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PrivacyAuditing\\Operational Event Log channel."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0419","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Limit Diagnostic Log Collection' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Limit Diagnostic Log Collection\n\nCheck that 'Limit Diagnostic Log Collection' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Limit Diagnostic Log Collection\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: LimitDiagnosticLogCollection\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Limit Diagnostic Log Collection\n\nCheck that 'Limit Diagnostic Log Collection' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: LimitDiagnosticLogCollection\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In high-security environments, data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"Diagnostic logs and information such as crash dumps will not be collected for transmission to Microsoft."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0420","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Limit Dump Collection' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Limit Dump Collection\n\nCheck that 'Limit Dump Collection' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Limit Dump Collection\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: LimitDumpCollection\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Data Collection and Preview Builds\\Limit Dump Collection\n\nCheck that 'Limit Dump Collection' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DataCollection\nValue name: LimitDumpCollection\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"Windows Error Reporting will not send full and/or heap memory dumps to Microsoft they will be limited to kernel mini and/or user mode triage memory dumps (if sending optional diagnostic data is permitted)."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0421","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Download Mode' is NOT set to 'Enabled: Internet'","Expected":"Any value other than: 3","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Delivery Optimization\\Download Mode\n\nCheck that 'Download Mode' is not set to Enabled: Internet.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Delivery Optimization\\Download Mode\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeliveryOptimization\nValue name: DODownloadMode\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: any value other than 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Delivery Optimization\\Download Mode\n\nCheck that 'Download Mode' is not set to Enabled: Internet.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\DeliveryOptimization\nValue name: DODownloadMode\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: any value other than 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends that Delivery Optimization is not allowed to exchange update content with peers on the internet, to avoid untrusted peer connections and uncontrolled bandwidth use.","Impact":"Updates are obtained from Microsoft, internal update services or peers on the local network or organisation only, depending on the mode selected."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0422","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable App Installer' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Desktop App Installer\\Enable App Installer\n\nCheck that 'Enable App Installer' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Desktop App Installer\\Enable App Installer\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppInstaller\nValue name: EnableAppInstaller\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Desktop App Installer\\Enable App Installer\n\nCheck that 'Enable App Installer' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\AppInstaller\nValue name: EnableAppInstaller\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Windows Package 