{"Id":"CHECK_0438","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off account-based insights, recent, favorite, and recommended files in File Explorer' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off account-based insights, recent, favorite, and recommended files in File Explorer\n\nCheck that 'Turn off account-based insights, recent, favorite, and recommended files in File Explorer' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off account-based insights, recent, favorite, and recommended files in File Explorer\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: DisableGraphRecentItems\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off account-based insights, recent, favorite, and recommended files in File Explorer\n\nCheck that 'Turn off account-based insights, recent, favorite, and recommended files in File Explorer' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: DisableGraphRecentItems\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends turning off account-based insights in File Explorer so that cloud file activity and recommendations are not surfaced on the device.","Impact":"File Explorer no longer shows cloud-sourced recent, favourite and recommended files."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0439","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Data Execution Prevention for Explorer' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off Data Execution Prevention for Explorer\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Data Execution Prevention for Explorer' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off Data Execution Prevention for Explorer\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: NoDataExecutionPrevention\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off Data Execution Prevention for Explorer\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Data Execution Prevention for Explorer' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: NoDataExecutionPrevention\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Data Execution Prevention is an important security feature supported by Explorer that helps to limit the impact of certain types of malware.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0440","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off heap termination on corruption' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off heap termination on corruption\n\nCheck that 'Turn off heap termination on corruption' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off heap termination on corruption\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: NoHeapTerminationOnCorruption\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off heap termination on corruption\n\nCheck that 'Turn off heap termination on corruption' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Explorer\nValue name: NoHeapTerminationOnCorruption\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Allowing an application to function after its session has become corrupt increases the risk posture to the system.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0441","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off shell protocol protected mode' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off shell protocol protected mode\n\nCheck that 'Turn off shell protocol protected mode' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off shell protocol protected mode\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: PreXPSP2ShellProtocolBehavior\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\File Explorer\\Turn off shell protocol protected mode\n\nCheck that 'Turn off shell protocol protected mode' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Explorer\nValue name: PreXPSP2ShellProtocolBehavior\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Limiting the opening of files and folders to a limited set reduces the attack surface of the system.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0442","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off location' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Location and Sensors\\Turn off location\n\nCheck that 'Turn off location' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Location and Sensors\\Turn off location\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LocationAndSensors\nValue name: DisableLocation\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Location and Sensors\\Turn off location\n\nCheck that 'Turn off location' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\LocationAndSensors\nValue name: DisableLocation\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"This setting affects the location feature (e.g. GPS or other location tracking).","Impact":"The location feature is turned off, and all programs on the computer are prevented from using location information from the location feature."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0443","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Message Service Cloud Sync' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Messaging\\Allow Message Service Cloud Sync\n\nCheck that 'Allow Message Service Cloud Sync' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Messaging\\Allow Message Service Cloud Sync\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Messaging\nValue name: AllowMessageSync\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Messaging\\Allow Message Service Cloud Sync\n\nCheck that 'Allow Message Service Cloud Sync' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Messaging\nValue name: AllowMessageSync\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In high-security environments, data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"Cellular text messages will not be backed up to (or restored from) Microsoft's cloud services."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0444","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Block all consumer Microsoft account user authentication' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft accounts\\Block all consumer Microsoft account user authentication\n\nCheck that 'Block all consumer Microsoft account user authentication' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft accounts\\Block all consumer Microsoft account user authentication\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\MicrosoftAccount\nValue name: DisableUserAuth\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft accounts\\Block all consumer Microsoft account user authentication\n\nCheck that 'Block all consumer Microsoft account user authentication' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\MicrosoftAccount\nValue name: DisableUserAuth\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Organizations that want to effectively implement identity management policies and maintain firm control of what accounts are used on their computers will probably want to block Microsoft accounts.","Impact":"All applications and services on the device will be prevented from new authentications using consumer Microsoft accounts via the Windows OnlineID and WebAccountManager APIs."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0445","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow auditing events in Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow auditing events in Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nCheck that 'Allow auditing events in Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow auditing events in Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: AuditApplicationGuard\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow auditing events in Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nCheck that 'Allow auditing events in Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: AuditApplicationGuard\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends auditing Application Guard container events so that activity inside isolated browsing sessions is available for investigation.","Impact":"Additional events are written to the event log when Application Guard is used."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0446","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow camera and microphone access in Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow camera and microphone access in Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nCheck that 'Allow camera and microphone access in Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow camera and microphone access in Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: AllowCameraMicrophoneRedirection\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow camera and microphone access in Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nCheck that 'Allow camera and microphone access in Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: AllowCameraMicrophoneRedirection\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends blocking camera and microphone access from Application Guard sessions so that untrusted sites cannot capture audio or video.","Impact":"Websites opened in an Application Guard session cannot use the camera or microphone."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0447","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow data persistence for Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow data persistence for Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nCheck that 'Allow data persistence for Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow data persistence for Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: AllowPersistence\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow data persistence for Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nCheck that 'Allow data persistence for Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: AllowPersistence\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling data persistence in Application Guard so that anything downloaded or changed by an untrusted site is discarded when the session ends.","Impact":"Favourites, cookies and downloaded files in Application Guard sessions are deleted when the user signs out or the device restarts."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0448","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow files to download and save to the host operating system from Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow files to download and save to the host operating system from Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nCheck that 'Allow files to download and save to the host operating system from Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow files to download and save to the host operating system from Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: SaveFilesToHost\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Allow files to download and save to the host operating system from Microsoft Defender Application Guard\n\nCheck that 'Allow files to download and save to the host operating system from Microsoft Defender Application Guard' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: SaveFilesToHost\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing files being saved from Application Guard to the host, so that malicious files from untrusted sites cannot leave the isolated container.","Impact":"Users cannot save files from an Application Guard session to the host operating system."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0449","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure Microsoft Defender Application Guard clipboard settings: Clipboard behavior setting' is set to 'Enabled: Enable clipboard operation from an isolated session to the host'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Configure Microsoft Defender Application Guard clipboard settings: Clipboard behavior setting\n\nCheck that 'Configure Microsoft Defender Application Guard clipboard settings: Clipboard behavior setting' is set to Enabled: Enable clipboard operation from an isolated session to the host.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Configure Microsoft Defender Application Guard clipboard settings: Clipboard behavior setting\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: AppHVSIClipboardSettings\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Configure Microsoft Defender Application Guard clipboard settings: Clipboard behavior setting\n\nCheck that 'Configure Microsoft Defender Application Guard clipboard settings: Clipboard behavior setting' is set to Enabled: Enable clipboard operation from an isolated session to the host.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: AppHVSIClipboardSettings\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends allowing clipboard operations only from the isolated session to the host, so that sensitive data on the host cannot be pasted into untrusted sites.","Impact":"Content can be copied from an Application Guard session to the host, but not from the host into the session."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0450","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn on Microsoft Defender Application Guard in Managed Mode' is set to 'Enabled: 1'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Turn on Microsoft Defender Application Guard in Managed Mode\n\nCheck that 'Turn on Microsoft Defender Application Guard in Managed Mode' is set to Enabled: 1.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Turn on Microsoft Defender Application Guard in Managed Mode\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: AllowAppHVSI_ProviderSet\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Microsoft Defender Application Guard\\Turn on Microsoft Defender Application Guard in Managed Mode\n\nCheck that 'Turn on Microsoft Defender Application Guard in Managed Mode' is set to Enabled: 1.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\AppHVSI\nValue name: AllowAppHVSI_ProviderSet\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends turning on Application Guard in managed mode so that untrusted websites open in a hardware-isolated container.","Impact":"Requires supported hardware and the Application Guard feature; untrusted sites open in a separate isolated browser window. The feature is deprecated on newer Windows 11 releases."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0451","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable news and interests on the taskbar' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\News and interests\\Enable news and interests on the taskbar\n\nCheck that 'Enable news and interests on the taskbar' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\News and interests\\Enable news and interests on the taskbar\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Feeds\nValue name: EnableFeeds\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\News and interests\\Enable news and interests on the taskbar\n\nCheck that 'Enable news and interests on the taskbar' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Feeds\nValue name: EnableFeeds\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling news and interests on the taskbar to stop externally sourced content being delivered to the desktop.","Impact":"The news and interests feed is not available on the taskbar."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0452","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prevent the usage of OneDrive for file storage' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\OneDrive\\Prevent the usage of OneDrive for file storage\n\nCheck that 'Prevent the usage of OneDrive for file storage' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\OneDrive\\Prevent the usage of OneDrive for file storage\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\OneDrive\nValue name: DisableFileSyncNGSC\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\OneDrive\\Prevent the usage of OneDrive for file storage\n\nCheck that 'Prevent the usage of OneDrive for file storage' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\OneDrive\nValue name: DisableFileSyncNGSC\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing OneDrive file storage where it is not an approved service, so that organisational data is not synchronised to unmanaged cloud storage.","Impact":"Users cannot access or synchronise OneDrive from the OneDrive app or File Explorer. Do not apply where OneDrive is the approved storage service."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0453","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Push To Install service' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Push To Install\\Turn off Push To Install service\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Push To Install service' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Push To Install\\Turn off Push To Install service\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\PushToInstall\nValue name: DisablePushToInstall\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Push To Install\\Turn off Push To Install service\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Push To Install service' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\PushToInstall\nValue name: DisablePushToInstall\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends turning off Push To Install so that applications cannot be installed on the device remotely from the Microsoft Store.","Impact":"Applications cannot be pushed to this device from the Microsoft Store website or another device."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0454","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Disable Cloud Clipboard integration for server-to-client data transfer' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Connection Client\\Disable Cloud Clipboard integration for server-to-client data transfer\n\nCheck that 'Disable Cloud Clipboard integration for server-to-client data transfer' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Connection Client\\Disable Cloud Clipboard integration for server-to-client data transfer\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\\Client\nValue name: DisableCloudClipboardIntegration\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Connection Client\\Disable Cloud Clipboard integration for server-to-client data transfer\n\nCheck that 'Disable Cloud Clipboard integration for server-to-client data transfer' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\\Client\nValue name: DisableCloudClipboardIntegration\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling Cloud Clipboard integration for Remote Desktop so that data copied on a remote system is not synchronised to other devices through the cloud.","Impact":"Content copied in a remote session is not added to the local Cloud Clipboard history."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0455","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Do not allow passwords to be saved' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Connection Client\\Do not allow passwords to be saved\n\nCheck that 'Do not allow passwords to be saved' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Connection Client\\Do not allow passwords to be saved\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: DisablePasswordSaving\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Connection Client\\Do not allow passwords to be saved\n\nCheck that 'Do not allow passwords to be saved' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: DisablePasswordSaving\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x0000