{"Id":"CHECK_0463","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Do not allow WebAuthn redirection' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Device and Resource Redirection\\Do not allow WebAuthn redirection\n\nCheck that 'Do not allow WebAuthn redirection' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Device and Resource Redirection\\Do not allow WebAuthn redirection\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: fDisableWebAuthn\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Device and Resource Redirection\\Do not allow WebAuthn redirection\n\nCheck that 'Do not allow WebAuthn redirection' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: fDisableWebAuthn\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In a more security-sensitive environment, it is desirable to reduce the possible attack surface.","Impact":"Users in a Remote Desktop Services session will not be able to authenticate to resources inside the Remote Desktop session using their local authenticator."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0464","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Restrict clipboard transfer from server to client' is set to 'Enabled: Disable clipboard transfers from server to client'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Device and Resource Redirection\\Restrict clipboard transfer from server to client\n\nCheck that 'Restrict clipboard transfer from server to client' is set to Enabled: Disable clipboard transfers from server to client.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Device and Resource Redirection\\Restrict clipboard transfer from server to client\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: SCClipLevel\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Device and Resource Redirection\\Restrict clipboard transfer from server to client\n\nCheck that 'Restrict clipboard transfer from server to client' is set to Enabled: Disable clipboard transfers from server to client.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: SCClipLevel\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In a more security-sensitive environment, it is desirable to reduce the possible attack surface.","Impact":"Users will not be able to transfer clipboard data from the Remote Desktop session to the client."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0465","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Always prompt for password upon connection' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Always prompt for password upon connection\n\nCheck that 'Always prompt for password upon connection' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Always prompt for password upon connection\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: fPromptForPassword\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Always prompt for password upon connection\n\nCheck that 'Always prompt for password upon connection' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: fPromptForPassword\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Users have the option to store both their username and password when they create a new Remote Desktop Connection shortcut.","Impact":"Users cannot automatically log on to Remote Desktop Services by supplying their passwords in the Remote Desktop Connection client."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0466","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Require secure RPC communication' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Require secure RPC communication\n\nCheck that 'Require secure RPC communication' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Require secure RPC communication\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: fEncryptRPCTraffic\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Require secure RPC communication\n\nCheck that 'Require secure RPC communication' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: fEncryptRPCTraffic\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Allowing unsecure RPC communication can exposes the server to man in the middle attacks and data disclosure attacks.","Impact":"Remote Desktop Services accepts requests from RPC clients that support secure requests, and does not allow unsecured communication with untrusted clients."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0467","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Require use of specific security layer for remote (RDP) connections' is set to 'Enabled: SSL'","Expected":"Equals: 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Require use of specific security layer for remote (RDP) connections\n\nCheck that 'Require use of specific security layer for remote (RDP) connections' is set to Enabled: SSL.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Require use of specific security layer for remote (RDP) connections\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: SecurityLayer\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Require use of specific security layer for remote (RDP) connections\n\nCheck that 'Require use of specific security layer for remote (RDP) connections' is set to Enabled: SSL.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: SecurityLayer\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"The native RDP encryption is now considered a weak protocol, so enforcing the use of stronger TLS encryption for all RDP communications between clients and RD Session Host servers is preferred.","Impact":"TLS will be required to authenticate to the RD Session Host server."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0468","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Require user authentication for remote connections by using Network Level Authentication' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Require user authentication for remote connections by using Network Level Authentication\n\nCheck that 'Require user authentication for remote connections by using Network Level Authentication' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Require user authentication for remote connections by using Network Level Authentication\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: UserAuthentication\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Require user authentication for remote connections by using Network Level Authentication\n\nCheck that 'Require user authentication for remote connections by using Network Level Authentication' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: UserAuthentication\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Requiring that user authentication occur earlier in the remote connection process enhances security.","Impact":"Only client computers that support Network Level Authentication can connect to the RD Session Host server."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0469","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Set client connection encryption level' is set to 'Enabled: High Level'","Expected":"Equals: 3","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Set client connection encryption level\n\nCheck that 'Set client connection encryption level' is set to Enabled: High Level.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Set client connection encryption level\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: MinEncryptionLevel\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Security\\Set client connection encryption level\n\nCheck that 'Set client connection encryption level' is set to Enabled: High Level.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: MinEncryptionLevel\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 3 (decimal; 0x00000003)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"If Remote Desktop client connections that use low level encryption are allowed, it is more likely that a threat actor will be able to decrypt any captured Remote Desktop Services network traffic.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0470","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Set time limit for active but idle Remote Desktop Services sessions' is set to 'Enabled: 15 minutes or less, but not Never (0)'","Expected":"Range: 1 | 900000","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Session Time Limits\\Set time limit for active but idle Remote Desktop Services sessions\n\nCheck that 'Set time limit for active but idle Remote Desktop Services sessions' is set to Enabled: 15 minutes or less, but not Never (0).\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Session Time Limits\\Set time limit for active but idle Remote Desktop Services sessions\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: MaxIdleTime\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 through 900000, inclusive (decimal)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Under Computer Configuration, inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Session Time Limits\\Set time limit for active but idle Remote Desktop Services sessions\n\nCheck that 'Set time limit for active but idle Remote Desktop Services sessions' is set to Enabled: 15 minutes or less, but not Never (0).\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: MaxIdleTime\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 through 900000 milliseconds, inclusive (no more than 15 minutes; 0/Never is not permitted)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends limiting idle Remote Desktop sessions to reduce session-hijacking opportunities and unnecessary consumption of system resources.","Impact":"Idle RDP sessions disconnect after 15 minutes or less; running jobs continue unless a separate policy ends the session."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0471","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Do not delete temp folders upon exit' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Temporary Folders\\Do not delete temp folders upon exit\n\nCheck that 'Do not delete temp folders upon exit' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Temporary Folders\\Do not delete temp folders upon exit\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: DeleteTempDirsOnExit\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Remote Desktop Services\\Remote Desktop Session Host\\Temporary Folders\\Do not delete temp folders upon exit\n\nCheck that 'Do not delete temp folders upon exit' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\Terminal Services\nValue name: DeleteTempDirsOnExit\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Sensitive information could be contained inside the temporary folders and visible to other administrators that log into the system.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0472","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prevent downloading of enclosures' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\RSS Feeds\\Prevent downloading of enclosures\n\nCheck that 'Prevent downloading of enclosures' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\RSS Feeds\\Prevent downloading of enclosures\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Internet Explorer\\Feeds\nValue name: DisableEnclosureDownload\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\RSS Feeds\\Prevent downloading of enclosures\n\nCheck that 'Prevent downloading of enclosures' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Internet Explorer\\Feeds\nValue name: DisableEnclosureDownload\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Allowing attachments to be downloaded through the RSS feed can introduce files that could have malicious intent.","Impact":"Users cannot set the Feed Sync Engine to download an enclosure through the Feed property page."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0473","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Cloud Search' is set to 'Enabled: Disable Cloud Search'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow Cloud Search\n\nCheck that 'Allow Cloud Search' is set to Enabled: Disable Cloud Search.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow Cloud Search\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: AllowCloudSearch\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow Cloud Search\n\nCheck that 'Allow Cloud Search' is set to Enabled: Disable Cloud Search.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: AllowCloudSearch\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In high-security environments, data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"Search and Cortana will not be permitted to search cloud sources like OneDrive and SharePoint."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0474","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Cortana' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow Cortana\n\nCheck that 'Allow Cortana' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow Cortana\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: AllowCortana\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow Cortana\n\nCheck that 'Allow Cortana' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: AllowCortana\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling Cortana so that voice input and search activity are not sent to cloud services.","Impact":"Cortana is turned off; local search continues to work."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0475","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Cortana above lock screen' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow Cortana above lock screen\n\nCheck that 'Allow Cortana above lock screen' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow Cortana above lock screen\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: AllowCortanaAboveLock\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow Cortana above lock screen\n\nCheck that 'Allow Cortana above lock screen' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: AllowCortanaAboveLock\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing Cortana from responding on the lock screen so that someone near an unattended device cannot interact with it.","Impact":"Cortana cannot be used while the device is locked."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0476","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow indexing of encrypted files' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow indexing of encrypted files\n\nCheck that 'Allow indexing of encrypted files' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow indexing of encrypted files\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: AllowIndexingEncryptedStoresOrItems\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow indexing of encrypted files\n\nCheck that 'Allow indexing of encrypted files' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: AllowIndexingEncryptedStoresOrItems\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Indexing and allowing users to search encrypted files could potentially reveal confidential data stored within the encrypted files.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0477","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow search and Cortana to use location' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow search and Cortana to use location\n\nCheck that 'Allow search and Cortana to use location' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow search and Cortana to use location\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: AllowSearchToUseLocation\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow search and Cortana to use location\n\nCheck that 'Allow search and Cortana to use location' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: AllowSearchToUseLocation\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing search and Cortana from using location data, to limit location information sent to cloud services.","Impact":"Search and Cortana results are not tailored to the device location."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0478","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow search highlights' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow search highlights\n\nCheck that 'Allow search highlights' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow search highlights\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: EnableDynamicContentInWSB\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Search\\Allow search highlights\n\nCheck that 'Allow search highlights' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Windows Search\nValue name: EnableDynamicContentInWSB\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"In high-security environments, data must never be shared with third-parties without explicit consent, as it may contain sensitive information.","Impact":"\"Interesting\", \"informative\", and \"noteworthy\" information about the current date will not be displayed (by Microsoft) to the user."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0479","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off KMS Client Online AVS Validation' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Software Protection Platform\\Turn off KMS Client Online AVS Validation\n\nCheck that 'Turn off KMS Client Online AVS Validation' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Software Protection Platform\\Turn off KMS Client Online AVS Validation\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Software Protection Platform\nValue name: NoGenTicket\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Software Protection Platform\\Turn off KMS Client Online AVS Validation\n\nCheck that 'Turn off KMS Client Online AVS Validation' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\Software Protection Platform\nValue name: NoGenTicket\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Even though the KMS licensing method does not require KMS clients to connect to Microsoft, they still send KMS client activation state data to Microsoft automatically.","Impact":"The computer is prevented from sending data to Microsoft regarding its KMS client activation state."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0480","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Disable all apps from Microsoft Store' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Disable all apps from Microsoft Store\n\nCheck that 'Disable all apps from Microsoft Store' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Disable all apps from Microsoft Store\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsStore\nValue name: DisableStoreApps\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Disable all apps from Microsoft Store\n\nCheck that 'Disable all apps from Microsoft Store' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsStore\nValue name: DisableStoreApps\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends blocking Microsoft Store applications on high-security workstations so that only centrally managed software can run. The policy wording is inverted: the Disabled state is the one that blocks the applications.","Impact":"Pre-installed and downloaded Microsoft Store applications, including built-in applications, will not launch or update. Applies to Enterprise and Education editions."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0481","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Automatic Download and Install of updates' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 4","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Turn off Automatic Download and Install of updates\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Automatic Download and Install of updates' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Turn off Automatic Download and Install of updates\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsStore\nValue name: AutoDownload\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Turn off Automatic Download and Install of updates\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Automatic Download and Install of updates' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsStore\nValue name: AutoDownload\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 4 (decimal; 0x00000004)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends leaving automatic download and installation of Microsoft Store application updates turned on, so that application security fixes are applied promptly.","Impact":"None - Microsoft Store applications update automatically."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0482","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off the offer to update to the latest version of Windows' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Turn off the offer to update to the latest version of Windows\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the offer to update to the latest version of Windows' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Turn off the offer to update to the latest version of Windows\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsStore\nValue name: DisableOSUpgrade\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Turn off the offer to update to the latest version of Windows\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the offer to update to the latest version of Windows' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsStore\nValue name: DisableOSUpgrade\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends turning off the Microsoft Store offer to upgrade Windows so that operating system upgrades are delivered only through the managed update process.","Impact":"The Microsoft Store does not offer upgrades to the latest version of Windows."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0483","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off the Store application' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Turn off the Store application\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the Store application' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Turn off the Store application\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsStore\nValue name: RemoveWindowsStore\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Store\\Turn off the Store application\n\nCheck that 'Turn off the Store application' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\WindowsStore\nValue name: RemoveWindowsStore\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends turning off the Microsoft Store application where users should not install software themselves, so that only approved applications reach the device.","Impact":"Users cannot open the Microsoft Store app; applications must be delivered through managed deployment."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0484","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow widgets' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Widgets\\Allow widgets\n\nCheck that 'Allow widgets' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Widgets\\Allow widgets\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Dsh\nValue name: AllowNewsAndInterests\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Widgets\\Allow widgets\n\nCheck that 'Allow widgets' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Dsh\nValue name: AllowNewsAndInterests\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling widgets to stop externally sourced news and other content being delivered to the desktop and lock screen.","Impact":"The Widgets board is not available."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0485","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow Recall to be enabled' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows AI\\Allow Recall to be enabled\n\nCheck that 'Allow Recall to be enabled' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows AI\\Allow Recall to be enabled\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsAI\nValue name: AllowRecallEnablement\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows AI\\Allow Recall to be enabled\n\nCheck that 'Allow Recall to be enabled' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsAI\nValue name: AllowRecallEnablement\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing Recall from being enabled, because periodic snapshots of the screen can capture passwords, personal data and other sensitive information.","Impact":"Recall is unavailable to users and existing snapshots are removed; other Copilot+ features are unaffected."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0486","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Automatic Data Collection' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Automatic Data Collection\n\nCheck that 'Automatic Data Collection' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Automatic Data Collection\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WTDS\\Components\nValue name: CaptureThreatWindow\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Automatic Data Collection\n\nCheck that 'Automatic Data Collection' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WTDS\\Components\nValue name: CaptureThreatWindow\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends enabling automatic data collection for Enhanced Phishing Protection so that suspicious password-entry events can be analysed and blocked.","Impact":"Additional information about suspicious sites and applications is sent to Microsoft when a user enters their password into one."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0487","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Notify Malicious' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Notify Malicious\n\nCheck that 'Notify Malicious' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Notify Malicious\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WTDS\\Components\nValue name: NotifyMalicious\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Notify Malicious\n\nCheck that 'Notify Malicious' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WTDS\\Components\nValue name: NotifyMalicious\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends warning users who enter their work or school password into a reported phishing site or a malicious application.","Impact":"Users see a warning and are prompted to change their password after typing it into a malicious site or application."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0488","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Notify Password Reuse' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Notify Password Reuse\n\nCheck that 'Notify Password Reuse' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Notify Password Reuse\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WTDS\\Components\nValue name: NotifyPasswordReuse\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Notify Password Reuse\n\nCheck that 'Notify Password Reuse' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WTDS\\Components\nValue name: NotifyPasswordReuse\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends warning users who reuse their work or school password on other sites or applications, because reused passwords are exposed by third-party breaches.","Impact":"Users see a warning when they reuse their work or school password elsewhere."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0489","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Notify Unsafe App' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Notify Unsafe App\n\nCheck that 'Notify Unsafe App' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Notify Unsafe App\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WTDS\\Components\nValue name: NotifyUnsafeApp\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Notify Unsafe App\n\nCheck that 'Notify Unsafe App' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WTDS\\Components\nValue name: NotifyUnsafeApp\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends warning users who type their work or school password into applications such as text editors or office documents, where it would be stored unprotected.","Impact":"Users see a warning when they type their password into Notepad, Word or other Microsoft 365 applications."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0490","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Service Enabled' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Service Enabled\n\nCheck that 'Service Enabled' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Service Enabled\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WTDS\\Components\nValue name: ServiceEnabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Enhanced Phishing Protection\\Service Enabled\n\nCheck that 'Service Enabled' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WTDS\\Components\nValue name: ServiceEnabled\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends enabling the Enhanced Phishing Protection service so that password-entry events are monitored for phishing and unsafe storage.","Impact":"Enhanced Phishing Protection runs in audit mode at minimum and reports unsafe password entry through Microsoft Defender."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0491","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure Windows Defender SmartScreen' is set to 'Enabled: Warn and prevent bypass'","Expected":"EnableSmartScreen: 1; ShellSmartScreenLevel: Block","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Explorer\\Configure Windows Defender SmartScreen\n\nCheck that 'Configure Windows Defender SmartScreen' is set to Enabled: Warn and prevent bypass.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Explorer\\Configure Windows Defender SmartScreen\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: EnableSmartScreen\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: ShellSmartScreenLevel\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: \"Block\"\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Defender SmartScreen\\Explorer\\Configure Windows Defender SmartScreen\n\nCheck that 'Configure Windows Defender SmartScreen' is set to Enabled: Warn and prevent bypass.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: EnableSmartScreen\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\System\nValue name: ShellSmartScreenLevel\nExpected type: REG_SZ\nExpected data: \"Block\"\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Windows SmartScreen helps keep PCs safer by warning users before running unrecognized programs downloaded from the Internet.","Impact":"Users will be warned and prevented from running unrecognized programs downloaded from the Internet."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0492","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enables or disables Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting\\Enables or disables Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting\n\nCheck that 'Enables or disables Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting\\Enables or disables Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\GameDVR\nValue name: AllowGameDVR\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting\\Enables or disables Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting\n\nCheck that 'Enables or disables Windows Game Recording and Broadcasting' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\GameDVR\nValue name: AllowGameDVR\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling game recording and broadcasting so that screen content cannot be recorded or streamed from business devices.","Impact":"Game Bar recording and broadcasting are unavailable."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0493","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable ESS with Supported Peripherals' is set to 'Enabled: 1'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Hello for Business\\Enable ESS with Supported Peripherals\n\nCheck that 'Enable ESS with Supported Peripherals' is set to Enabled: 1.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Hello for Business\\Enable ESS with Supported Peripherals\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Policies\\PassportForWork\\Biometrics\nValue name: EnableESSwithSupportedPeripherals\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Hello for Business\\Enable ESS with Supported Peripherals\n\nCheck that 'Enable ESS with Supported Peripherals' is set to Enabled: 1.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Policies\\PassportForWork\\Biometrics\nValue name: EnableESSwithSupportedPeripherals\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends Enhanced Sign-in Security with supported peripherals so that biometric data is isolated by virtualisation-based security and only trusted sensors are accepted.","Impact":"External fingerprint readers and cameras that do not support Enhanced Sign-in Security cannot be used for Windows Hello on capable devices."},
