{"Id":"CHECK_0511","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow clipboard sharing with Windows Sandbox' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Sandbox\\Allow clipboard sharing with Windows Sandbox\n\nCheck that 'Allow clipboard sharing with Windows Sandbox' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Sandbox\\Allow clipboard sharing with Windows Sandbox\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Sandbox\nValue name: AllowClipboardRedirection\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Sandbox\\Allow clipboard sharing with Windows Sandbox\n\nCheck that 'Allow clipboard sharing with Windows Sandbox' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Sandbox\nValue name: AllowClipboardRedirection\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling clipboard sharing with Windows Sandbox so that data cannot move between the host and untrusted content running in the sandbox.","Impact":"Copy and paste between the host and Windows Sandbox will not work."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0512","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow mapping folders into Windows Sandbox' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Sandbox\\Allow mapping folders into Windows Sandbox\n\nCheck that 'Allow mapping folders into Windows Sandbox' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Sandbox\\Allow mapping folders into Windows Sandbox\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Sandbox\nValue name: AllowWriteToMappedFolders\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Sandbox\\Allow mapping folders into Windows Sandbox\n\nCheck that 'Allow mapping folders into Windows Sandbox' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Sandbox\nValue name: AllowWriteToMappedFolders\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends preventing writable mapped folders in Windows Sandbox so that untrusted content in the sandbox cannot change files on the host.","Impact":"Folders mapped into Windows Sandbox are read-only."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0513","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Allow networking in Windows Sandbox' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Sandbox\\Allow networking in Windows Sandbox\n\nCheck that 'Allow networking in Windows Sandbox' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Sandbox\\Allow networking in Windows Sandbox\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Sandbox\nValue name: AllowNetworking\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Sandbox\\Allow networking in Windows Sandbox\n\nCheck that 'Allow networking in Windows Sandbox' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\Sandbox\nValue name: AllowNetworking\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends disabling networking in Windows Sandbox so that untrusted content running in the sandbox cannot reach the internal network or the internet.","Impact":"Windows Sandbox has no network access."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0514","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Prevent users from modifying settings' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Security\\App and browser protection\\Prevent users from modifying settings\n\nCheck that 'Prevent users from modifying settings' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Security\\App and browser protection\\Prevent users from modifying settings\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows Defender Security Center\\App and Browser protection\nValue name: DisallowExploitProtectionOverride\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Security\\App and browser protection\\Prevent users from modifying settings\n\nCheck that 'Prevent users from modifying settings' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows Defender Security Center\\App and Browser protection\nValue name: DisallowExploitProtectionOverride\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Only authorized IT staff should be able to make changes to the exploit protection settings in order to ensure the organizations specific configuration is not modified.","Impact":"Local users cannot make changes in the Exploit protection settings area."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0515","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'No auto-restart with logged on users for scheduled automatic updates installations' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Legacy Policies\\No auto-restart with logged on users for scheduled automatic updates installations\n\nCheck that 'No auto-restart with logged on users for scheduled automatic updates installations' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Legacy Policies\\No auto-restart with logged on users for scheduled automatic updates installations\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\\AU\nValue name: NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Legacy Policies\\No auto-restart with logged on users for scheduled automatic updates installations\n\nCheck that 'No auto-restart with logged on users for scheduled automatic updates installations' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\\AU\nValue name: NoAutoRebootWithLoggedOnUsers\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Some security updates require that the computer be restarted to complete an installation.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0516","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure Automatic Updates' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Configure Automatic Updates\n\nCheck that 'Configure Automatic Updates' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Configure Automatic Updates\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\\AU\nValue name: NoAutoUpdate\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Under Computer Configuration, inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Configure Automatic Updates\n\nCheck that 'Configure Automatic Updates' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\\AU\nValue name: NoAutoUpdate\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends managed automatic updates so critical operating-system fixes can be installed promptly and exposure to known vulnerabilities reduced.","Impact":"Update installation may require service interruption or restarts; align update behaviour with existing patch management and agreed maintenance windows."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0517","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Configure Automatic Updates: Scheduled install day' is set to '0 - Every day'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Configure Automatic Updates: Scheduled install day\n\nCheck that 'Configure Automatic Updates: Scheduled install day' is set to 0 - Every day.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Configure Automatic Updates: Scheduled install day\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\\AU\nValue name: ScheduledInstallDay\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Configure Automatic Updates: Scheduled install day\n\nCheck that 'Configure Automatic Updates: Scheduled install day' is set to 0 - Every day.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\\AU\nValue name: ScheduledInstallDay\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Although each version of Windows is thoroughly tested before release, it is possible that problems will be discovered after the products are shipped.","Impact":"If 4 - Auto download and schedule the install is selected in recommendation 'Configure Automatic Updates', critical operating system updates and service packs will automatically download every day (at 3:00 A.M., by default)."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0518","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable features introduced via servicing that are off by default' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Enable features introduced via servicing that are off by default\n\nCheck that 'Enable features introduced via servicing that are off by default' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Enable features introduced via servicing that are off by default\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: AllowTemporaryEnterpriseFeatureControl\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Enable features introduced via servicing that are off by default\n\nCheck that 'Enable features introduced via servicing that are off by default' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: AllowTemporaryEnterpriseFeatureControl\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends that features delivered through servicing remain off until the next annual feature update, so that new functionality is tested before it reaches users.","Impact":"New features shipped in monthly quality updates stay disabled until enabled deliberately or by the next feature update."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0519","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Remove access to 'Pause updates' feature' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Remove access to 'Pause updates' feature\n\nCheck that 'Remove access to 'Pause updates' feature' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Remove access to 'Pause updates' feature\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: SetDisablePauseUXAccess\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage end user experience\\Remove access to 'Pause updates' feature\n\nCheck that 'Remove access to 'Pause updates' feature' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: SetDisablePauseUXAccess\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends removing the ability for users to pause updates, so that security updates are not delayed on individual devices.","Impact":"Users cannot pause Windows updates from Settings."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0520","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Enable optional updates' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage updates offered from Windows Update\\Enable optional updates\n\nCheck that 'Enable optional updates' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage updates offered from Windows Update\\Enable optional updates\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: AllowOptionalContent\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage updates offered from Windows Update\\Enable optional updates\n\nCheck that 'Enable optional updates' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: AllowOptionalContent\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Cyberfort recommends that optional and preview updates are not installed automatically, so that only tested, production-quality updates reach devices.","Impact":"Optional non-security preview updates and gradual feature rollouts are not offered automatically."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0521","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Manage preview builds' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage updates offered from Windows Update\\Manage preview builds\n\nCheck that 'Manage preview builds' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage updates offered from Windows Update\\Manage preview builds\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: ManagePreviewBuildsPolicyValue\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage updates offered from Windows Update\\Manage preview builds\n\nCheck that 'Manage preview builds' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: ManagePreviewBuildsPolicyValue\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"It can be risky for experimental features to be allowed in an enterprise managed environment because this can introduce bugs and security holes into systems, making it easier for a threat actor to gain access.","Impact":"Preview builds are prevented from installing on the device."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0522","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Select when Quality Updates are received' is set to 'Enabled: 0 days'","Expected":"DeferQualityUpdates: 1; DeferQualityUpdatesPeriodInDays: 0","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage updates offered from Windows Update\\Select when Quality Updates are received\n\nCheck that 'Select when Quality Updates are received' is set to Enabled: 0 days.\n\nGP UI path:\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage updates offered from Windows Update\\Select when Quality Updates are received\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: DeferQualityUpdates\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: DeferQualityUpdatesPeriodInDays\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nComputer Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Windows Update\\Manage updates offered from Windows Update\\Select when Quality Updates are received\n\nCheck that 'Select when Quality Updates are received' is set to Enabled: 0 days.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: DeferQualityUpdates\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\WindowsUpdate\nValue name: DeferQualityUpdatesPeriodInDays\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 0 (decimal; 0x00000000)\n\nEvery listed value must meet its expected data and type requirement.\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Quality Updates can contain important bug fixes and/or security patches, and should be installed as soon as possible.","Impact":"None - this is the default behavior."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0523","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Disable HTTP proxy features: Disable WPAD' is set to 'Enabled: Checked'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":"Registry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Internet Settings\\WinHttp\nValue name: DisableWpad\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"Registry verification","Rationale":"WPAD could expose the system to Man-In-The-Middle (MITM) attacks. If an organization depends on HTTP proxy configuration, it is recommended that other client configuration mechanisms be used instead, such as Group Policy.","Impact":"After WPAD is disabled, all proxies must be manually configured."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0524","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Disable HTTP proxy features: Disable proxy authentication' is set to 'Enabled: Disable authentication over loopback interfaces' or higher","Expected":"One of: 256 | 287","Steps":"Registry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\n\nKey: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\\SOFTWARE\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Internet Settings\nValue name: DisableProxyAuthenticationSchemes\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: one of 256 (decimal; 0x00000100), 287 (decimal; 0x0000011F)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","CheckType":"Registry verification","Rationale":"It is best to limit the sign-in interface to only known and trusted services, so malicious actors can't impersonate them.","Impact":"Windows will not be able to authenticate over a loopback interface"},
{"Id":"CHECK_0525","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off toast notifications on the lock screen' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nUser Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Start Menu and Taskbar\\Notifications\\Turn off toast notifications on the lock screen\n\nCheck that 'Turn off toast notifications on the lock screen' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nUser Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Start Menu and Taskbar\\Notifications\\Turn off toast notifications on the lock screen\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\nUse the user SID identified in Review Notes. Inspect only an already loaded user hive; do not load or modify a hive.\n\nKey: HKEY_USERS\\[USER SID]\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\PushNotifications\nValue name: NoToastApplicationNotificationOnLockScreen\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer and the relevant user identified in Review Notes, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nUser Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Start Menu and Taskbar\\Notifications\\Turn off toast notifications on the lock screen\n\nCheck that 'Turn off toast notifications on the lock screen' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\nUse the user SID identified in Review Notes. Inspect only an already loaded user hive; do not load or modify a hive.\n\nKey: HKEY_USERS\\[USER SID]\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\PushNotifications\nValue name: NoToastApplicationNotificationOnLockScreen\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"While this feature can be handy for users, applications that provide toast notifications might display sensitive personal or business data while the device is left unattended.","Impact":"Applications will not be able to raise toast notifications on the lock screen."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0526","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Turn off Help Experience Improvement Program' is set to 'Enabled'","Expected":"Equals: 1","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nUser Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication Settings\\Turn off Help Experience Improvement Program\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Help Experience Improvement Program' is set to Enabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nUser Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication Settings\\Turn off Help Experience Improvement Program\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\nUse the user SID identified in Review Notes. Inspect only an already loaded user hive; do not load or modify a hive.\n\nKey: HKEY_USERS\\[USER SID]\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Assistance\\Client\\1.0\nValue name: NoImplicitFeedback\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer and the relevant user identified in Review Notes, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nUser Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\System\\Internet Communication Management\\Internet Communication Settings\\Turn off Help Experience Improvement Program\n\nCheck that 'Turn off Help Experience Improvement Program' is set to Enabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\nUse the user SID identified in Review Notes. Inspect only an already loaded user hive; do not load or modify a hive.\n\nKey: HKEY_USERS\\[USER SID]\\Software\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Assistance\\Client\\1.0\nValue name: NoImplicitFeedback\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 1 (decimal; 0x00000001)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence."},"CheckType":"GUI and registry verification","Rationale":"Large enterprise managed environments may not want to have information collected by Microsoft from managed client computers.","Impact":"Users cannot participate in the Help Experience Improvement program."},
{"Id":"CHECK_0527","Area":"Administrative templates","AuditCheck":"'Do not preserve zone information in file attachments' is set to 'Disabled'","Expected":"Equals: 2","Steps":{"Workgroup":"On {HOST}, open Local Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). Navigate to:\n\nUser Configuration\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Attachment Manager\\Do not preserve zone information in file attachments\n\nCheck that 'Do not preserve zone information in file attachments' is set to Disabled.\n\nGP UI path:\nUser Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Attachment Manager\\Do not preserve zone information in file attachments\n\nRead the displayed setting.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\nUse the user SID identified in Review Notes. Inspect only an already loaded user hive; do not load or modify a hive.\n\nKey: HKEY_USERS\\[USER SID]\\Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Policies\\Attachments\nValue name: SaveZoneInformation\nExpected type: REG_DWORD\nExpected data: 2 (decimal; 0x00000002)\n\nRead the type and data without changing them. A missing value remains unresolved unless the check explicitly requires absence.","Shared":"Open Group Policy Management (gpmc.msc) on an administrative computer. Right-click Group Policy Results and start Group Policy Results Wizard. Select {HOST} as the target computer and the relevant user identified in Review Notes, complete the wizard, and open the Settings tab. Inspect the applied setting at:\n\nUser Configuration\\Policies\\Administrative Templates\\Windows Components\\Attachment Manager\\Do not preserve zone information in file attachments\n\nCheck that 'Do not preserve zone information in file attachments' is set to Disabled.\n\nRegistry verification on {HOST}:\nOpen Registry Editor (regedit.exe) and read the following existing values in the 64-bit registry view:\nUse the user SID identifi